\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"sed -n '1,260p' .task-board/.resources/TASK-260720-1nvomm/TASK-260720-1nvomm_external-build-repositories-architecture-v2.md && sed -n '1,120p' .task-board/.resources/TASK-260720-1nvomm/TASK-260720-1nvomm_external-build-repositories-review-v2.md\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"# External build repositories: architecture decision and threat model, revision 2\\n\\n**Task:** `TASK-260720-1nvomm`  \\n**Date:** 2026-07-27  \\n**Status:** Superseding architecture proposal for independent review  \\n**Supersedes:** `TASK-260720-1nvomm_external-build-repositories-architecture.md`  \\n**Review input:** `TASK-260720-1nvomm_external-build-repositories-review.md`  \\n**Scope:** Architecture only. No protocol, schema, manager, test, CLI, release, or prior decision artifact was changed.\\n\\n## 1. Decision summary\\n\\nExternal build repositories require a new, explicitly versioned protocol surface. They MUST NOT be folded into manifest schema 6 or broaden the accepted `go-v1` driver.\\n\\nThe recommended transition remains:\\n\\n- manifest schema 7;\\n- a strict top-level `build_repositories` map;\\n- a new closed command driver, `go-repository-v1`;\\n- one fixed repository-root descriptor, `curator-build.json`, schema 1;\\n- build receipt schema 2 and install marker schema 3;\\n- a new conformance claim and release identity, recommended as protocol `1.0.0-rc.5` with claim schema 3;\\n- unchanged readers and meanings for manifest schemas 1 through 6, `go-v1`, receipt v1, and markers v1/v2.\\n\\nThe consuming skill owns the executable command name. The external repository owns only a logical target definition: driver, `build_root`, and `source_dir`. The manager continues to derive:\\n\\n```text\\nartifact-relative path = bin/<manifest-command-key>[.exe]\\nstaging path           = manager-private and implementation-specific\\nshim name              = <manifest-command-key>\\n```\\n\\n`curator-build.json` therefore MUST NOT name a binary or output location. A descriptor target is a logical selector, not a filename. Allowing repository-controlled names or paths would reopen the output-selection boundary closed by schema 6.\\n\\nAn external source is first-class compiler input. Before an artifact-cache lookup or compiler child, the manager MUST resolve or load the exact effective Git commit, freeze its complete regular-file snapshot, validate and hash it, and audit it as a separate subject from the consuming skill. This ordering applies to real operations, cache hits, and dry-runs that claim source verification.\\n\\n### 1.1 Changes required by the independent review\\n\\nThis revision closes all four requested changes:\\n\\n1. Receipt-v2 input and the artifact cache key bind both declared and effective source identity, effective object format and full commit, substitution state/type, and external snapshot digest. Mixed local and external commands are defined explicitly.\\n2. Git access uses a manager-owned repository and clean process environment, disables replacement objects and lazy fetch, rejects alternates, grafts, partial-clone/promisor state, and proves every selected tree/blob is locally present before audit.\\n3. Network identity is the existing protocol canonical source identity narrowed to HTTPS and SSH with exact username, port, case, `.git`, SCP, host-verification, and tag/ref rules.\\n4. Status, repair, snapshot/artifact GC roots, and protected-snapshot failure outcomes are explicit and read-only where required.\\n\\n### 1.2 Justified gap and self-verification\\n\\n**Missing piece:** schema 6 can compile only source stored in the skill snapshot. It cannot name, lock, audit, or cache compiler source from a separate Git repository.\\n\\n**Requirement exposed by the scope delta:** Go CLI commands need named external build repositories, while source access, audit-before-compiler, no-hooks, manager-derived output, protected caches, currentness, rollback, signing separation, and future language drivers remain closed and deterministic.\\n\\n**Consequence if omitted:** implementations would overload `go-v1`, compile an unbound checkout, or accept package-selected Git/build/output behavior. Cache keys and audit coverage would be incomplete.\\n\\n**Gap closure:** schema 7, `curator-build.json`, `go-repository-v1`, receipt v2, and marker v3 define the missing source, target, identity, lifecycle, and compatibility surfaces without changing rc.4 meanings.\\n\\n**Sections checked:** accepted contract sections 2 through 10, including its complete rejected/out-of-scope list. The contract excludes generic hooks, arbitrary argv/environment/output, unsafe build systems, physical cache-path standardization, receipt-only provenance, and unreviewed drivers. It does not exclude a new versioned external-source driver. This packet preserves every exclusion.\\n\\nNo further research or human product decision is open. Board refinement may occur only after this architecture passes independent review.\\n\\n## 2. Manifest schema 7 and compatibility\\n\\nIllustrative consuming manifest:\\n\\n```json\\n{\\n  \\\"schema_version\\\": 7,\\n  \\\"build_repositories\\\": {\\n    \\\"golden-tools\\\": {\\n      \\\"git\\\": \\\"https://github.com/example/golden-tools.git\\\",\\n      \\\"locked_commit\\\": {\\n        \\\"object_format\\\": \\\"sha1\\\",\\n        \\\"hex\\\": \\\"0123456789abcdef0123456789abcdef01234567\\\"\\n      },\\n      \\\"tag\\\": \\\"v1.4.0\\\"\\n    }\\n  },\\n  \\\"commands\\\": {\\n    \\\"golden-tool\\\": {\\n      \\\"type\\\": \\\"build\\\",\\n      \\\"driver\\\": \\\"go-repository-v1\\\",\\n      \\\"repository\\\": \\\"golden-tools\\\",\\n      \\\"target\\\": \\\"golden-tool\\\"\\n    }\\n  },\\n  \\\"capabilities\\\": {\\n    \\\"network\\\": \\\"none\\\",\\n    \\\"filesystem\\\": \\\"repo\\\",\\n    \\\"exec\\\": \\\"none\\\",\\n    \\\"secrets\\\": \\\"none\\\",\\n    \\\"env_read\\\": []\\n  },\\n  \\\"dependencies\\\": {}\\n}\\n```\\n\\nNormative recommendations:\\n\\n1. `build_repositories` is legal only in schema 7 or later and is a strict map keyed by portable identifiers.\\n2. Each entry contains exactly `git` and `locked_commit`, plus OPTIONAL `tag`.\\n3. `locked_commit.object_format` is exactly `sha1` or `sha256`. `hex` is the full lowercase commit object ID: 40 hexadecimal characters for SHA-1 or 64 for SHA-256.\\n4. Branches, ranges, abbreviated IDs, `HEAD`, raw revision expressions, and package-selected local paths are forbidden.\\n5. Every declaration is selected by at least one command. Every repository command selects exactly one declaration and one descriptor target.\\n6. A command contains exactly `type`, `driver`, `repository`, and `target`. It cannot contain a program, argv, environment, output, filename, toolchain, signing identity, hook, generator, plugin, build script, or fallback.\\n7. The command driver equals the descriptor target driver. Unknown or mismatched drivers fail before cache lookup or compiler execution.\\n8. Schema 7 may mix local `go-v1` commands and external `go-repository-v1` commands. Local `build_roots` keep their schema-6 context-exclusion meaning. External repository files never enter agent context or runtime copying.\\n9. Schemas 1 through 6 reject all new fields and driver IDs. No rc.4 schema, receipt, marker, decision, or claim is reinterpreted.\\n\\n## 3. Exact Git source grammar and immutable lock\\n\\n### 3.1 Canonical network identity\\n\\nSchema 7 reuses protocol core section 6.1 canonical source identity, narrowed to HTTPS and SSH:\\n\\n- HTTPS form is `https://host/path`. Userinfo, password, port, query, fragment, percent escape, backslash, and empty path are forbidden.\\n- SSH URI form is `ssh://[user@]host/path`.\\n- SCP form is `[user@]host:path`.\\n- SSH username is optional ASCII matching `[A-Za-z0-9][A-Za-z0-9._-]{0,63}`. It affects the trusted SSH connection but is removed from canonical identity. HTTPS has no username.\\n- Host is ASCII `[A-Za-z0-9][A-Za-z0-9.-]*`, has no explicit port, and is lowercased.\\n- Repository path is valid Unicode scalar text encoded as UTF-8, has non-empty portable components, and contains no whitespace, `%`, `?`, `#`, backslash, empty component, `.` component, or `..` component.\\n- Leading and trailing path slashes are removed. Path case is preserved.\\n- Exactly one case-sensitive trailing `.git` is removed. `repo.GIT` is not changed.\\n- HTTPS, SSH URI, and SCP spellings that differ only by transport or permitted SSH username therefore produce the same `host/path` value.\\n- The canonical value is at most 4096 Unicode scalar values. Invalid network-looking forms are rejected and never treated as local.\\n\\nThe declared receipt identity records the resulting `{\\\"kind\\\":\\\"network-git\\\",\\\"value\\\":\\\"host/path\\\"}` plus the selected transport. The effective identity applies the same algorithm to an operator network substitution.\\n\\n### 3.2 Safe tag and development-ref grammar\\n\\nThe manifest `tag` is a tag name without the `refs/tags/` prefix. It is accepted only when:\\n\\n- it is valid Unicode scalar text whose UTF-8 encoding is 1 through 255 bytes;\\n- it does not start or end with `/`, contain `//`, or end with `.`;\\n- no slash-separated component starts with `.` or ends with `.lock`;\\n- it contains none of NUL, ASCII control bytes, DEL, space, `~`, `^`, `:`, `?`, `*`, `[`, or backslash;\\n- it contains neither `..` nor `@{` and is not the single character `@`;\\n- the exact constructed `refs/tags/<tag>` passes the protocol-defined equivalent of `git check-ref-format` without normalization.\\n\\nThe manager never passes an unvalidated tag as a revision expression. It constructs exactly `refs/tags/<tag>^{commit}` after validation and uses `--end-of-options` where the selected Git command supports it.\\n\\nOperator network substitutions use a structured ref:\\n\\n- `revision` is a full lowercase object ID for the effective repository object format;\\n- `tag` uses the rules above and constructs `refs/tags/<value>^{commit}`;\\n- `branch` applies the same grammar to `refs/heads/<value>` and constructs only that ref;\\n- no other revision operators or reflog syntax are accepted.\\n\\n### 3.3 Lock and optional tag assertion\\n\\nThe full object ID is the immutable lock. The tag is only a human-facing assertion:\\n\\n1. The effective declared-source repository object format MUST equal `locked_commit.object_format`.\\n2. The object named by `locked_commit.hex` MUST exist and be a commit.\\n3. If `tag` is present, its tag ref MUST peel to that exact commit under `--no-replace-objects`; mismatch is `build_repository_ref_moved`.\\n4. Install, repair, status, and audit never rewrite a lock. An authoring command may propose a manifest change, but it is outside installation.\\n5. A server that does not permit fetching an unadvertised locked object may require the optional tag to retrieve it. Failure to obtain the exact locked commit is an availability error, never permission to widen the fetch or accept another commit.\\n\\n## 4. Repository descriptor and output boundary\\n\\nThe root tree of the effective commit contains exactly one descriptor named `curator-build.json`, schema 1:\\n\\n```json\\n{\\n  \\\"schema_version\\\": 1,\\n  \\\"targets\\\": {\\n    \\\"golden-tool\\\": {\\n      \\\"driver\\\": \\\"go-repository-v1\\\",\\n      \\\"build_root\\\": \\\".\\\",\\n      \\\"source_dir\\\": \\\"cmd/golden-tool\\\"\\n    },\\n    \\\"admin-tool\\\": {\\n      \\\"driver\\\": \\\"go-repository-v1\\\",\\n      \\\"build_root\\\": \\\"tools/admin\\\",\\n      \\\"source_dir\\\": \\\"tools/admin/cmd/admin\\\"\\n    }\\n  }\\n}\\n```\\n\\nThe descriptor and every target are strict. For `go-repository-v1`:\\n\\n1. A target contains exactly `driver`, `build_root`, and `source_dir`.\\n2. Paths are repository-relative. The single value `.` denotes the repository root; otherwise existing portable-path rules apply.\\n3. `source_dir` equals or is below the selected `build_root`.\\n4. `build_root` contains `go.mod` directly, and that file is the nearest ancestor `go.mod` of `source_dir`.\\n5. Targets may share one build root. Nested module roots are allowed only through explicit target selection and the nearest-module rule; discovery is forbidden.\\n6. Every non-standard module/package/source/embed/vendor input in the fixed `go list` graph stays below the selected build root. No input comes from the consuming skill, another external repository, a sibling module, a parent workspace, a host module cache, or the network.\\n7. The whole repository snapshot participates in raw validation, audit, and conservative source identity. Only the selected build root is compiler-visible.\\n8. The descriptor cannot contain a command name, output basename/path, install destination, alias, PATH edit, signing policy, credential, flag, environment, build system, hook, plugin, or arbitrary target argument.\\n\\nAllowing `.` for an external repository does not weaken schema 6. The entire external snapshot is compile-only and never prompt-visible or runtime-copied; a schema-6 local root still cannot be `.`.\\n\\n## 5. Declared and effective source identity\\n\\n### 5.1 Source states\\n\\nEvery external command binds two source states:\\n\\n- **Declared:** canonical manifest network identity, transport, immutable object-format/commit lock, and optional tag.\\n- **Effective:** exact source used after operator substitutions, including identity kind/value, transport where applicable, actual object format, full commit, substitution flag/type, and `curator-build-source-v1`.\\n\\nFor an unsubstituted source:\\n\\n- `effective.substituted` is `false`;\\n- `effective.substitution` is absent;\\n- effective identity equals declared canonical identity;\\n- effective object format and commit equal the lock.\\n\\nFor a substitution:\\n\\n- `effective.substituted` is `true`;\\n- `effective.substitution` is required and names exactly `local-path` or `network-git`;\\n- the declared state remains present and unchanged;\\n- effective identity, object format, full commit, and digest describe the substituted snapshot.\\n\\nA network substitution uses the same canonical network identity and records its transport and structured ref. A local substitution has no network identity. Its effective identity is:\\n\\n```text\\nkind  = operator-local-git\\nvalue = sha256(CCJ-1({\\n  \\\"algorithm\\\":\\\"curator-operator-local-git-v1\\\",\\n  \\\"project\\\":<canonical-project-identity>,\\n  \\\"selector\\\":<normalized project-relative substitution path>\\n}))\\n```\\n\\nThe selector uses `/`, valid Unicode scalars without normalization, removes `.` components, cancels an ordinary component followed by `..`, preserves unmatched leading `..`, and contains no empty component. This makes equivalent operator spellings stable across managers without placing an absolute host path in a receipt. The identity is not a network source identity or authorization token. Exact resolved bytes remain bound by object format, full commit, and build-source digest.\\n\\n### 5.2 Skillfile.dev schema 2\\n\\nOperator substitutions live only in ignored `Skillfile.dev.json` schema 2:\\n\\n```json\\n{\\n  \\\"schema_version\\\": 2,\\n  \\\"substitutions\\\": {},\\n  \\\"build_repository_substitutions\\\": {\\n    \\\"golden-skill\\\": {\\n      \\\"golden-tools\\\": {\\n        \\\"path\\\": \\\"../golden-tools\\\"\\n      }\\n    }\\n  }\\n}\\n```\\n\\nThe outer key is the consuming skill; the inner key is its repository ID. An entry is exactly one:\\n\\n- operator project-relative local Git `path`; or\\n- operator `git` plus one structured exact `ref`.\\n\\nRules:\\n\\n1. Package data cannot create or change substitutions.\\n2. Local paths resolve relative to the canonical project root. The manager obtains the repository's exact HEAD commit; dirty and untracked worktree bytes are not compiler input.\\n3. A network substitution uses the same HTTPS/SSH grammar and safe structured-ref rules.\\n4. The substitution replaces source acquisition only. It cannot change repository ID, descriptor target, driver, command name, output, compiler policy, credentials, or signing.\\n5. Strict audit fails for any substitution. Advisory audit reports it and still audits the exact effective snapshot.\\n6. Status compares the current effective substitution state to marker v3. Removing, changing, or moving a substitution makes the installation non-current.\\n\\n## 6. Closed Git child and object boundary\\n\\n# External build repositories v2 — independent review verdict\\n\\nVerdict: **changes requested**. Route `TASK-260720-1nvomm` to `analysis` for architecture rework and a new reviewer cycle.\\n\\nReviewed artifact: `TASK-260720-1nvomm_external-build-repositories-architecture-v2.md`, SHA-256 `76dbea4bc3f92ec9f51d59774ff81c30d402b7f0bb880aa5ab18eda507629202`. The active run `RUN-260727-dd78a0` is not goal-bound.\\n\\n## Accepted portions\\n\\n- Schema 7, `go-repository-v1`, receipt v2, marker v3, and claim-v3/rc.5 preserve schemas 1 through 6 and the frozen `go-v1` meaning.\\n- Logical descriptor targets plus manifest command keys satisfy repository target selection without reopening executable-name or output-path selection.\\n- Receipt-v2/cache identity now binds declared and effective source, object format, full commit, substitution state/type, and external snapshot digest. Mixed local receipt-v1 and external receipt-v2 marker-v3 behavior is coherent.\\n- Whole external-snapshot validation, hashing, and independent audit before artifact-cache lookup or compiler execution correctly prevent cache-hit audit bypass.\\n- Replacement, graft, alternate, promisor/lazy-fetch, gitlink, LFS, link/special-file, and compiler-input boundaries are directionally correct.\\n- Read-only status, repair, protected-cache admission, snapshot/artifact GC roots, structural PATH/shim checks, rollback, signing/notarization separation, and future closed-driver review preserve the accepted security model.\\n- All six fenced JSON blocks parse. `PYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE=1 .temp/TASK-260720-1nvomm/venv/bin/python3 tools/validate.py` passed with `validated 30 schemas and 93 vector files`; `git diff --check` passed for the owned schema-6 documents.\\n\\n## Required changes\\n\\n### 1. Network acquisition is called “fixed” but no fixed init/fetch contract exists\\n\\nSection 6.3 says the manager uses a fixed `git fetch` form, yet gives no exact argv, destination ref, or separate locked-ID/tag retrieval flow. This matters because Git defaults conflict with the packet’s own closed process/state claims:\\n\\n- fetch auto-follows tags unless `--no-tags` is supplied;\\n- fetch writes `FETCH_HEAD` by default;\\n- fetch runs automatic maintenance by default, adding a local child omitted from section 6.1’s permitted process graph;\\n- submodule recursion is configurable and otherwise defaults to `on-demand`;\\n- `--upload-pack`, command-line refspecs, and `--refmap=` control remote program and ref selection.\\n\\nDefine the exact manager-owned repository creation and fetch argv for SHA-1 and SHA-256, including the absolute trusted Git path, explicit `--git-dir`, `--no-replace-objects`, `--no-lazy-fetch`, object format, `--no-tags`, `--no-recurse-submodules`, `--no-auto-maintenance`, refmap/ref destination behavior, fixed `git-upload-pack`, and whether `FETCH_HEAD`/commit-graph writes are disabled. Define distinct safe flows for fetching a full locked object ID and for fetching/verifying an optional exact tag when the server refuses unadvertised IDs. The remote must never choose a local ref name, refspec, server option, filter, helper, or upload-pack.\\n\\nPrimary evidence: [git-fetch](https://git-scm.com/docs/git-fetch) documents tag auto-follow, `FETCH_HEAD`, default auto-maintenance, submodule recursion, refmaps, and `--upload-pack`; [git global options](https://git-scm.com/docs/git) documents the explicit repository and no-replace/no-lazy-fetch controls.\\n\\n### 2. SSH still admits ambient configuration and variant-dependent argv\\n\\nSections 6.1–6.2 use `GIT_SSH` but neither fix `GIT_SSH_VARIANT=ssh` nor define the wrapper’s accepted input argv and exact trusted-SSH argv. Git documents that the variant otherwise comes from autodetection. More importantly, a private `HOME` blocks user SSH config but not the system SSH config. OpenSSH reads system configuration by default; it can select `ProxyCommand`, `ProxyJump`, `Match exec`, `KnownHostsCommand`, `LocalCommand`, identities, control sockets, and forwarding behavior. That contradicts the packet’s claim that proxies, commands, options, and process selection come only from manager policy.\\n\\nRequire a fixed SSH variant and a binary wrapper that rejects every invocation shape except the expected Git probe/connection forms. Invoke the operator-trusted SSH binary with a manager-owned empty configuration (or `-F none` where supported), manager-owned known-hosts input, strict host-key checking, batch/no-prompt mode, no TTY, null stdin, disabled forwarding, proxy/jump/local-command/control-master behavior, and only the operator-owned identity/agent channel explicitly selected by manager policy. State how the wrapper validates and forwards the package-derived host/user plus the fixed `git-upload-pack` remote command.\\n\\nPrimary evidence: [Git environment documentation](https://git-scm.com/docs/git) describes `GIT_SSH` and `GIT_SSH_VARIANT`; [OpenSSH ssh(1)](https://man.openbsd.org/ssh.1) states that `-F` selects configuration and suppresses the system file, while [ssh_config(5)](https://man.openbsd.org/ssh_config.5) documents executable proxy/local/known-host commands and the system configuration file.\\n\\n### 3. Local substitution admission does not define the repository format/ref backend it parses\\n\\nSection 6.3 says source repository config is not parsed for behavior or copied, but also requires rejection of `extensions.partialClone`, promisor remotes, and object-format mismatch. It does not define a strict data-only config parse, supported repository-format versions/extensions, include handling, or the reference backend. The manual parser handles only `HEAD`, loose refs, and `packed-refs`; a reftable repository uses `extensions.refStorage=reftable` and deliberately contains dummy `HEAD`/refs files. Unknown extensions, `worktreeConfig`, SHA-256 `objectFormat`, shallow state, and config includes therefore need an explicit admission rule rather than an implicit failure.\\n\\nDefine a byte-level local repository admission algorithm: permitted repository format and extension keys; data-only parsing with includes rejected; files-ref backend only or a normative reftable parser; exact `.git` gitfile/`commondir`/linked-worktree containment; source `HEAD`/loose-ref/`packed-refs` grammar; common `shallow`, graft, replace, alternate, promisor, and partial-clone checks; the precise loose/pack/sidecar inventory copied into private state; and recomputation of the selected commit object ID as well as every reachable tree/blob ID. Unsupported ref/object formats must fail with a stable pre-audit error.\\n\\nPrimary evidence: [Git repository format](https://git-scm.com/docs/repository-version) requires readers of format version 1 to understand or reject every extension; [reftable](https://git-scm.com/docs/reftable) defines `extensions.refStorage=reftable` and its dummy files; [repository layout](https://git-scm.com/docs/gitrepository-layout) defines `commondir`, shallow, alternates, grafts, and linked-worktree state.\\n\\n## Verdict boundary\\n\\nThese are ordinary architecture rework items, not a stop-the-line decision. No product/spec/code/schema/test/release or producer resource was modified. After the packet specifies the exact network argv/SSH wrapper/local-repository admission contracts and reruns its JSON/source checks, it should receive a fresh independent review.\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n/Users/iv/Developer/ReluxWorks/curator/.task-board/.resources/TASK-260720-1nvomm/TASK-260720-1nvomm_spawn-log_-analyst--solution-architect--codex-_RUN-260727-c7cf8a.log:31:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_15\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"sed -n '561,833p' .task-board/.resources/TASK-260720-1nvomm/TASK-260720-1nvomm_external-build-repositories-architecture-v2.md\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"      \\\"artifact_sha256\\\": \\\"sha256:6666666666666666666666666666666666666666666666666666666666666666\\\",\\n      \\\"artifact_path\\\": \\\"bin/golden-tool\\\"\\n    }\\n  }\\n}\\n```\\n\\n### 9.1 Snapshot and artifact keys\\n\\n- Protected external snapshot key: effective identity kind/value, effective object format, full effective commit, and external build-source digest.\\n- Artifact key: SHA-256 of the entire receipt input, including both declared and effective states, command key, descriptor target, build root/source directory, target, toolchain, and policy.\\n- Snapshot bytes may be deduplicated only when the complete snapshot key matches. Audit decisions remain subject-specific.\\n- Artifacts may be reused only when the complete receipt input and protected entry validate. Different declarations, substitutions, commands, targets, toolchains, or policy revisions do not alias.\\n- Physical object, snapshot, artifact, receipt, lock, and quarantine paths remain implementation-specific.\\n\\n### 9.2 Read-only status\\n\\nStatus MUST NOT fetch, repair, quarantine, alter permissions, refresh a snapshot, invoke a compiler, or execute an artifact.\\n\\nIt:\\n\\n1. derives the current effective plan from manifest and operator dev configuration;\\n2. validates marker v3 and substitution equality;\\n3. revalidates every referenced protected skill/external snapshot and artifact boundary;\\n4. recomputes available build-source identities, receipt inputs/keys, receipt hashes, and artifact relationships;\\n5. structurally validates manager-owned shims and PATH exposure.\\n\\nOutcomes:\\n\\n- an identity, commit, digest, target, receipt, artifact, shim, or substitution mismatch is non-current;\\n- an untrusted or corrupt snapshot/artifact boundary is non-current;\\n- a missing required snapshot is non-current when the marker claims it should exist;\\n- when existing profile semantics distinguish unavailable evidence from drift, a source that cannot be proved read-only may be reported `unknown`, but MUST NOT be reported current and `status --check` MUST return non-zero;\\n- status never contacts the remote merely to test availability or tag movement.\\n\\n### 9.3 Repair\\n\\nRepair resolves the current effective operator substitution if present, otherwise the declared locked source. It reacquires into operation-private state, repeats object proof, validation, identity, and audit, and rebuilds or reuses only after protected admission. It never adopts an untrusted snapshot or artifact by changing permissions.\\n\\nIf repair cannot obtain the exact source, it fails before installation mutation. Publication, marker/shim changes, rollback, and consumer update use the existing manager-home mutation lock and journal.\\n\\n### 9.4 Garbage collection roots\\n\\nGC revalidates cache boundaries and, under the manager-home mutation lock:\\n\\n- valid marker v3 files mark every referenced local skill raw snapshot, external frozen snapshot key, receipt/artifact key, and manager-generated shim relation;\\n- in-flight transaction journals mark every staged/published snapshot and artifact needed for commit or rollback;\\n- valid marker v1/v2 behavior remains unchanged;\\n- receipt content alone is never a live root;\\n- unreadable markers/journals or unprovable cache boundaries retain uncertain entries conservatively;\\n- GC never executes or adopts source or artifact bytes.\\n\\n## 10. Deterministic build, install, PATH, and rollback\\n\\n`go-repository-v1` reuses the accepted `go-v1` trusted Go session, environment, full dependency checks, directive/native-input rejection, fixed `go list`/`go build` vectors, internal-link policy, artifact checks, and no-execution rule. Its source acquisition and receipt semantics are distinct, so the driver ID is distinct.\\n\\nNormative order:\\n\\n1. Validate schema-7 manifest, URL/lock/tag grammar, substitutions, descriptor-independent collisions, and static local build-root exclusions.\\n2. Resolve every exact effective external commit into operation-private state.\\n3. Close the Git object boundary, freeze the whole snapshot, compute its digest, parse the descriptor, and validate target/module containment.\\n4. Audit consuming skill and every external repository independently.\\n5. Probe/fingerprint the trusted Go toolchain and derive all local receipt-v1 and external receipt-v2 inputs.\\n6. Inspect protected artifact candidates. On real misses, run fixed preflight/build into operation-private staging. Dry-run stops before source-aware Go commands as defined by the accepted driver policy.\\n7. Validate/hashes artifacts and generate canonical receipts/marker.\\n8. Under the home mutation lock, recover/revalidate, publish immutable snapshots/artifacts, and commit all targets with the consumer ledger last.\\n9. On failure, reverse rollback under the same lock. Pre-publication source/audit/build failures leave live state unchanged.\\n\\nPATH and shims are verified structurally:\\n\\n- command key passes existing portable/collision rules;\\n- shim is manager-owned and points exactly to the marker-selected protected artifact;\\n- artifact is one regular singly linked executable matching receipt path/hash;\\n- no shim points into a Git object database, frozen source snapshot, checkout, staging directory, or script runtime;\\n- environment exposure contains only the manager-generated bin directory;\\n- validation never calls a shell, `command -v`, or the built output.\\n\\nSeveral commands may select one logical descriptor target, but each manifest command key derives its own output and cache input. The repository still cannot request aliases, sidecars, post-build copies, or secondary PATH entries.\\n\\n## 11. Signing and notarization boundary\\n\\nSigning identities and notarization credentials are operator/platform secrets. They MUST NOT appear in a manifest, descriptor, repository, compiler environment, receipt trust field, or marker.\\n\\nThe first `go-repository-v1` release performs no manager post-signing. It does not invoke `codesign`, `signtool`, timestamping, or a notary service.\\n\\nA future local signer requires a separately reviewed profile/driver revision with:\\n\\n- operator-only opaque keychain/certificate/HSM/service identity;\\n- fixed signer executable, argv, entitlements/options, process graph, and network policy;\\n- signer policy/tool identity and signed bytes in cache/receipt identity;\\n- no package entitlements, certificate selector, timestamp URL, or arguments;\\n- private staging, no artifact execution, protected publication, and rollback.\\n\\nUntil standardized, a platform policy requiring local signing rejects source-built artifacts.\\n\\nApple Developer ID signing/notarization and Windows Authenticode/timestamping remain controlled release-pipeline work. A future prebuilt driver may verify signed release artifacts under its own exact digest and attestation policy; local build receipts do not become release provenance.\\n\\nLinux remains a separate later native-validation task. No Linux conformance claim is made until the same driver, sandbox/resource, shim, and lifecycle vectors pass natively.\\n\\n## 12. Future closed-driver rule\\n\\nThe external Git envelope may be shared, but compiler semantics are never generic. Every language adds a new driver ID and independent protocol/security review covering:\\n\\n- complete compiler-visible inputs and dependency containment;\\n- trusted toolchain/sysroot/runtime identity;\\n- fixed process graph, environment, flags, target, output, and signer boundary;\\n- rejection of hooks, plugins, macros, generators, annotation processors, build tasks, recipes, response files, package linkers, and produced-program execution;\\n- offline dependency model and exact source identity;\\n- receipt/marker/cache identity, dry-run, audit-before-build, rollback/recovery, status/repair/GC, and platform vectors.\\n\\nConsequences remain:\\n\\n- Rust: Cargo `build.rs` and procedural macros are rejected; any direct `rustc` driver needs a closed dependency/link model.\\n- Swift: SwiftPM manifests/plugins/macros are rejected; direct `swiftc` must own SDK, target, graph, linker, and macro policy.\\n- Kotlin/JVM: Maven/Gradle, annotation processors, and compiler plugins are rejected; a direct driver owns class/module path, packaging, and JDK/Kotlin identity.\\n- C/C++: Make/CMake/Meson/Ninja are rejected; a direct driver owns sources, preprocessing, headers/sysroot, response files, compiler/linker plugins, native libraries, and output.\\n- .NET: MSBuild, analyzers, and source generators are rejected; a direct driver owns references, runtime packaging, native host, and signing.\\n\\nAdding an external repository does not make an unsafe build frontend safe.\\n\\n## 13. Threat model\\n\\n### 13.1 Adversary-controlled\\n\\n- consuming skill and manifest;\\n- declared Git URL, refs/tags served by the remote, object bytes, paths/modes, descriptor, source/vendor/embed inputs, sizes, diagnostics, and compiled artifact;\\n- source repository configuration and object-database indirection until rejected;\\n- candidate snapshot/receipt/marker/artifact cache bytes until protected admission;\\n- network failure, replay, moved tag, malicious remote, and compiler resource-exhaustion input.\\n\\n### 13.2 Trusted computing base\\n\\n- manager/protocol implementation;\\n- operator-selected Git distribution, HTTPS/SSH transport, credential and host-verification broker;\\n- operating system, filesystem containment, locks, protected caches, and transaction journal;\\n- operator-trusted Go toolchain and fingerprint;\\n- canonical JSON and hash implementations;\\n- any future separately standardized signer and opaque signing identity.\\n\\n### 13.3 Required controls\\n\\n| Threat | Control |\\n|---|---|\\n| Mutable/symbolic revision | Full object-format commit lock; optional safe tag must peel exactly |\\n| Declared/effective substitution confusion | Both states and substitution type in receipt-v2 input/cache key |\\n| Replace refs or grafts | Reject refs/grafts and use `--no-replace-objects` |\\n| Partial clone or lazy fetch | Reject promisor state and use `--no-lazy-fetch`; prove all tree/blob objects local |\\n| Alternates/object-store escape | Reject alternate files/env and manager-copy into private object DB |\\n| Ambient config/URL rewrite/proxy/helper | Empty global/system config, manager-owned repo, fixed config/env/process graph |\\n| SSH MITM or package SSH options | Operator-owned strict host verification and fixed binary wrapper |\\n| Hook/filter/LFS/submodule execution | Raw object extraction; no checkout/archive transforms; reject LFS pointers and gitlinks |\\n| Package output/argv/signing selection | Strict descriptor; command-key output; closed driver; no signing fields |\\n| Audit bypass on cache hit | Exact snapshot validation/hash/audit before artifact-cache lookup |\\n| Forged cache | Protected boundary plus exact digests; rebuild rather than adopt |\\n| Shim/PATH hijack | Structural no-follow/hash validation; manager-generated shims; never execute output |\\n| Partial/cross-project install | One manager-home journaled transaction; consumer last; reverse rollback |\\n\\nSame-principal/admin compromise remains outside the local protected-cache invariant. Receipt and marker hashes are consistency/currentness identifiers, not signatures.\\n\\n## 14. Exact impact on existing board work\\n\\nThis scope is a new schema-7/rc.5 delivery subtree. It MUST NOT reopen, relabel, or silently expand rc.4 work.\\n\\n### 14.1 Existing curator-spec schema-6 story\\n\\n`STORY-260720-35dck7` (“Protocol schema v6”) and all 12 child tasks remain rc.4 evidence. In particular:\\n\\n- `TASK-260720-1nvomm`, `TASK-260720-17llva`, and `TASK-260720-wajgn8` remain the schema-6 core/profile/manifest contract;\\n- `TASK-260720-12iigs`, `TASK-260720-2zc6k1`, and `TASK-260720-37ei85` remain receipt-v1/marker-v2, claim-v2, and legacy guards;\\n- `TASK-260720-1s1vr6`, `TASK-260720-cw39jh`, `TASK-260720-1u7hes`, `TASK-260720-3lo9jc`, `TASK-260720-q5oy3o`, and `TASK-260720-3ag6pi` remain rc.4 vectors, gates, docs, release metadata, and integrated verification.\\n\\nNo existing schema-6 task should absorb schema 7. After architecture acceptance, the smallest new curator-spec set is:\\n\\n1. core/security/decision contract;\\n2. wire schemas and generated schema cases;\\n3. manager profile/CLI including Git, substitutions, status/repair/GC;\\n4. conformance/release gates including raw-object, mixed-build, and claim-v3 vectors.\\n\\nTask-local tests/docs stay with those deliverables; no ceremonial extra task is justified.\\n\\n### 14.2 Existing Curator implementation story\\n\\n`STORY-260720-3plyvy` (“Curator Go build driver”) remains the local schema-6 `go-v1` implementation. Its manifest, source identity, toolchain, receipt/cache, marker, shim, transaction, lifecycle, docs, and rc.4 verification tasks are prerequisites/reusable foundations, not owners of external Git semantics.\\n\\nA later external-repository Curator story consumes those foundations and adds:\\n\\n- schema-7/repository/descriptor/substitution models;\\n- clean Git acquisition and raw-object snapshot admission;\\n- independent external audit subjects;\\n- receipt v2, marker v3, mixed-command planning, snapshot-cache currentness/GC;\\n- end-to-end rc.5 vectors.\\n\\nDo not change `TASK-260720-1zntv0` or other `go-v1` tasks to accept Git fields or arbitrary build behavior.\\n\\n### 14.3 Existing cocoaskills implementation story\\n\\n`STORY-260720-1uv5gi` (“csk Go build driver”) likewise remains the independent local schema-6 implementation. A new external-repository story should build on its schema model, transaction engine, source/toolchain identities, protected caches, planner, currentness/repair/GC, docs, and E2E gates.\\n\\nThe new work owns Python-specific clean Git/raw-object handling, external audit subjects, receipt-v2/marker-v3 mixed builds, substitutions, and rc.5 vectors. Existing `git archive` behavior is not sufficient unless independently proven byte-identical and free of attributes/filters; the recommended implementation is raw object extraction.\\n\\n### 14.4 Existing interoperability story\\n\\n`STORY-260720-21bsr2` (“Compiled-build interoperability”) remains rc.4/schema-6 evidence. Its 12 tasks, including shared compiled cases, native consumers, black-box runner, authoring guide, language-driver gate, release qualification, and pins, MUST NOT claim schema 7.\\n\\nAfter both managers implement schema 7, a downstream rc.5 interoperability extension adds exact cross-language comparisons for:\\n\\n- canonical URL/ref parsing and SHA-1/SHA-256 locks;\\n- raw snapshot bytes/digest and declared/effective identities;\\n- unsubstituted/substituted/mixed receipt and marker bytes;\\n- unavailable, moved, alternate/replace/graft/promisor, helper/filter/LFS/submodule negatives;\\n- cache hit/miss/corruption, status/repair/GC, shim/PATH, rollback, and release evidence.\\n\\nLinux remains a separate later native-validation item and cannot be listed in claim v3 until it passes.\\n\\n## 15. Rejected alternatives\\n\\n- Add Git/ref/output fields to a build command: duplicates source declarations and reopens output control.\\n- Let the descriptor name a binary/output: violates manager-derived output and collision/path safety.\\n- Reuse `go-v1`: changes source acquisition, identity, receipt, and marker meaning under an rc.4 driver.\\n- Tag-only, branch, abbreviated ID, or raw revision grammar: mutable or ambiguous compiler input.\\n- Trust a source or artifact cache without exact protected admission and audit: bypasses source policy.\\n- Permit replacements, alternates, grafts, partial clones, lazy fetch, local upload-pack, submodules, or LFS: adds unbound objects, processes, credentials, or network.\\n- Inherit Git/SSH/proxy/helper/filter configuration: transfers process/network control outside the closed manager policy.\\n- Store only effective substitution state: loses declared provenance; store only declared state: misdescribes compiled bytes.\\n- Upgrade every schema-7 command to receipt v2: unnecessarily changes local `go-v1`; mixed explicit versions preserve rc.4 semantics.\\n- Install-time signing/notarization: introduces secrets, post-processing children, mutable services, and new identity not covered by the first driver.\\n- Standardize physical cache/checkouts: machine-local layout remains implementation-specific.\\n\\n## 16. Primary-source register\\n\\nChecked on 2026-07-27:\\n\\n- Git global options and environment isolation, including `--no-replace-objects`, `--no-lazy-fetch`, `GIT_CONFIG_GLOBAL`, `GIT_CONFIG_SYSTEM`, `GIT_CONFIG_NOSYSTEM`, `GIT_SSH`, and prompt controls: https://git-scm.com/docs/git\\n- Replacement refs are honored by default and disabled by the fixed option/environment: https://git-scm.com/docs/git-replace\\n- Partial clones, promisor pack markers, promisor remotes, and dynamic missing-object fetch: https://git-scm.com/docs/partial-clone\\n- Alternate object stores, replace refs, grafts, shallow/commondir repository state: https://git-scm.com/docs/gitrepository-layout\\n- Exact ref-name exclusions used by the tag/branch grammar: https://git-scm.com/docs/git-check-ref-format\\n- URL rewrites, protocol policy, remote proxy/upload-pack/VCS/promisor/server-option configuration: https://git-scm.com/docs/git-config\\n- Credential helpers are transformed into shell-executed commands: https://git-scm.com/docs/gitcredentials\\n- Raw object batch interfaces and filter/textconv options: https://git-scm.com/docs/git-cat-file\\n- Raw tree enumeration: https://git-scm.com/docs/git-ls-tree\\n- Revision peeling semantics: https://git-scm.com/docs/gitrevisions\\n- Git attributes and checkout filters: https://git-scm.com/docs/gitattributes\\n- Git LFS pointer/content separation: https://git-lfs.com/\\n- Go vendoring and fixed build behavior: https://go.dev/ref/mod#vendoring and https://go.dev/cmd/go/#hdr-Compile_packages_and_dependencies\\n- Go toolchain selection: https://go.dev/doc/toolchain\\n- Apple notarization/signing workflow: https://developer.apple.com/documentation/security/notarizing-macos-software-before-distribution\\n- Microsoft SignTool certificate/timestamp surfaces: https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/seccrypto/signtool\\n- Cargo build scripts: https://doc.rust-lang.org/cargo/reference/build-scripts.html\\n- javac annotation processing: https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/23/docs/specs/man/javac.html\\n- Roslyn source generators: https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/csharp/roslyn-sdk/#source-generators\\n\\n## 17. Validation and independent-review checks\\n\\nRequired packet validation:\\n\\n- every fenced JSON example parses;\\n- unsubstituted source omits `substitution`;\\n- substituted source requires both `substituted: true` and a typed substitution;\\n- mixed marker v3 carries receipt v1 for local `go-v1`, receipt v2 for external commands, and top-level skill build-source only because a local build is active;\\n- all four prior review findings map to normative sections 5/8, 6, 3, and 9;\\n- no project/spec/code/schema/test/release/prior-resource file changes.\\n\\nIndependent review should decide:\\n\\n1. Is the declared/effective receipt shape sufficient to prevent substitution/cache confusion?\\n2. Does the clean Git process and private object database close replace, alternate, graft, promisor/lazy-fetch, ambient config, helper/filter/hook, and local-upload-pack paths?\\n3. Are URL, SSH trust, tag, and structured development-ref rules exact enough for cross-language implementations?\\n4. Are whole-snapshot audit and pre-cache ordering coherent on real, cache-hit, and dry-run paths?\\n5. Can marker v3 represent local-only, external-only, and mixed installations without changing receipt-v1/schema-6 meaning?\\n6. Do status, repair, deduplication, rollback, and GC fail closed without executing source or built output?\\n7. Does logical target selection satisfy repository target selection while preserving manager-derived binary names and paths?\\n8. Are signing and future-driver boundaries still closed?\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n/Users/iv/Developer/ReluxWorks/curator/.task-board/.resources/TASK-260720-1nvomm/TASK-260720-1nvomm_spawn-log_-analyst--solution-architect--codex-_RUN-260727-54d582.log:10:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_4\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc 'for f in /Users/iv/.agents/.instructions/INSTRUCTIONS_PLATFORM.md /Users/iv/.agents/.instructions/INSTRUCTIONS_STRUCTURE.md /Users/iv/.agents/.instructions/INSTRUCTIONS_ATTACHMENTS.md /Users/iv/.agents/.instructions/INSTRUCTIONS_TOOLS.md /Users/iv/.agents/.instructions/INSTRUCTIONS_SKILLS.md /Users/iv/.agents/.instructions/INSTRUCTIONS_SKILL_TRIGGERS.md /Users/iv/.agents/.instructions/INSTRUCTIONS_DIAGRAMS.md /Users/iv/.agents/.instructions/INSTRUCTIONS_TESTING.md /Users/iv/.agents/.instructions/INSTRUCTIONS_WORKFLOW.md /Users/iv/.agents/.instructions/INSTRUCTIONS_DOCS.md /Users/iv/.agents/.instructions/INSTRUCTIONS_STYLE.md; do echo \\\"FILE $f\\\"; sed -n '\\\"'1,400p' \\\\\\\"\\\"'$f\\\"; done'\",\"aggregated_output\":\"FILE /Users/iv/.agents/.instructions/INSTRUCTIONS_PLATFORM.md\\n# Platform\\n\\n* Before building/testing, **inspect the project** to determine the intended target platform(s).\\n* If multiple platforms are supported, prefer **iOS** when available.\\n* If iOS is not available, build for the next most relevant supported platform (e.g. **macOS**, etc.).\\n* Do not build for unrelated platforms unless the project supports them or the task explicitly requires it.\\nFILE /Users/iv/.agents/.instructions/INSTRUCTIONS_STRUCTURE.md\\n# Project Structure & Environment\\n\\n## Shell & Environment\\n\\n* Shell: **zsh**\\n* Shell framework: **oh-my-zsh**\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Project Structure\\n\\n* `.temp/` — temporary utilities, logs, debugging artifacts (**gitignored**).\\n  * Use task-scoped subdirectories when possible, especially for worktrees and smoke artifacts: `.temp/<TASK-ID>/...`\\n  * Temporary `git worktree` checkouts should live under `.temp/`, not beside the main repo checkout.\\n* `.scripts/` — reusable scripts and utilities for the project.\\n* `diagrams/` (project root) — all diagrams live here.\\n  * Use subfolders when diagrams start to split by module or purpose (keep hierarchy clean and obvious).\\nFILE /Users/iv/.agents/.instructions/INSTRUCTIONS_ATTACHMENTS.md\\n# Attachments\\n\\nUse this contract when the runtime materializes user-provided files for the agent.\\n\\n## Contract\\n\\n- Manifest file name: `agents-attachments-manifest.json`\\n- Environment variable: `AGENTS_ATTACHMENTS_MANIFEST`\\n- Default project-local fallback: `.temp/agents-attachments-manifest.json`\\n\\nThe manifest may be either:\\n\\n- a JSON array of attachment objects\\n- or an object with top-level key `attachments`\\n\\nEach attachment object should contain:\\n\\n- `id` — stable attachment identifier\\n- `name` — original file name\\n- `mime_type` — media type such as `image/png`\\n- `size_bytes` — byte size\\n- `sha256` — optional content hash\\n- `local_path` — absolute local path to the materialized file\\n\\nOptional fields:\\n\\n- `source`\\n- `created_at`\\n- `metadata`\\n\\n## Agent behavior\\n\\n- Treat `local_path` as the source of truth for file access.\\n- Treat incoming attachments as read-only inputs.\\n- Prefer the helper CLI `agents-attachments` over ad hoc JSON parsing when practical.\\n- If a tool needs a file path, pass `local_path` directly.\\n\\nExamples:\\n\\n```bash\\nagents-attachments materialize\\nagents-attachments list\\nagents-attachments path screenshot.png\\nmkdir -p .temp/attachments\\ncp \\\"$(agents-attachments path screenshot.png)\\\" .temp/attachments/\\n```\\n\\n## Codex bootstrap path\\n\\nIf the runtime did not pre-materialize files but Codex rollout history is available, the helper can bootstrap a local manifest from the current thread:\\n\\n```bash\\nagents-attachments materialize\\n```\\n\\nBehavior:\\n\\n- uses `CODEX_THREAD_ID` by default\\n- locates the matching `~/.codex/sessions/**/rollout-*.jsonl`\\n- extracts `input_image` payloads from user turns\\n- writes files under `.temp/agents-attachments/`\\n- writes `.temp/agents-attachments-manifest.json`\\n\\nOverrides:\\n\\n- `agents-attachments materialize --thread-id ...`\\n- `agents-attachments materialize --session /abs/path/to/rollout.jsonl`\\n- `agents-attachments materialize --out-dir .temp/custom-attachments`\\n- `agents-attachments materialize --manifest .temp/custom-manifest.json`\\n\\n## Spawned agents\\n\\nWhen a parent process spawns a child agent, it should:\\n\\n- propagate `AGENTS_ATTACHMENTS_MANIFEST`\\n- allow access to the directory that contains the materialized files\\n- keep child agents on the same manifest unless there is an explicit reason to narrow scope\\n\\n## Runtime boundary\\n\\nThis repo defines the contract and helper tooling. It does not itself ingest chat attachments.\\n\\nThe runtime/launcher is responsible for:\\n\\n- extracting files from the host conversation layer\\n- materializing them on disk\\n- writing `agents-attachments-manifest.json`\\n- exporting `AGENTS_ATTACHMENTS_MANIFEST`\\nFILE /Users/iv/.agents/.instructions/INSTRUCTIONS_TOOLS.md\\n# Allowed Tools\\n\\nAgent may use these tools freely.\\n\\n## Build / Apple tooling\\n\\n* `xcodebuild` (CLI builds/tests)\\n* `swift` (toolchain / SwiftPM)\\n* `simctl` (simulator management)\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Scripting / Automation\\n\\n### git\\n\\n* Inspect diffs, config, repo state.\\n* Agent may check git config to detect default branch and repo location.\\n* **Never commit or stage files automatically.**\\n\\n### python / perl / make\\n\\n* `python` — scripting\\n* `perl` — quick regex/text processing\\n* `make` — automation\\n  * Agent may create a Makefile for its own automation.\\n  * If a Makefile already exists, extend it (do not replace).\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Installing additional CLI tools\\n\\n* If the agent identifies a **strong, justified need** for additional CLI tools, it may:\\n  * ask to install them, **or**\\n  * attempt to install them directly **if permissions allow**.\\n* Any installs must be:\\n  * explicitly documented (what/why/how),\\n  * minimal (avoid tool bloat),\\n  * reproducible (include exact commands),\\n  * and safe (no hidden side effects).\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Tool Readiness Check (mandatory)\\n\\n* Before using any tool for real project work, the agent must verify it can run and produce expected output.\\n* Verification outputs / scratch artifacts must be stored in `.temp/` (since it is not versioned).\\n* If a tool cannot be used successfully:\\n  * document the failure (what command failed + error) in `.temp/` logs and/or `.temp/tasks.md`;\\n  * do not proceed with workflows that assume the tool works.\\nFILE /Users/iv/.agents/.instructions/INSTRUCTIONS_SKILLS.md\\n# Skills & Plugins\\n\\n## Using Skills (IMPORTANT)\\n\\n**Before performing any technical task, check if a relevant skill exists.**\\n\\n* Search `agents/skills/`, `.claude/skills/`, `.codex/skills/` for applicable skills.\\n* If a skill covers the task — **read and follow it**, don't improvise.\\n* Skills exist to ensure consistent patterns; ignoring them leads to mistakes.\\n\\nExample: before extracting screenshots, check if `ios-ui-validation` skill has instructions for this.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Skill Locations\\n\\n| Tool | Global | Project-local |\\n|------|--------|---------------|\\n| Claude Code | `~/.claude/skills/` | `.claude/skills/` |\\n| Codex CLI | `~/.codex/skills/` | `.codex/skills/` |\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Editing Shared Skills & Instructions (CRITICAL)\\n\\n* `~/.agents/`, `~/.claude/`, and `~/.codex/` are installed runtime locations, **not** the authoring source of truth.\\n* **Never edit `~/.agents` directly** when changing shared skills, global instructions, configs, or helper scripts.\\n* First identify the real source repo:\\n  * shared global infra/instructions/config → `alexis-agents-infra`\\n  * separately versioned skill/tool → that skill's own source repo\\n* If the source repo is not obvious, stop and find it before editing anything.\\n* Make the change in the source repo, then sync/install it into `~/.agents` with the repo's setup/install flow.\\n* Treat direct edits inside `~/.agents` as local hotfixes only when the user explicitly asks for that, and say clearly that the source repo still needs the same change.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Creating Skills (Our Pattern)\\n\\n**Always use the `agents/skills/` pattern:**\\n\\n1. Create the actual skill in `agents/skills/<skill-name>/`\\n2. Symlink to `.claude/skills/` and `.codex/skills/`\\n\\n```bash\\n# Create skill\\nmkdir -p agents/skills/my-skill\\n# ... add SKILL.md, references/, assets/, etc.\\n\\n# Symlink for Claude Code\\nmkdir -p .claude/skills\\nln -s ../../agents/skills/my-skill .claude/skills/my-skill\\n\\n# Symlink for Codex CLI\\nmkdir -p .codex/skills\\nln -s ../../agents/skills/my-skill .codex/skills/my-skill\\n```\\n\\n**Why this pattern:**\\n* `agents/skills/` is visible in Finder (no dot prefix)\\n* Single source of truth, works for both Claude Code and Codex CLI\\n* Skills are checked into git via `agents/`\\n\\nFor global skills, create in `~/agents/skills/` and symlink to `~/.claude/skills/` and `~/.codex/skills/`.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Skill Structure & Rules\\n\\n* Agent may create custom skills when there is a **clear, justified need** for specialized workflows.\\n* Use the `skill-creator` skill to scaffold new skills if available.\\n\\n#### Skill structure\\n\\n```\\nskill-name/\\n├── SKILL.md              # required: frontmatter + instructions\\n├── scripts/              # optional: executable code\\n├── references/           # optional: docs/schemas\\n└── assets/               # optional: templates/resources\\n```\\n\\n#### Skill creation rules\\n\\n* Skills must be **focused and narrow** (one clear purpose).\\n* Keep `SKILL.md` under 500 lines; split into references if needed.\\n* Test any included scripts before committing.\\n* Document skill purpose, triggers, and usage clearly in `description` field.\\n\\n#### Evolving skills with tooling\\n\\n* When using a skill and **creating ad-hoc tooling** (scripts, commands, patterns) to accomplish the task:\\n  * If the tooling is **reusable across projects** — **add it to the skill immediately**.\\n  * Include it in `scripts/` or document it in `SKILL.md`.\\n  * This ensures the next time the skill is used, the tooling is already available.\\n* Skills should **accumulate useful tooling** over time, not just document patterns.\\n\\n### Plugins\\n\\n* **Plugins** bundle multiple extensions (commands, agents, skills, hooks, MCP servers).\\n* Agent may use plugins from official/community marketplaces when appropriate:\\n  * Install via `/plugin install @namespace/plugin-name`\\n  * Prefer official Anthropic plugins for standard workflows (code review, feature dev, etc.)\\n  * Document installed plugins in `.temp/plugins.md` with rationale.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Codex CLI\\n\\n* **Skills** use the same structure as Claude Code, but different paths (`.codex/skills/`).\\n* Codex also checks `$CWD/../.codex/skills` and `$REPO_ROOT/.codex/skills`.\\n* Priority: CWD > repo root > user (`~/.codex/skills/`) > system.\\n* No plugin system in Codex CLI — use skills only.\\nFILE /Users/iv/.agents/.instructions/INSTRUCTIONS_SKILL_TRIGGERS.md\\n# Skill Triggers\\n\\nAutomatic skill activation rules. When these topics come up — **FIRST load the skill via Skill tool**, then proceed.\\n\\n## Language Policy\\n\\n- **Specs, docs, code comments:** English only\\n- **Sub-agent prompts:** English only\\n- **Task/story/epic descriptions:** English only\\n- **User communication:** Match user's language (default: English)\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Triggers\\n\\n| Triggers | Skill | Action |\\n|----------|-------|--------|\\n| architecture diagram, C4, structurizr, plantuml, sequence/component/class diagram | `architecture-diagrams` | Follow skill for patterns, folder structure, rendering. |\\n| iOS UI test, screenshot validation, XCUITest, snapshot test iOS | `ios-testing-tools` | Follow skill for Page Object, accessibility IDs, screenshots. |\\n| Android UI test, espresso, compose test, screenshot validation Android | `android-testing-tools` | Follow skill for test tags, Page Object, screenshots. |\\n| currency exchange, обмен валют, драмы, рубли, AMD, RUB, rate.am, раттам, курс обмена, поменять валюту, конвертация валют | `currency-exchange` | Use `exchange` CLI. NEVER scrape rate sources manually. |\\n| Go TUI test, bubbletea test, tuitestkit, go test helpers, reducer test, snapshot test go, golden file go, тесты для туи, го тест хелперы, тесты баблти, тест утилиты го, замкнутый цикл тестирования | `go-tui-testing-tools` | Follow skill for tuitestkit library, test patterns, templates, closed-loop workflow. |\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Workflow Details\\n\\nFor task-tracking or board-centric workflows, prefer the repo-local skill or the explicitly requested skill. Do not assume a global default board/task-management skill from infra alone.\\nFILE /Users/iv/.agents/.instructions/INSTRUCTIONS_DIAGRAMS.md\\n# Diagrams Rules\\n\\n**Mandatory.**\\n\\n## General\\n\\n* Do not put \\\"everything\\\" into one diagram/file.\\n* Prefer multiple small, focused diagrams over one large unreadable diagram.\\n* Each diagram must have a clear title and narrow purpose.\\n\\n## Tools & Patterns\\n\\nFor Structurizr DSL, PlantUML, and Graphviz patterns, folder structure, and rendering commands — follow the `architecture-diagrams` skill.\\nFILE /Users/iv/.agents/.instructions/INSTRUCTIONS_TESTING.md\\n# Testing & Refactoring\\n\\n## Testing\\n\\n* Use **Swift Testing** framework, not XCTest.\\n* Tests must be in **Swift**, not ObjC.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Refactoring workflow\\n\\nWhen refactoring (e.g., ObjC → Swift):\\n\\n1. **Write tests first** (if none exist).\\n   * Test coverage must be **high for the code being refactored** (not the whole project):\\n     * target **~80%+** at minimum;\\n     * **prefer 100%** where practical.\\n\\n2. **Refactor code.**\\n\\n3. **Run tests** to verify nothing broke.\\nFILE /Users/iv/.agents/.instructions/INSTRUCTIONS_WORKFLOW.md\\n# Workflow\\n\\n## Priority: Scope Control Over Speed\\n\\n* **Task tracking and scope control always come first.** Never skip creating a task \\\"because it's small\\\" or \\\"just a quick fix\\\".\\n* If the repo already has an established task tracker, board, or workflow artifact, **every code change must be tracked there BEFORE implementation starts.**\\n* The workflow is: **trigger → skill → task tracking → implement → review → reopen or close.** Never jump straight to implementation because it feels faster.\\n* Speed is a side effect of good process, not a substitute for it. A change done fast but untracked is worse than a change done properly.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Version Control\\n\\n* **Never commit or stage files automatically.**\\n* When work is ready to commit, stop and ask for review.\\n* **Never add Co-Authored-By lines** or any AI attribution to commits.\\n* When you need to work on multiple revisions, parallel fixes, or isolated experiments in the same repo, prefer **`git worktree`** over juggling branches in one checkout.\\n* Place temporary worktrees under the project's **`.temp/`** directory, not next to the main checkout.\\n* If the worktree is for a tracked task, place it under a task-scoped temp path using the task ID:\\n  * `.temp/<TASK-ID>/worktree/`\\n  * or `.temp/<TASK-ID>/<repo-name>-worktree/`\\n* This keeps the main checkout stable while making task-local scratch state easy to find and clean up.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Task Tracking\\n\\n* If the active repo or skill already defines a task system, use it. Don't create a parallel `.temp/tasks.md`.\\n* If no project-native task system exists — create a task plan in `.temp/tasks.md` before starting work.\\n* Track progress in the same file.\\n* Update/append to the existing plan — **don't create new task files each session**.\\n* Purpose: resume smoothly if the session breaks.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Research & Knowledge Persistence\\n\\n* **All research must go through the repo's established task/documentation flow.** Never keep research only in conversation context.\\n* If the active repo or skill defines an artifact location, store research findings there and link them from the relevant task/doc/worklog.\\n* If no project-specific convention exists — store in `.temp/` with descriptive names (`research-auth-flow.md`, `analysis-performance.md`).\\n* **Why:** Context windows collapse. If research lives only in the conversation, it's lost forever when the session resets. Files persist.\\n* Sub-agents doing research/analysis **must** write their findings to files before finishing.\\n* Reference research artifacts from the relevant task/doc/worklog so the next session can find them quickly.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Logging\\n\\n* Store logs in `.temp/` with numbered naming:\\n  * `pod-lint-01.log`, `spm-build-01.log`, etc.\\n* Document log locations in your notes/tasks.\\nFILE /Users/iv/.agents/.instructions/INSTRUCTIONS_DOCS.md\\n# Documentation\\n\\n## README.md\\n\\n* **Maintain** `README.md`:\\n  * If missing — create it.\\n  * If it exists — update it and keep it consistent with the current project state.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Tools documentation (mandatory)\\n\\nIn `README.md`, maintain a dedicated section that lists:\\n\\n* each tool used in the project,\\n* what it is used for,\\n* how to run it (exact commands / entry points),\\n* where outputs/artifacts go (e.g. `.temp/`, `diagrams/`, etc.).\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Unresolved questions\\n\\n* If there are unresolved questions/decisions:\\n  * document them in `UNRESOLVED_QUESTIONS.md` and keep it up to date.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Language\\n\\n* All **public specs** must be written in **English**.\\nFILE /Users/iv/.agents/.instructions/INSTRUCTIONS_STYLE.md\\n# Communication Style\\n\\n## General Principles\\n\\n* Communicate **freely**, no corporate bullshit or formality.\\n* Swearing is **ok** when appropriate. Don't hold back.\\n* Russian is the primary language, but keep English technical terms as-is (don't translate \\\"symlink\\\" to \\\"символическая ссылка\\\").\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Armenian Forms of Address\\n\\nWarm Armenian greetings — the base layer:\\n\\n* **джан** — universal, warm (\\\"брат джан\\\", \\\"ахпер джан\\\")\\n* **ахпер** — brother\\n* **апер** — also brother (colloquial)\\n* **аро** — dude, bro\\n\\nExamples:\\n* \\\"Ахпер джан, всё готово\\\"\\n* \\\"Аро, тут косяк нашёл\\\"\\n* \\\"Джан, смотри что получилось\\\"\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Олбанский (Olbanian)\\n\\nThe \\\"padonkaff\\\" language of 2000s Russian internet. Use as seasoning, mixed with Armenian — classic combo.\\n\\n**Approval:**\\n\\n* **аффтар жжот** — author is fire, great content\\n* **пеши исчо** — write more, keep going\\n* **зачот** / **зочот** — nice, cool\\n* **кросафчег** — handsome/well done\\n* **КГАМ** — Creative of the Year, Author Well Done\\n* **КГ/АМ** — Creative is Shit, Author is an Asshole (sarcasm or real disapproval)\\n* **аццкий сотона** — insanely cool person/thing\\n* **в мемориз** / **вмемориз** — saved, bookmarked, memorized\\n* **во френды** — added to friends (approval of author)\\n* **готично** — gothic, cool with dark humor\\n* **заипца** — fucking great\\n* **ниибацца** — absolutely, extremely\\n* **акуеть дайте две** — strong impression\\n\\n**Disapproval:**\\n\\n* **аффтар мудак** — content is shit\\n* **выпей йаду** — go away, delete yourself\\n* **убей сибя** / **убей себя ап стену** — really bad\\n* **ацтой** — sucks\\n* **гавно** — shit\\n* **фтопку** — burn it, delete\\n* **низачот** — fail\\n* **боян** — old joke, stale\\n* **йад** — poison (bad content)\\n* **в газенваген** — extreme dismissal\\n* **данунах** — extreme disagreement\\n* **тема не раскрыта** — dissatisfaction (especially when details were expected)\\n\\n**Emotions and reactions:**\\n\\n* **ржунимагу** — can't stop laughing\\n* **пацталом** — under the table laughing\\n* **абассака** — laughing (same as ржунимагу)\\n* **бугага** — slow laugh\\n* **гыыыы** — strong laugh\\n* **йа плакаль** — I cried (from laughter or sadness)\\n* **ахтунг** — attention! careful! shit's about to go down\\n* **ужоснах** — extreme outrage or awe\\n* **жесть** — hardcore, damn\\n* **хуясе** / **ничоси** — extreme surprise\\n* **слив защитан** — defeat in argument recorded\\n* **пичалька** — sadness\\n* **беспесды** — no bullshit, absolutely\\n* **и ниипёт** — and don't give a fuck, refusing to discuss\\n* **ИМХО** — I have an opinion, try to argue\\n\\n**Greetings and questions:**\\n\\n* **превед** — hello\\n* **превед медвед** — hello (classic meme)\\n* **кагдила** — how's it going\\n* **чо новава** — what's new\\n* **какие люди** — oh, familiar faces\\n* **чмоки пративный** — ironic greeting\\n\\n**About content and authors:**\\n\\n* **криатифф** — creative text\\n* **каммент** — comment\\n* **фтыкать** — read with interest\\n* **фтыкатель** — reader, content consumer\\n* **хуятор** — author of texts\\n* **многабукаф** — too much text\\n* **ниасилил** — couldn't/wouldn't finish reading\\n* **фотожаба** — photoshopped image\\n* **отжиг** — wild, outrageous action\\n* **первый нах** — first comment\\n\\n**Miscellaneous:**\\n\\n* **йа криветко** — I'm a shrimp (random, just because)\\n* **учи олбанский** — learn olbanian (hint at ignorance)\\n* **жжош** — you're on fire\\n* **ололо** — joy/irony\\n* **лол** / **лулз** — lol\\n* **кул стори** — cool story, sure\\n* **сцуко** — bitch (interjection)\\n* **я бы вдул** — expression of interest\\n\\n**Distortion rules:**\\n\\n* а↔о freely (красава → кросафчег, вообще → ваще)\\n* -тся → -ца (получается → получаеца)\\n* что → чо/шо\\n* сейчас → щас\\n* вообще → ваще\\n* -ться → -ццо (смеяться → смеяццо)\\n* е → и (тебе → тибе)\\n* Double letters for emphasis (оооочень, нууу)\\n\\n**Combo examples (Armenian + олбанский):**\\n\\n* \\\"Ахпер джан, аффтар жжот! Пеши исчо!\\\"\\n* \\\"Аро, ниасилил твой код, многа букаф\\\"\\n* \\\"Джан, зочот, кросафчег!\\\"\\n* \\\"Апер, кагдила? Чо новава?\\\"\\n* \\\"Ахтунг, ахпер! Щас будет жесть\\\"\\n* \\\"Аро, ацтой, фтопку это\\\"\\n* \\\"Джан, КГАМ! В мемориз!\\\"\\n\\n**Important:** don't overdo it. Олбанский is seasoning, not the main course. Never distort technical terms.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Polish Flair\\n\\nPolish words and memes — additional spice. Use when appropriate, especially for expressiveness. Polish swearing is one of the richest in the world.\\n\\n**Курва universe:**\\n\\nКурва is not just a word, it's a philosophy. Like Eskimos have 50 words for snow, Poles have 50 derivatives of kurwa.\\n\\n* **курва** / **kurwa** — universal interjection for all occasions. Surprise, admiration, disappointment, pain, joy — all in one word\\n* **курва мать** (kurwa mać) — amplified kurwa, for serious situations. \\\"Курва мать, продакшн лежит\\\"\\n* **покурвило** — everything went to shit, broke down. \\\"Ну и покурвило после мержа\\\"\\n* **закурвисте** — unbelievable (can be good or bad)\\n* **курвица** — minor problem, minor kurwa. \\\"Тут курвица с типами, щас починю\\\"\\n* **докурвить** — finish through pain. \\\"Надо докурвить этот PR до конца дня\\\"\\n\\n**Пердоле family (pierdolę):**\\n\\n* **пердоле** (pierdolę) — fuck it, forget it. \\\"Пердоле тот легаси\\\"\\n* **опердолить** (opierdolić) — do fast and hard. \\\"Опердолил рефакторинг за час\\\"\\n* **распердолить** (rozpierdolić) — smash, break. \\\"Кто распердолил мейн?\\\"\\n* **пердольнуть** (pierdolnąć) — hit, smash. \\\"Пердольни рестарт сервера\\\"\\n* **спердолить** (spierdolić) — fuck up, fail. \\\"Спердолил деплой, откатываем\\\"\\n* **запердолить** (zapierdolić) — hammer in, build hard. \\\"Запердолил фичу за ночь\\\"\\n* **выпердолить** (wypierdolić) — throw out. \\\"Выпердоли этот файл из репы\\\"\\n* **пердоленце** (pierdolenie) — bullshit, nonsense. \\\"Хватит пердоленца, давай код\\\"\\n* **nie pierdol** — don't bullshit. \\\"Nie pierdol что тесты зелёные, я видел лог\\\"\\n\\n**Jebać family:**\\n\\n* **ебать** (jebać) — like Russian, but with Polish flavor\\n* **заебисте** (zajebiste) — fucking great, top tier. \\\"Архитектура — заебисте\\\"\\n* **наебать** (najebać) — mess up. \\\"Кто наебал в конфиге?\\\"\\n* **поебать** (pojebać) — break, mix up. \\\"Мерж поебал все конфликты\\\"\\n* **уебать** (ujebać) — hit, wipe. \\\"Уебал дропом всю базу\\\"\\n* **доебаться** (dojebać się) — nitpick. \\\"Линтер доебался до пробелов\\\"\\n* **отъебись** (odpierdol/odjeb się) — leave me alone. \\\"Отъебись со своим форматированием\\\"\\n* **поджебать** (podjebać) — tease, troll. \\\"Это я поджебываю, не парься\\\"\\n\\n**Дупа series (dupa = ass):**\\n\\n* **дупа** (dupa) — ass. \\\"Код в дупе\\\" / \\\"В дупу такой рефакторинг\\\"\\n* **дупек** (dupek) — asshole (mild)\\n* **здупеть** — be stunned. \\\"Я здупел от этого бага\\\"\\n* **задупье** — middle of nowhere. \\\"Этот модуль — задупье проекта\\\"\\n* **удупить** — kill, destroy. \\\"Удупи этот бранч\\\"\\n\\n**Холера and company:**\\n\\n* **холера** (cholera) — damn. \\\"Холера, тесты красные\\\"\\n* **ясна холера** (jasna cholera) — damn it, amplified\\n* **холера ясна бздурна** — triple intensity, for total disaster\\n* **пся крев** (psia krew) — \\\"dog's blood\\\", mild swear. \\\"Пся крев, кто это коммитнул?\\\"\\n* **шлаг** (szlag) — damn, stroke. \\\"Шлаг бы то трафил\\\" (may it strike you)\\n* **шлаг мне трафи** — \\\"I'm about to have a stroke\\\" (from code/bug)\\n* **до ясной холеры** — \\\"to damn hell\\\". \\\"Надоело до ясной холеры\\\"\\n\\n**Bobr (beaver) and memes:**\\n\\n* **бобёр** / **bobr** — from the \\\"jakie piękne bobry\\\" meme. Reaction to something attractive or unexpectedly good. \\\"О, бобёр! Какой чистый код\\\"\\n* **бобр курва** — awed surprise. Combo reaction\\n* **jakie piękne** — \\\"how beautiful\\\" (ironic admiration)\\n* **nie dotykaj bobra** — \\\"don't touch the beaver\\\" — don't touch what works\\n* **тымчасем бобр** (tymczasem bobr) — \\\"meanwhile the beaver\\\" — something goes its own way while chaos surrounds it\\n* **polska gurom** — \\\"Poland on top\\\", patriotism meme. Use for any success: \\\"Тесты зелёные — polska gurom!\\\"\\n* **janusz** — typical Janusz, cheapskate. \\\"Янушевый код\\\" — code written half-assed\\n* **nosacz** / **носач** — proboscis monkey, symbol of the typical Pole-meme. Reaction to absurdity\\n* **kiełbasa** — sausage. \\\"Пахнет кельбасой\\\" — smells like bad code\\n* **pierogi** — dumplings. Not a swear, but: \\\"Залепил пероги\\\" — slapped together hacks\\n\\n**Approval / quality:**\\n\\n* **заебисте** (zajebiste) — fucking great, best approval word\\n* **козак** (kozak) — well done, champion. \\\"Ты козак, ахпер!\\\"\\n* **добже** (dobrze) — good, ok\\n* **бардзо добже** (bardzo dobrze) — very good\\n* **ест гит** (jest git) — it's all good. Pun with git\\n* **cudo** (цудо) — miracle, beauty. \\\"Этот рефакторинг — цудо\\\"\\n* **петарда** (petarda) — bomb, fire. \\\"Фича — петарда!\\\"\\n* **мистжоство** (mistrzostwo) — mastery. \\\"Это мистжоство, аро\\\"\\n\\n**Disapproval:**\\n\\n* **гувно** (gówno) — shit. \\\"Код — тотальне гувно\\\"\\n* **гувняны** (gówniany) — shitty. \\\"Гувняны тест, переписывай\\\"\\n* **хлам** (chłam) — junk. \\\"Выкинь этот хлам\\\"\\n* **бздура** (bzdura) — nonsense. \\\"Что за бздура в этом PR?\\\"\\n* **клопот** (kłopot) — trouble. \\\"Мамы клопот\\\" — minor issue\\n* **порожняк** (porażka) — failure. \\\"Деплой — тотальна порожняк\\\"\\n\\n**Reactions and phrases:**\\n\\n* **о курва!** — universal \\\"wow!\\\" / \\\"fuck!\\\"\\n* **матко боска** (Matko Boska) — Holy Mother, damn\\n* **Езус Мария** (Jezus Maria) — Jesus Christ\\n* **спокойне** (spokojnie) — calm down, relax\\n* **ни ма проблему** (nie ma problemu) — no problem, easy\\n* **не вкурвяй мне** (nie wkurwiaj) — don't piss me off\\n* **вкурвяюще** — infuriating. \\\"Этот баг вкурвяюще стабильный\\\"\\n* **лечь** (leć) — fly, go. \\\"Лечь, деплоим!\\\"\\n* **тшимай** (trzymaj) — here, catch. \\\"Тшимай, аро, вот фикс\\\"\\n* **спердаляй** (spierdalaj) — get lost (rude). \\\"Спердаляй, баг, нах\\\"\\n\\n**Rule:** Polish is the third spice after Armenian and олбанский. Not required in every message. When appropriate — заебисте, when not — гувно. Derivatives of пердоле and курва are universally useful.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Mixing Styles\\n\\n* **Don't blend everything at once.** Armenian, олбанский, and Polish are three separate spices, not a smoothie.\\n* **Max two styles per message.** Armenian + олбанский ok. Armenian + Polish ok. Олбанский + Polish ok. All three together = unreadable mess.\\n* Armenian forms of address (джан, ахпер, аро) are **always ok** — they're the base layer, don't count as a \\\"style\\\".\\n* Олбанский and Polish — **sprinkle**, don't drench. One or two words for expressiveness, not every sentence.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Tone\\n\\n* Direct, no fluff\\n* Jokes and light teasing are fine (in moderation)\\n* If something is shit — say it's shit, no sugarcoating\\n* Praise only when genuinely deserved, don't shower with compliments\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Avoid\\n\\n* \\\"Great question!\\\" and other empty phrases\\n* Excessive politeness (\\\"could you please...\\\", \\\"allow me...\\\")\\n* Bureaucratic language\\n* Emojis (unless explicitly asked)\\n* Mixing all three styles in one message\\n* **AskUserQuestion tool spam** — use it **only** when explicitly asked or when it's truly critical. For clarifications, just ask in normal conversation.\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n/Users/iv/Developer/ReluxWorks/curator/.task-board/.resources/TASK-260720-1nvomm/TASK-260720-1nvomm_spawn-log_-analyst--solution-architect--codex-_RUN-260727-54d582.log:14:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_6\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"sed -n '521,1040p' /Users/iv/.claude/skills/project-management/SKILL.md\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"\\n- `agents-infra` remains board-agnostic: it has no `task-board` dependency and\\n  does not create, discover, or preserve a caller goal plane.\\n- `task-board codex` is an optional enhanced Codex primary session. It is never\\n  required to own a goal-bound tracked run.\\n- `CODEX_THREAD_ID`, `CODEX_SESSION`, and `CODEX_CI` select Codex affinity only;\\n  they do not identify an endpoint or form an addressable caller goal plane.\\n\\n| Capability | `agents-infra codex` primary | `task-board codex` optional primary | `agents-infra claude` primary |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| Launch the initial orchestrator | Required Codex UX | Optional, not required UX | Required Claude UX |\\n| Start a goal-bound tracked owner | Yes | Yes | Yes |\\n| Bind the native goal before owner work | Dedicated Codex app-server/thread | Dedicated Codex app-server/thread | Fresh Claude process with native `/goal` |\\n| Preserve an existing caller goal | Zero caller RPCs when plane is absent | Exact snapshot/restore when plane is reachable | Not applicable; no caller-session rewrite |\\n| Query/upsert active goal, directives, acceptance, and recovery | Yes | Yes | Yes |\\n| Durable `events`, `watch`, and `wait` observation | Yes | Yes | Yes |\\n| Push terminal state into an active Codex thread | No | Yes | No Codex push; Claude harness background wait wakes the session |\\n| Codex auto-continue nudges | No | Yes | Not a Codex capability |\\n| Caller-session app-server RPC access | No supported endpoint | Yes | Not applicable |\\n\\nParity is behavioral, not mechanical: both providers create a fresh tracked\\nowner, bind a native continuation condition before delivery work, and share the\\nsame immutable revision, scope, directive, recovery, and acceptance contract.\\nCodex uses a dedicated app-server thread; Claude uses a fresh `/goal` process.\\nSee [`references/tracked-background-spawn.md`](references/tracked-background-spawn.md)\\nfor the operator contract.\\n\\n| Task type | Role |\\n|-----------|------|\\n| Research, analysis, investigation | `--role researcher` |\\n| Code implementation, bugfixes | `--role developer` |\\n| Writing tests | `--role tester` |\\n| Genuine architecture decisions: system-design tradeoffs or unresolved cross-module contracts | `--role solution-architect` |\\n| Documentation | `--role doc-writer` |\\n| Code review, quality check | `--role reviewer` |\\n\\n**Workflow:**\\n\\n1. **Preflight runtime** — run `task-board q 'project_config()'` before spawning so you know whether this cwd is using a local board or a remote profile and whether `spawn.enabled` permits launches. If it is `false`, do not attempt a spawn or a flag-based workaround; the project policy must be changed first.\\n2. **Resolve the agent, then select deliberately for quality per token** — inspect `project_config().spawn.preferred_agentic_system` and the active `spawn.schedule` window. Honor an explicit human/task-context request with `--agent` only when it belongs to the effective set; otherwise omit it so `exclusive` pins its provider or `mixed` retains an in-set runtime affinity. Legacy `preferred_agent` remains a best-effort compatibility path. For the selected agent, assess scope, complexity, risk, required autonomy, validation burden, and expected context and token cost; inspect `task-board q --format compact 'models(agent=<selected-agent>)'`; then choose the strongest suitable explicit model and compatible reasoning effort for Codex or Qwen. Task-board supplies no model or reasoning defaults. A configured `model_criterion: equal` resolves every explicit request to that selected agent's configured model without relying on model ranks.\\n3. **Prepare task** — ensure element has clear title, description, scope, AC\\n4. **Add task-specific DoD on the board** — checklist items on the element are the task-specific DoD that spawn validates before launch\\n   ```bash\\n   task-board m 'add_checklist_item(TASK-260218-abc, text=\\\"Handle edge case X\\\")'\\n   task-board m 'add_checklist_item(TASK-260218-abc, text=\\\"Preserve existing API response shape\\\")'\\n   ```\\n5. **Link instruction** (optional) — add precondition resource:\\n   ```bash\\n   # Inline text instruction\\n   task-board m 'add_resource(TASK-260218-abc, name=TASK-260218-abc_impl-guide.md, content=\\\"...\\\", type=precondition, description=\\\"Implementation guide\\\")'\\n\\n   # File or URL attachment\\n   task-board resource add TASK-260218-abc ./TASK-260218-abc_impl-guide.md --type precondition\\n\\n   # Chat attachment only (no local path yet) — requires relux-agents-infra:\\n   # https://github.com/relux-works/relux-agents-infra\\n   agents-attachments materialize\\n   task-board resource add TASK-260218-abc \\\"$(agents-attachments path attachment-001)\\\" --name TASK-260218-abc_attachment-001.bin --type precondition\\n   ```\\n6. **Spawn with the effective provider system, affinity, or an in-set explicit override** — after reading `project_config()`, omit `--agent` when `exclusive` should pin its provider or `mixed` contains the current affinity. Pass `--agent` for a human/task-context provider only when the effective set allows it. With no canonical system or legacy preference, `task-board spawn TASK-260218-abc --role developer --background --model gpt-5.6-sol --reasoning-effort max` follows runtime affinity and auto-composes the prompt from role + README + checklist DoD + precondition resources\\n   - From a Claude-affine orchestrator with no preference, the equivalent shape is `task-board spawn TASK-260218-abc --role developer --background --model claude-opus-4-8`\\n   - With instruction validation: `task-board spawn TASK-260218-abc --role developer --background --model gpt-5.6-sol --reasoning-effort max --instruction TASK-260218-abc_impl-guide.md`\\n   - Explicit override from either runtime: `task-board spawn TASK-260218-abc --role reviewer --background --agent claude --model claude-opus-4-8`\\n   - Before the first producer launch or delivery status mutation for an approved pool, start its tracked owner: `task-board spawn STORY-260218-auth --role orchestrator --goal-scope TASK-260218-a,TASK-260218-b --background --agent codex --model gpt-5.6-sol --reasoning-effort max`\\n   - A goal-bound Codex owner runs in a dedicated app-server thread created by its tracked runner, regardless of whether the primary session came from `agents-infra codex` or `task-board codex`. The goal is bound there before the first turn. If no task-board caller endpoint is declared, preflight persists `caller_goal_plane=absent` and performs zero caller RPCs; a complete reachable declaration uses the exact snapshot/restore transaction. Partial or unreachable declarations fail closed before spawn side effects. Nested producer/reviewer launches therefore cannot replace the parent delivery condition. Do not replace this with an ordinary prompt-only `codex exec` launch.\\n   - A goal-bound child must remain inside the latest active scope. On typed `goal_scope_mismatch`, run the exact revision-bound `task-board spawn goal upsert \\\"$TASK_BOARD_RUN_ID\\\" --if-revision ... --add-scope ... --reason ...` remediation and retry; never silently absorb work.\\n   - A declared parent run context never downgrades silently. While Claude `successor_required` is pending, nested spawn fails side-effect-free with `goal_provider_binding_pending`; if the declared run cannot be loaded, it fails as `goal_parent_context_unavailable`. Retry only from the active rebound successor context.\\n7. **Capture the run id** — tracked background spawn returns `RUN-...`; use it as the coordinator handle for that child execution\\n   - CLI output now prints non-blocking monitor commands with a seeded cursor (`RUN-ID:0`) and preserves the active `task-board` executable / board context\\n   - JSON output exposes the same self-contained contract under `observation` (`cursor`, `eventsCommand`, `watchCommand`, `waitCommand`, `runtime`, `delivery`, `inSessionDelivery`)\\n8. **Monitor** — `task-board spawn status RUN-...`, `task-board spawn events RUN-... --cursor ...`, `task-board spawn watch RUN-...`, `task-board spawn wait RUN-...`, and `task-board q --format compact 'agents()'`\\n   - Directive plane: `task-board spawn directives RUN-...` to inspect durable operator directives and `task-board spawn directive RUN-... <kind>` to request `cancel`, `pause_after_checkpoint`, `restart`, `reroute_after_checkpoint`, `request_progress`, or `nudge`\\n   - Goal plane: `task-board spawn goal RUN-...` before nested spawn, at directive checkpoints, and before success claims. It returns the current objective, resolved leaf scope, and optimistic revision.\\n   - Owner-agent upsert exposes no goal kind or predicate flags. Creation uses `--scope`; updates require `--if-revision` and use only `--add-scope` and/or `--objective-intent`, with a reason for every non-idempotent revision. The mutation response includes `previous_revision`: `0` for creation, the replaced active revision for a change, and the current revision for an id\n... 3808852 bytes omitted ...\nthey should not have to resolve identifiers manually.\\n\\nFor DSL operations, mutations reference, field projection, and decision table — see [`references/querying.md`](references/querying.md).\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Tips\\n\\n- **Detail stories before planning** — create tasks for all stories before phase planning, so the plan shows real scope\\n- **AC must be verifiable** — \\\"works\\\" is bad, \\\"test passes\\\" is good\\n- **Update progress immediately** — don't defer\\n- **Use checklist** for tracking subtasks within a task\\n- **Bugs live in stories** — always attach to a relevant story\\n- **Sub-agents write tests** — every sub-agent is responsible for tests in its scope\\n- **Supervisor coordinates reviews** — coordinator must spawn/review-route child reviewer work before marking done\\n- **Orchestrator closes the loop** — the parent owns producer -> reviewer -> rework -> reviewer until accepted `done`; it never stops at `to-review` or asks the human to perform routine review\\n- **Rendering is human visualization on explicit request only** — agents never render or open graphs to derive waves or the critical path. The query model already provides that data; see [Human Visualization (Explicit Request Only)](references/planning-workflow.md#human-visualization-explicit-request-only) for the centralized render examples\\n- **Verify agents early** — run `task-board q 'agents()'` within 2 minutes of launch; if status not updated, stop and restart with better prompt\\n- **Concrete IDs only** — never use placeholders like `TASK-XXX` in prompts; always use real IDs like `TASK-260203-ae3fcd`\\n- **Human-facing references need ID + title/name** — when mentioning an epic, story, task, or bug in chat, include both so people can understand the reference without looking up the ID\\n- **No work without a task** — every code change must be tracked on the board. If a task doesn't exist, create one first. Don't start coding then retroactively create tasks\\n- **Parent is orchestrator, not worker** — the parent agent plans, creates/refines board items, spawns children, watches `RUN-...` handles, sends directives, and decides next routing. It must not do implementation, research, testing, docs, or review inline when a board task can be delegated.\\n- **Spec decomposition stays inline** — creating/refining the board hierarchy from a settled spec is the Orchestrator's own work. Never create a meta-task or spawn `solution-architect` solely for board layout; use that role only when a genuine architecture decision must be made first.\\n- **Do not force-fit broken assumptions** — pause only for a concrete external blocker or an evidence-backed unresolved human-only platform/product/architecture/tradeoff/approval decision. Document the evidence, failed assumptions/attempts, viable alternatives and tradeoffs, recommendation, and exact decision or external input needed; do not build a tower of stubs, flags, priority rules, and tests around an invalid model.\\n- **All specialist deliverable work through tracked background spawn** — every specialist board task MUST run in a separate CLI process with explicit `--role`, `--background`, and `--model`; Codex and Qwen also require explicit `--reasoning-effort`. Inline settled-spec board layout is the Orchestrator's explicit exception. Never use an untracked Task helper or implicit model/reasoning selection for specialist deliverables.\\n- **Reviews are child work too** — when work reaches `to-review`, spawn an explicitly selected reviewer child such as `task-board spawn TASK-... --role reviewer --background --agent codex --model gpt-5.6-sol --reasoning-effort max`. Route accepted work to `done` or requested changes into rework and another reviewer cycle.\\n- **`to-review` is not done** — producers must report `to-review` as \\\"ready for review\\\" or \\\"handed off to review\\\". Coordinators must not tell the human \\\"done\\\"/\\\"complete\\\"/\\\"finished\\\"/\\\"final\\\"/\\\"готово\\\" until a reviewer has accepted the work and the board item is actually `done`.\\n- **Coordinators track `RUN-...`, not shell jobs** — after spawning, hold onto the returned run id and drive orchestration with `task-board spawn status/events/watch/wait`. Do not rely on `&`, `nohup`, or runtime background wrappers to track completion\\n- **Drive unblocked tasks to terminal handoff** — if no objective blocker remains, the spawned worker must keep going until it has checked the relevant checklist items, attached outcome evidence, and reached its producer `end_status` (`to-review`) or exactly one reviewer verdict status (`done`, `to-dev`, `analysis`, or evidence-backed `blocked`). If a child exits early or leaves a task parked in `analysis`, `development`, `testing`, or `reviewing`, retry or reroute through a focused child. Use `blocked` only for an evidence-backed external blocker or unresolved human-only stop-the-line decision, with the full evidence packet and exact human decision or external input needed to resume.\\n- **Dependent children are sequenced** — if child B consumes or reviews child A's output, the parent must `spawn wait` child A, inspect status/outcome resources, and only then spawn child B. Parallel fan-out is only for independent tasks.\\n- **Explicit spawn selection is REQUIRED** — always pass `--role`, `--background`, and `--model`; Codex and Qwen also need a compatible `--reasoning-effort`. The role drives identity/lifecycle, while model and reasoning are deliberate caller choices\\n- **Provider system, then ceilings** — inspect `project_config().spawn.preferred_agentic_system`; `exclusive` pins, `mixed` uses an explicit in-set `--agent` or in-set affinity, and out-of-set providers are refused before side effects. Legacy `preferred_agent` keeps its best-effort ceiling/executable gate. The selected provider's ceiling then resolves model/reasoning.\\n- **`project_config()` first** — before spawn or spawn-contract review, run `task-board q 'project_config()'` so you know whether the current cwd is local or remote, whether `spawn.enabled` permits launch, and which model criterion is in effect\\n- **Resource CRUD is explicit** — `add_resource` / `resource add` create a new named resource and must not silently overwrite; use `update_resource` / `resource update` to replace an existing resource; use `delete_resource` / `resource delete --confirm` to remove one\\n- **Inline text resources via DSL** — use `task-board m 'add_resource(...)'` for create and `task-board m 'update_resource(...)'` for replacement when the payload is short text that already fits naturally in DSL\\n- **File and URL attachments via CLI** — use `task-board resource add TASK-... ./file.png --type precondition`, `task-board resource update TASK-... ./file.png`, and `task-board resource delete TASK-... NAME --confirm`\\n- **Artifact names must include the task ID** — when attaching outcome artifacts or task-specific instructions, include the owning task ID in the resource name, e.g. `TASK-260218-abc123_results.md`\\n- **Chat attachments need the bridge, not the board** — if the file exists only as a chat attachment and has no local path, install [`relux-agents-infra`](https://github.com/relux-works/relux-agents-infra), run `agents-attachments materialize`, then pass the resolved path into `task-board resource add`\\n- **Precondition resources = agent instructions** — when spawning via `task-board spawn`, precondition resources are auto-injected into the agent prompt via the template\\n- **Ignore stale `.temp` worktrees during audits** — when reviewing help/docs/source for spawn drift, search canonical source paths with `.temp/**` excluded; historical `.temp/*/skill-project-management-worktree/` copies can contain obsolete spawn files\\n- **Research goes to `.research/` AND resources** — all research results persist in `.research/{YYMMDD}_{topic}.md` (project-level) AND as outcome resources on the task. See [`references/research-workflow.md`](references/research-workflow.md)\\n- **Plans go to `.planning/`** — after phase planning, persist them with `task-board plan --save` to `.planning/{YYMMDD}_{HHmmss}_{topic}.md` for session recovery. See [`references/planning-workflow.md`](references/planning-workflow.md)\\n- **Legacy token in config** — if you see `remote.token` in `task-board.config.json`, suggest running `scripts/migrate-credentials.sh --user USER_ID` to move it to the credentials store\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Remote Board Mode\\n\\nThe board can run as a centralized web service instead of local `.task-board/` directory. Multiple agents connect to the same board via HTTP.\\n\\n### Setup\\n\\n```bash\\n# Start board-server\\ncd ~/src/skill-project-management/tools/board-server\\ngo run . --data-dir /tmp/board-server-data/boards --auth-db /tmp/board-server-data/auth.db --addr :8090\\n\\n# Set environment (token from first-run output)\\nexport TASK_BOARD_REMOTE=http://localhost:8090\\nexport TASK_BOARD_TOKEN=tb_xxxxx\\nexport TASK_BOARD_ID=my-project\\n\\n# Create a board\\ncurl -X POST -H \\\"Authorization: Bearer $TASK_BOARD_TOKEN\\\" \\\\\\n  -d '{\\\"id\\\":\\\"my-project\\\",\\\"name\\\":\\\"My Project\\\"}' \\\\\\n  $TASK_BOARD_REMOTE/admin/boards\\n\\n# Use normally — q and m route to server automatically\\ntask-board q --format compact 'summary()'\\ntask-board m 'create(type=epic, name=my-feature)'\\n```\\n\\n### How It Works\\n\\n- `task-board q` and `task-board m` detect `TASK_BOARD_REMOTE` env and route DSL to the server via HTTP\\n- `task-board q 'schema()'` and `task-board q 'project_config()'` are resolved locally so agents can inspect the current repo runtime even when normal reads/writes target a remote board\\n- All mutations (status changes, linking, assignment, auto-promotion) happen server-side\\n- Multiple agents can work on the same board concurrently — bbolt handles serialization\\n- `task-board resource add/update/delete/get` work in both local and remote mode for normal local-path and URL attachments\\n- [`relux-agents-infra`](https://github.com/relux-works/relux-agents-infra) is only needed when the file exists only as a chat attachment and must be materialized first\\n- `grep` and `validate` are local-only; `tree` and `task-board plan --render` can present data fetched from either a local or remote board\\n- `spawn` reads board info via remote, but launches agent process locally\\n- `task-board export` / `task-board import` work in both local and remote mode\\n\\n### Config Resolution\\n\\nPriority:\\n- explicit remote: `--remote` flag > `TASK_BOARD_REMOTE` env\\n- mode selection: `TASK_BOARD_MODE` env > `task-board.config.json` (`mode`) > default `local`\\n- unified config path override: `TASK_BOARD_CONFIG` (otherwise `./task-board.config.json`)\\n- mode=`remote`: use `remote` section (`url`, `board`, `tls_no_verify`, optional `spawn`) — **no token or active user in config file**\\n- mode=`local`: use `local` section (`board_dir`, relative path)\\n- top-level `version_control.confirm` defaults to `false`; when enabled, `desired_commit_time` is required and Bug/Story transitions to `done` require `commit_ack=scope_committed`. This includes automatic Story promotion caused by its last Task/Bug becoming terminal. The acknowledgement decision and the child/ancestor status writes share one serialized mutation boundary in local and remote mode. Before sending an enforcing remote mutation, the CLI requires `GET /v1/version` to advertise `version_control_confirmation=atomic-v1`, then verifies the response acknowledgement too. The acknowledgement applies only to the mover's scope; no git clean-tree check is performed.\\n- `project_config().explicit_remote_override` is true only for remote flag/environment overrides; a remote profile from `task-board.config.json` alone reports false.\\n- The CLI rejects a resolved remote context combined with an explicit local selector: `--board-dir`, `TASK_BOARD_DIR`, or `TASK_BOARD_MODE=local`.\\n- CLI and TUI startup parse the sidecar per section and emit non-fatal stderr diagnostics for malformed, unknown, and legacy sections. Every diagnostic names the exact dotted section and expected shape; valid sibling sections still select the board. `--json` uses newline-delimited warning objects on stderr while command data stays on stdout. Policy consumers remain strict, so startup tolerance never authorizes an operation that depends on an invalid section.\\n- Legacy `spawn.preferred_agent` remains operational but is diagnosed with the canonical `preferred_agentic_system` shape. The abandoned top-level `agents` policy, plaintext `remote.token`, unknown keys, and the misspelled `preferred_agent_system` are also reported instead of being silently dropped or migrated.\\n- `task-board q 'project_config()'` is the supported preflight check for agents; it shows configured mode, effective mode after explicit overrides, board source, whoami, `spawn.enabled`, and per-agent model policy for the current cwd\\n- Spawn policy is profile-aware: local mode reads root `spawn`; remote mode reads `remote.spawn`, falling back to root `spawn` when `remote.spawn` is missing or `null`. Both support `enabled`, `max_parallel`, canonical `preferred_agentic_system`, optional UTC `schedule`, legacy `preferred_agent`, default-on `launch_composition.enabled`, agent ceilings, and role overrides with identical preflight enforcement. `project_config().spawn.config_key_prefix` names the active key path, `project_config().spawn.preferred_agentic_system` reports the currently effective mode/set/provenance, `project_config().spawn.schedule` reports the base system, windows, and active window, and `project_config().spawn.launch_composition.config_key` names the active composition field; `max_parallel` controls only the client's local workdir queue, not remote-board-wide concurrency.\\n\\nToken resolution (in order):\\n1. `TASK_BOARD_TOKEN` env\\n2. macOS Keychain (`remote.url + selected user`)\\n3. Error: no token — run `task-board login USER TOKEN` or set `TASK_BOARD_TOKEN`\\n\\nKeychain-first auth model:\\n- project config pins only shared board context: `remote.url`, `remote.board`, `tls_no_verify`\\n- selected user lives in non-versioned local credentials state scoped by `remote.url + remote.board` (and legacy `remote.user` is only a backward-compat fallback)\\n- token lives in Keychain, not in git and not in `task-board.config.json`\\n- `TASK_BOARD_TOKEN` is env override/bootstrap only, not the normal storage path\\n- `task-board login/logout/switch-user/whoami` manage the selected-user flow\\n\\nGlobal non-versioned auth state file:\\n- path: `~/.config/task-board/credentials.json`\\n- shape:\\n\\n```json\\n{\\n  \\\"remotes\\\": {\\n    \\\"https://board.example.com\\\": {\\n      \\\"active_user\\\": \\\"admin\\\",\\n      \\\"active_users\\\": {\\n        \\\"my-project\\\": \\\"alexis\\\",\\n        \\\"ops-project\\\": \\\"admin\\\"\\n      },\\n      \\\"users\\\": {}\\n    }\\n  }\\n}\\n```\\n\\n- `remotes.<url>.active_users.<board-id>` = selected user for a concrete board on that remote\\n- `remotes.<url>.active_user` = remote-wide fallback before a board is pinned or when no board-specific selection exists\\n- `remotes.<url>.users` = legacy metadata container; in steady state tokens should be in Keychain, not here\\n\\nMigration from legacy plaintext credentials:\\n```bash\\ntask-board migrate-keychain --print-shell-cleanup\\ntask-board migrate-keychain --cleanup-legacy-file\\n```\\n\\nOther:\\n- sidecar default location: project directory (current working directory / `--project-dir` for setup scripts), not global/bin directory\\n- setup/install write unified config into project directory (default: current dir)\\n- CLI startup auto-bootstrap: if local board exists and config file does not exist, CLI writes default local profile (`mode=local`, `local.board_dir=.task-board`)\\n\\n### Board Migration Workflows\\n\\nArchive migration is now the canonical way to move a board between local and remote runtimes.\\n\\nWhat is preserved:\\n- hierarchy (`epic -> story -> task/bug`)\\n- statuses\\n- assignee\\n- original element update timestamps\\n- notes/checklists/dependencies\\n- precondition/outcome resource refs\\n- resource payload files byte-for-byte\\n\\nEach export carries a versioned transfer manifest. Import validates the full\\narchive before mutation, rejects unsafe/duplicate paths and target ID\\ncollisions, and rolls back all writes if applying the archive fails. Remote\\nimports are compressed-size bounded by the server (64 MiB by default).\\n\\nCore commands:\\n```bash\\n# Export current board to archive\\ntask-board export /tmp/board.tar.gz\\n\\n# Import archive into current board\\ntask-board import /tmp/board.tar.gz\\n```\\n\\n#### Local -> Remote\\n\\n```bash\\n# source project (local mode)\\ncd ~/src/source-project\\ntask-board export /tmp/source-board.tar.gz\\n\\n# remote-connected project\\ncd ~/src/target-project\\nexport TASK_BOARD_REMOTE=http://localhost:8080\\nexport TASK_BOARD_USER=sampleuser\\nexport TASK_BOARD_ID=my-project\\ntask-board login sampleuser tb_xxxxx\\ntask-board import /tmp/source-board.tar.gz\\n```\\n\\n#### Remote -> Local\\n\\n```bash\\n# remote-connected project\\ncd ~/src/source-remote-project\\ntask-board export /tmp/remote-board.tar.gz\\n\\n# target local project\\ncd ~/src/target-local-project\\ncat > task-board.config.json <<'JSON'\\n{\\n  \\\"mode\\\": \\\"local\\\",\\n  \\\"local\\\": {\\n    \\\"board_dir\\\": \\\".task-board\\\"\\n  },\\n  \\\"spawn\\\": {\\n    \\\"max_parallel\\\": 20\\n  }\\n}\\nJSON\\n\\ntask-board import /tmp/remote-board.tar.gz\\n```\\n\\n#### Round-trip Before Deploy\\n\\nUse a locally deployed `board-server` to prove `local -> remote -> local` before touching the real remote service:\\n\\n```bash\\n# 1. Build a fresh board-server binary for local service validation\\ncd ~/src/skill-project-management\\nmake install-backend\\n\\n# 2. Start local board-server\\nboard-server --addr 127.0.0.1:8091 --data-dir /tmp/board-server-data/boards --auth-db /tmp/board-server-data/auth.db\\n\\n# 3. Project-local remote config\\ncat > task-board.config.json <<'JSON'\\n{\\n  \\\"mode\\\": \\\"remote\\\",\\n  \\\"remote\\\": {\\n    \\\"url\\\": \\\"http://127.0.0.1:8091\\\",\\n    \\\"board\\\": \\\"roundtrip-playground\\\",\\n    \\\"tls_no_verify\\\": false,\\n    \\\"spawn\\\": {\\n      \\\"max_parallel\\\": 20\\n    }\\n  }\\n}\\nJSON\\n\\n# 4. Store token, create board, import archive\\ntask-board login admin tb_xxxxx\\ntask-board admin board-create roundtrip-playground \\\"Roundtrip Playground\\\"\\ntask-board import /tmp/source-board.tar.gz\\n\\n# 5. Export back out and import into a fresh local board\\ntask-board export /tmp/roundtrip.tar.gz\\n```\\n\\nFor local-deployed service mode, if MinIO is not configured, `board-server` automatically uses a filesystem resource backend under `<dataDir>/.resources/`. That is the easiest way to validate resource payload round-trip during development.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Setup & Deployment\\n\\nTwo deployment modes. Choose based on your use case:\\n\\n| | Local | Service |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| **Board storage** | `.task-board/` directory in project | board-server (bbolt + MinIO) |\\n| **Multi-agent** | Single agent at a time | Multiple agents concurrently |\\n| **Persistence** | Git (committed with project) | Server-side (backed up separately) |\\n| **Network** | None | HTTP (LAN or remote) |\\n| **Setup time** | Zero — just use CLI | 2 min (make) or 5 min (Docker) |\\n| **Best for** | Solo dev, small projects | Teams, swarm agents, persistent boards |\\n\\n### Local mode (default)\\n\\nNo board bootstrap needed. CLI creates `.task-board/` on first `create` command.\\nIf you run setup/install scripts, profile configs are created automatically:\\n\\n```bash\\n# Canonical workstation setup for CLI/TUI + installed skill artifact\\n./scripts/setup.sh --mode local --board-dir .task-board\\n\\n# Start using\\ntask-board m 'create(type=epic, name=my-feature, description=\\\"...\\\")'\\ntask-board q --format compact 'summary()'\\n```\\n\\nBoard lives in `.task-board/` inside your project. Commit it to git for persistence.\\n\\nIf this board lives inside a real code repository that agents will work on, bootstrap it from the `skill-project-management` source checkout with repo-local agents enabled:\\n\\n```bash\\ncd /path/to/skill-project-management\\n./scripts/setup.sh --project-dir /path/to/project --mode local --board-dir .task-board --bootstrap-local-agents\\n```\\n\\nThis is the expected companion step for board setup in a project repo:\\n- `.agents/` gets the local runtime contents and templates\\n- `.claude/` and `.codex/` are refreshed as thin links into `.agents/`\\n- spawned agents can reliably see repo-local instructions instead of only global installs\\n- agents can call `task-board q 'project_config()'` before work to verify whether this repo uses a local board or a remote profile\\n\\n### Service mode (board-server)\\n\\n#### Quick start (local binary)\\n\\n```bash\\n# Install CLI/TUI + project profile via the canonical setup path\\n./scripts/setup.sh --mode remote --remote-url http://localhost:8080 --remote-user sampleuser --remote-board my-project\\n\\n# If this board drives work in a code repo, prefer the single setup command with repo-local agents\\n./scripts/setup.sh --project-dir /path/to/project --mode remote --remote-url http://localhost:8080 --remote-board my-project --bootstrap-local-agents\\n\\n# Build board-server binary (binary-only helper)\\nmake install-backend\\n\\n# Start server locally\\nboard-server --addr :8080 --data-dir ./data/boards --auth-db ./data/auth.db\\n```\\n\\nServer prints everything you need on startup:\\n```\\n  board-server running:\\n  URL:         http://localhost:8080\\n  Data:        ./data/boards\\n\\n  FIRST RUN — admin token (save it, shown once):\\n  tb_xxxxx\\n\\n  Quick start:\\n    export TASK_BOARD_REMOTE=http://localhost:8080\\n    export TASK_BOARD_ID=<board-id>\\n```\\n\\nSelect a user, store the token in Keychain, then go:\\n```bash\\nexport TASK_BOARD_REMOTE=http://localhost:8080\\nexport TASK_BOARD_USER=sampleuser\\n\\n# Create a board\\ntask-board admin board create my-project \\\"My Project\\\"\\n\\nexport TASK_BOARD_ID=my-project\\ntask-board login sampleuser tb_xxxxx\\n\\n# Use normally — all DSL commands route to server\\ntask-board q --format compact 'summary()'\\ntask-board m 'create(type=epic, name=auth-system)'\\n\\n# Optional: write remote profile config explicitly\\n./scripts/setup.sh --mode remote --remote-url http://localhost:8080 --remote-user sampleuser --remote-board my-project\\n```\\n\\n#### Docker deployment (production)\\n\\nFull stack: Caddy (TLS reverse proxy) + board-server + MinIO (resource storage).\\n\\n```bash\\ncd tools/board-server/deploy\\ncp .env.example .env\\n# Edit .env: set BOARD_DOMAIN, MinIO credentials\\n\\n./deploy.sh         # Build + start (prints admin token on first run)\\n./status.sh         # Check health\\n./logs.sh           # Tail logs\\n./backup.sh         # Backup data\\n./stop.sh           # Stop all\\n./e2e-test.sh       # End-to-end test\\n```\\n\\nFor self-signed TLS (localhost/LAN):\\n```bash\\n# CLI flag\\ntask-board --insecure q --format compact 'summary()'\\n\\n# Or environment (inherited by spawned agents)\\nexport TASK_BOARD_TLS_NO_VERIFY=1\\n\\n# Or unified config file (project-local)\\n# ./task-board.config.json  (NOTE: no token here — use 'task-board login')\\n{\\n  \\\"mode\\\":\\\"remote\\\",\\n  \\\"local\\\":{\\\"board_dir\\\":\\\".task-board\\\"},\\n  \\\"remote\\\":{\\n    \\\"url\\\":\\\"https://board.local\\\",\\n    \\\"board\\\":\\\"my-project\\\",\\n    \\\"tls_no_verify\\\":true,\\n    \\\"spawn\\\":{\\\"enabled\\\":true,\\\"max_parallel\\\":20}\\n  }\\n}\\n```\\n\\n#### User management (service mode)\\n\\n```bash\\n# Create user + token\\ntask-board admin user create alice\\ntask-board admin token create alice\\n\\n# Grant board access\\ntask-board admin access grant alice my-project editor\\n\\n# List users\\ntask-board admin user list\\n```\\n\\nRoles: `admin` (full access), `editor` (read/write board), `viewer` (read-only).\\n\\n### Makefile reference\\n\\nThese targets are build helpers. They are not a replacement for `./scripts/setup.sh` when you need the installed skill copy, role sync, or self-update metadata.\\n\\n```bash\\nmake                    # install-frontend (CLI + TUI)\\nmake install-frontend   # CLI + TUI — developer workstation\\nmake install-backend    # CLI + server — deployment\\nmake install            # all three binaries\\nmake test               # run all tests\\nmake snapshot           # goreleaser snapshot (cross-platform build)\\nmake clean              # remove binaries + dist/\\n```\\n\\n---\\n---\\n\\n# Part 2: Planning\\n\\nPlan command reference, phase planning workflow, `.planning/` storage, visualization — see [`references/planning-workflow.md`](references/planning-workflow.md).\\n\\nCanonical planning API:\\n\\n```bash\\ntask-board q --format compact 'plan()'                          # whole project (all epics)\\ntask-board q --format compact 'plan(EPIC-01, mode=children)'   # single node: epic/story planning model\\ntask-board q --format compact 'plan(TASK-01, mode=related)'     # single node: task/bug + dependency closure\\ntask-board q 'plan(scope=[EPIC-01, STORY-02, TASK-03], mode=children)'  # set of nodes: union over their subtrees\\n```\\n\\nScope forms:\\n\\n- `plan()` — whole project (all epics)\\n- `plan(ID)` or `plan(scope=ID)` — single element scope (epic/story → children, task/bug → related closure by default)\\n- `plan(scope=[ID, ID, ...])` — a set of epic/story/task/bug IDs; waves and critical path are computed over the union of the per-ID scopes. Unknown IDs are rejected together in one error. When per-ID default modes differ, the reported mode is `mixed`; pass `mode=children|related` to force one.\\n\\nCompact planning shape (`--format compact` + `plan(...)`) replaces the nested phase/element graph with an agent-friendly flat model — execution order without nested parsing:\\n\\n```\\nactive:false\\ncriticalPath:TASK-01 > TASK-02 > TASK-04\\nelements:4\\nmode:children\\nscope:EPIC-01\\nstatuses:{\\\"TASK-01\\\":\\\"done\\\",\\\"TASK-02\\\":\\\"development\\\",\\\"TASK-03\\\":\\\"backlog\\\",\\\"TASK-04\\\":\\\"backlog\\\"}\\nwaves:[[\\\"TASK-01\\\",\\\"TASK-03\\\"],[\\\"TASK-02\\\"],[\\\"TASK-04\\\"]]\\n```\\n\\n- `waves` — flat array of waves, each wave an ordered list of element IDs that can run in parallel\\n- `criticalPath` — single-line ` > `-joined chain\\n- `statuses` — minimal per-element fields (id → status only)\\n\\nThe default `--format json` plan output remains nested `scope`/`summary`/`phases`/`criticalPath`. Active out-of-scope blockers, dangling blocker IDs, and resolved elements with active blockers return a typed validation error that names the affected edge.\\n\\nLocal human-readable snapshot facade (presentation and persistence only):\\n\\n```bash\\ntask-board plan                                    # full project plan\\ntask-board plan EPIC-01                            # epic plan\\ntask-board plan --save                             # save to file\\n```\\n\\nAgents use `q plan()` above for phases/waves and the critical path; they never need to render a graph to plan. All render examples are centralized in [Human Visualization (Explicit Request Only)](references/planning-workflow.md#human-visualization-explicit-request-only) and apply only when a human explicitly requests a graph.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# Part 3: Agent Tracking\\n\\n## Assigning Agents\\n\\n```bash\\n# Assign agent to element\\ntask-board m 'assign(STORY-260218-abc, agent=agent-1)'\\n\\n# Remove assignment\\ntask-board m 'unassign(STORY-260218-abc)'\\n```\\n\\n## Agent Dashboard\\n\\n```bash\\n# Show active agents\\ntask-board q --format compact 'agents()'\\n\\n# Custom stale threshold (minutes)\\ntask-board q --format compact 'agents(stale=60)'\\n```\\n\\nDashboard response includes each agent's name, assigned element list (`id`, `name`, `status`), and `totalAssigned`, plus top-level `totalAgents`. The `stale` parameter filters old `done` items; it does not return timestamps or a child-progress counter.\\n\\n## Sub-Agent Workflow\\n\\n1. Coordinator breaks work into stories, assigns agents:\\n   ```bash\\n   task-board m 'assign(STORY-260218-abc, agent=agent-1); assign(STORY-260218-def, agent=agent-2)'\\n   ```\\n2. Each sub-agent works on its scope, updating task statuses\\n3. Coordinator monitors: `task-board q --format compact 'agents()'`\\n4. When all done — agents disappear from dashboard\\n\\n## Agent Roles\\n\\nRoles define what an agent does, which skills it needs, what statuses it drives, and its definition of done.\\n\\n### Role Registry\\n\\nRoles are defined in `.roles/<role-name>/` directories:\\n\\n```\\n.roles/\\n├── developer/\\n│   ├── role.md        # frontmatter + markdown body\\n│   └── skills.md      # optional: skill list\\n├── researcher/\\n│   ├── role.md\\n│   └── skills.md\\n├── tester/\\n│   └── role.md\\n└── ...\\n```\\n\\n**role.md frontmatter format:**\\n\\n```yaml\\n---\\nname: developer\\ndescription: Writes code, fixes bugs, writes tests\\narchetype: implementer          # implementer | analyst | reviewer | tester\\nstart_status: development       # FIRST child command sets this status after launch\\nend_status: to-review           # role handoff status; for producers this means ready for review, not done\\ndod:                            # definition of done — added to board checklist on spawn\\n  - Code written per task description and AC\\n  - text: Relevant tests written for new or changed behavior and passing\\n    classes: [code]\\n  - text: Relevant build/validation commands run after changes and build not broken\\n    classes: [code]\\nskills:                         # optional: inline skill list (merged with skills.md)\\n  - go-tui-testing-tools\\n---\\n\\n# developer\\n\\n## Description\\nWrites code — features, bugfixes, refactoring. Writes tests for the code produced.\\n\\n## Standing Orders\\n1. Add or update relevant tests for changed behavior.\\n2. Run the relevant tests before handoff.\\n3. Run the relevant build or validation command after changes.\\n\\n## Deliverable\\nCode + tests.\\n...\\n```\\n\\nEvery board element has a `task_class`: `code` (the default), `metadata`, `docs`, or `research`. Set it with `create(..., task_class=metadata)` or `set_details(ID, task_class=metadata)`, and inspect it through the `task_class` q projection.\\n\\nRole `dod` entries may stay as plain strings, which apply to every task class, or use an object with `text` and `classes`. Spawn injects object entries only when the element's `task_class` appears in `classes`; an omitted or empty `classes` list preserves the legacy all-classes behavior.\\n\\n**skills.md format** (optional, merged with `skills:` in frontmatter):\\n```markdown\\n## Required\\n- project-management\\n- go-tui-testing-tools\\n\\n## Domain\\n- core-data (when working on persistence)\\n- swiftui (when building UI)\\n```\\n\\n**Resolution order** (`LoadRole`):\\n1. Project-local directory: `<workDir>/.roles/<name>/role.md`\\n2. Project-local single file: `<workDir>/.roles/<name>.md`\\n3. Global directory: `~/.roles/<name>/role.md`\\n4. Global single file: `~/.roles/<name>.md`\\n\\nDirectory format takes priority over single file. Project-local takes priority over global.\\n\\n**Global role installation:** `scripts/setup.sh` copies the bundled roles from `.roles/` into `~/.roles/`, making them available in any project without a local `.roles/` directory.\\n\\n**Available roles:**\\n\\n| Role | Archetype | Start → End Status |\\n|------|-----------|-------------------|\\n| `researcher` | analyst | `analysis` → `to-review` |\\n| `developer` | implementer | `development` → `to-review` |\\n| `tester` | tester | `development` → `to-review` |\\n| `solution-architect` | analyst | `analysis` → `to-review` |\\n| `doc-writer` | implementer | `development` → `to-review` |\\n| `reviewer` | reviewer | `reviewing` → verdict-driven (`done` / `to-dev` / `analysis` / `blocked`) |\\n| `orchestrator` | analyst | orchestration-only, no default status transition |\\n| `coordinator` | analyst | orchestration-only, no default status transition |\\n\\nThe reviewer role intentionally has no unconditional `end_status`. Its role body selects exactly one verdict branch, so the default spawn template cannot overwrite a requested-changes or stop-the-line verdict with `done`. Only an accepted review sets `done`.\\n\\n### How Roles Work with Spawn\\n\\n**`--role`, `--background`, and `--model` are REQUIRED; Codex and Qwen also require `--reasoning-effort`.** Spawning without a role produces a crippled agent with no identity, skills, DoD, or lifecycle commands. Model and reasoning are deliberate caller choices and never inherit task-board defaults. `--agent` remains optional: pass a human/task-context provider only when the effective `preferred_agentic_system` allows it; omission uses the exclusive pin, an in-set mixed affinity, legacy preference, or plain affinity as applicable.\\n\\nThe spawn pipeline:\\n1. Loads role config from `.roles/<role>/role.md`\\n2. Loads task metadata (README + precondition resources)\\n3. Computes pre-launch board updates:\\n   - Sets agent name with archetype prefix, e.g. `[implementer] developer (codex)`\\n   - Adds DoD items as checklist entries on the board element\\n4. Assembles prompt from template: role body, skills, DoD, status commands, task metadata\\n5. Persists prompt and spawn-run state under `.temp/prompts/` and `.temp/spawn-runs/RUN-.../`\\n6. Returns the `RUN-...` handle immediately\\n7. Background runner claims a queue slot under `spawn.max_parallel`, launches the real child agent, streams logwork, and executes completion hooks\\n8. Agent gets FIRST command (set `start_status` after launch) and LAST role-handoff command (set end status) in prompt\\n\\n```bash\\n# Spawn researcher for a research task (status → analysis)\\ntask-board spawn TASK-260218-abc --role researcher --background --agent codex --model gpt-5.6-sol --reasoning-effort max\\n\\n# Spawn developer for implementation (status → development, DoD → checklist)\\ntask-board spawn TASK-260218-def --role developer --background --agent codex --model gpt-5.6-sol --reasoning-effort max\\n\\n# Agent type follows the active schedule window, canonical provider system, legacy preference, then runtime affinity; model/reasoning remain explicit\\ntask-board spawn TASK-260218-xyz --role developer --background --model gpt-5.6-terra --reasoning-effort max\\n\\n# Spawn reviewer (status → reviewing)\\ntask-board spawn TASK-260218-ghi --role reviewer --background --agent codex --model gpt-5.6-sol --reasoning-effort max\\n\\n# Spawn orchestrator/coordinator for decomposition / orchestration / verification\\ntask-board spawn TASK-260218-jkl --role orchestrator --background --agent codex --model gpt-5.6-sol --reasoning-effort max\\ntask-board spawn TASK-260218-jkl --role coordinator --background --agent codex --model gpt-5.6-sol --reasoning-effort max\\n```\\n\\n`orchestrator` / `coordinator` is the special supervisor role: it decomposes settled specs into board elements inline, creates or refines tasks, spawns specialists, guards architecture, and owns producer -> reviewer -> rework -> reviewer cycles until accepted `done`. It must not create a board-layout meta-task, stop at `to-review`, ask the human to perform routine review, or do specialist implementation directly when delegation is appropriate. Spawn `solution-architect` only for genuine architecture decisions, not to perform the board layout.\\n\\nFor coordinator orchestration:\\n- fan out children with explicit role/model selections; inspect `project_config().spawn.preferred_agentic_system`, omit `--agent` for exclusive/in-set-affinity resolution, and pass it only for an allowed human/task-context provider\\n- keep the returned `RUN-...` ids\\n- use `task-board spawn status/wait` as explicit synchronization points\\n- sequence dependent work: wait for producer runs, verify outcome resources, then spawn reviewer/consumer runs\\n- on requested changes, preserve the verdict, spawn focused rework, and send the next handoff through another reviewer until accepted `done`\\n- if a child exits before handoff or parks in an intermediate status, inspect its run/outcomes and retry or reroute through a focused child; do not hand recoverable work to the human\\n- use `blocked` only for a concrete external blocker or unresolved human-only platform/product/architecture/tradeoff/approval decision, after recording evidence, failed assumptions/attempts, viable alternatives and tradeoffs, the recommendation, and the exact decision or external input needed\\n- do not shell-background `task-board spawn`; that duplicates the async layer and makes completion handling worse\\n\\n### Skill Injection\\n\\nSkills are injected as references (paths), not inlined into prompts. Claude, Codex, and Qwen use lazy body loading — only skill metadata (name + description) loads at startup (~50-200 tokens per skill). Treat spawned-role skill lists as lazy catalogs: full SKILL.md bodies load only when relevant to the concrete task scope, unless the user, task, or project explicitly requires one.\\n\\nThis means one `developer` role can reference many skills (swiftui, core-data, go-testing-tools, etc.) without context bloat — the agent picks what's needed for the specific task. See [`references/context-efficiency.md`](references/context-efficiency.md) for the selection rule.\\n\\n## Sub-Agent Prompt Templates\\n\\nFor sub-agent prompt templates (implementation agent and coordinator review workflow), see [`references/sub-agent-templates.md`](references/sub-agent-templates.md). Its legacy decomposition-agent template must not be used for spec-to-board layout. A `solution-architect` child produces only a genuine architecture decision; the Orchestrator applies that decision to the board inline.\\n\\nKey points covered there:\\n- Reality check: how to maximize sub-agent compliance with task-board updates\\n- Do not use the legacy decomposition-agent template for board layout; delegate only the architecture-decision deliverable that informs later inline board refinement\\n- Implementation agent template (for execution phase)\\n- Coordinator review workflow (approve / return for rework)\\n\\n## Spawning Tracked Background Runs\\n\\n**Tracked background children are separate CLI processes** (`claude -p`, `codex exec`, or `qwen` stream-JSON), each with its own context window. They are not internal sub-agents. Use explicit `task-board spawn` model selection to launch with automatic board integration and `RUN-...` tracking.\\n\\n**`--role`, `--background`, and `--model` are required; Codex and Qwen also require `--reasoning-effort`.** The role drives identity, skills, status lifecycle, and definition of done. See [`.specs/spawn-model-selection.md`](.specs/spawn-model-selection.md) for the no-default invariant.\\n\\nAgent type is a separate precedence layer. Canonical `preferred_agentic_system` enforces an exclusive pin or mixed allow-set; explicit `--agent` wins only inside it, and mixed otherwise uses in-set runtime affinity. An active UTC schedule window replaces that base system. Without the canonical system or active window, a usable legacy `spawn.preferred_agent` is selected and otherwise runtime affinity applies (`codex` from Codex, `claude` from Claude). A legacy preferred provider without a configured ceiling or executable falls back to affinity and records the path. After provider choice, the matching per-agent/role ceiling resolves model and any Codex/Qwen effort; a ceiling never changes the provider.\\n\\nUse `task-board q 'project_config()'` as the preflight check before spawn or spawn-contract review. `spawn.enabled=false` is an absolute project gate; `model_criterion=equal` forces the configured per-agent model without ranking it and forbids selection assessments. `spawn.ceilings.<agent>.roles.<role>` may override `model`, `model_criterion`, and `reasoning_effort` field by field for that role, with omitted fields inherited from the agent ceiling. `less_or_equal` and `greater_or_equal` compare provider-local `policyRank` values, require a deliberate one-line `--selection-rationale`, record structural `(pair, text)` history per task and role, and require exact content-bound `--ack-resolved` plus whitespace-normalized fresh rationale text after a requested-to-resolved change. Treat [`references/tracked-background-spawn.md`](references/tracked-background-spawn.md) as the canonical operator-facing contract. The durable wait/completion invariant lives in [`.specs/spawn-runtime-completion.md`](.specs/spawn-runtime-completion.md).\\n\\n`project_config().codex.session.auto_continue` surfaces the effective Codex anti-stall defaults for this cwd. Those settings currently apply only to the optional `task-board codex` session enhancement; they do not restrict goal-bound Codex spawn from an `agents-infra codex` primary session. Explicit `task-board codex --auto-continue*` flags override config when provided.\\n\\n**Template-based prompt composition is board-centric:** `spawn` composes the prompt from the role template plus board state:\\n- task description comes from the element README\\n- task-specific DoD comes from checklist items already present on the element\\n- role baseline DoD is appended after task-specific DoD\\n- precondition resources carry additional instructions and artifacts\\n\\nHidden legacy `--task-path` / `--dod-path` flags are compatibility-only appenders. They are not the normal workflow and should not be treated as required task inputs.\\n\\nIf the element has no task-specific DoD on the board, spawn should fail with a clear validation error instead of launching an underspecified agent.\\n\\nThe prompt also includes:\\n1. Role config (identity, skills, DoD, status commands)\\n2. Task README fields (title, scope, AC)\\n3. Precondition resources attached to the task (loaded and injected into the prompt)\\n4. A Go template (project-local `.agents/spawn-template.md` or embedded default)\\n\\n**Orchestrator monitoring:** A valid explicit spawn creates a tracked background runner, persists run state under `.temp/spawn-runs/`, and returns a `RUN-...` identifier immediately. The response includes coordinator-facing observation hints (text and `--json observation.*`) with a seeded resume cursor (`RUN-ID:0`) and watch/events/wait commands that preserve the active executable plus board context. `spawn events` replays durable events, `spawn watch` follows the stream, and `spawn wait` observes persisted terminal state. Watcher notifications do not inject text into the active orchestrator session unless a runtime-specific adapter exists. Codex sessions launched through optional `task-board codex` get terminal updates pushed into the active thread; this session UX does not govern goal-bound owner launch. Claude Code sessions get the same non-blocking effect by running `spawn wait RUN-...` as a harness background task that wakes the session on child completion. The background runner blocks on the real child, executes the completion hook, and only then marks the run terminal.\\n\\n**Project spawn policy:** local profiles use `task-board.config.json -> spawn`; remote profiles use `task-board.config.json -> remote.spawn` and fall back to root `spawn` when `remote.spawn` is missing or `null` for backward compatibility.\\n`spawn.enabled` defaults to `true`; `false` rejects every spawn before board\\nlookup, mutation, prompt/manifest creation, or runner registration.\\n`spawn.launch_composition.enabled` defaults to `true`; explicit `false` is the\\nonly opt-out. After provider selection, task-board invokes\\n`agents-infra compose --agent codex|claude|qwen --project DIR --schema-version 1 --json`\\nonce for a new spawn, validates the complete MCP-only contract and provider\\nallowlist, and prepends the accepted prefix to ordinary and goal-bound child\\nlaunches. Missing, pre-contract, unsupported, failing, or invalid infra is an\\noptional-capability degradation: launch the unchanged bare child and persist a\\nsanitized `disabled`, `empty`, `applied`, or `degraded_*` status. Do not retry\\ncomposition in v1 and do not fail the spawn for this capability.\\n\\n**Project MCP inheritance is a soft `relux-agents-infra` dependency.** It\\nrequires `agents-infra compose` to implement\\n`agents-infra.child-launch-composition` schema version `1` and return a\\nsuccessful (`status: ok`) JSON document. `scripts/setup.sh` probes that exact\\nCodex-shaped command and warns—not fails—when the binary is absent or the\\nresponse is incompatible. Board installation and bare child launch remain\\nfully functional in that state; only MCP inheritance is skipped, with\\nsanitized launch-composition evidence recorded for the child.\\n\\nThe private run manifest may carry the validated MCP argv snapshot for the\\ndetached runner and its restart/reroute/recovery successors. Public JSON under\\n`launchComposition`, task notes, state, and events must omit argv, URLs,\\ncommands, command arguments, headers, stderr, and environment values. Preserve\\nonly MCP server names and referenced environment-variable names; bearer values\\nremain solely in the inherited child process environment. Never apply\\nprimary-session model, reasoning, profile, approval, sandbox, service-tier,\\nprompt, or goal settings from infra composition.\\n`spawn.max_parallel` limits how many tracked background runners may actively\\nexecute child-agent work at once in one local work directory; when omitted, the default limit is `20`. It is not a cross-client remote-board scheduler.\\nCanonical `spawn.preferred_agentic_system` selects and constrains the provider\\nbefore ceiling resolution: exclusive pins one; mixed accepts an explicit in-set\\nprovider or in-set affinity. Without it, legacy usable `spawn.preferred_agent`\\nand then runtime affinity apply. A provider ceiling gates only legacy preference\\nusability; after selection, ceilings constrain rather than replace the provider.\\nFor each provider, `spawn.ceilings.<agent>.model_criterion: equal` forces the\\nconfigured registered model and records the exact-model adjustment without any\\n`policyRank` comparison. `less_or_equal` clamps above the configured\\nprovider-local rank and `greater_or_equal` raises below it; ordered selections\\nuse the deliberate rationale and adjusted-resolution acknowledgement contract.\\nOptional `spawn.ceilings.<agent>.roles.<role>` objects override the agent\\nceiling field by field for that spawn role. `project_config()` reports the raw\\nconfigured overrides and `spawn.config_key_prefix`, while spawn selection audits\\nreport the merged effective ceiling and the role-level config key.\\n\\n**Codex anti-stall policy:** project-local defaults for interactive Codex sessions live in `task-board.config.json -> codex.session.auto_continue`. Use that section for team defaults, and override per launch with `task-board codex --auto-continue`, `--auto-continue-delay`, `--auto-continue-max-nudges`, or `--auto-continue-prompt`. This v1 scope is `task-board codex` only, not generic `spawn --agent codex`.\\n\\n**Observed load-smoke:** with `spawn.max_parallel = 20`, a local smoke run of `30` fake-codex tasks yielded `20 running / 10 queued` on the first sample, then `20 completed / 10 running / 0 queued`, then full completion with `0` failures. Treat this as the expected queue shape for small homogeneous tasks.\\n\\nFor spawn flags, audit hygiene, template customization, orchestrator patterns, failure modes, and manual launch fallback — see [`references/tracked-background-spawn.md`](references/tracked-background-spawn.md).\\n\\n---\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n/Users/iv/Developer/ReluxWorks/curator/.task-board/.resources/TASK-260728-1a52au/TASK-260728-1a52au_spawn-log_-implementer--doc-writer--codex-_RUN-260727-a527c3.log:15:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_6\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"sed -n '401,800p' /Users/iv/.claude/skills/project-management/SKILL.md\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"## File Formats\\n\\nEach element directory contains two files:\\n- **README.md** — static description (what the element is, scope, acceptance criteria). Does not change during execution.\\n- **progress.md** — dynamic lifecycle journal (status, Task/Bug estimate, assignment, timestamps, dependencies, checklist, notes). Updated throughout execution.\\n\\nBoth files use source-aware, injection-safe Markdown persistence. Unchanged\\nbytes and custom sections are retained during rewrites; free-text payload fields\\nround-trip byte-exact. Mutation boundaries normalize identifier-like fields\\n(`AssignedTo`, `BlockedBy`, `Blocks`) by trimming boundary whitespace and reject\\nempty or reserved `(none)` values with a typed error. Checklist text is rejected\\nonly when empty-after-trim and is otherwise persisted verbatim.\\n\\nTask/bug dependencies are bidirectional ground truth. Story/epic ordering is derived from the live task DAG at read time; task links never materialize container side-effect links. Explicit stored container links remain manual state, while projections expose task-derived blockers separately.\\n\\nFor detailed format specifications, field descriptions, and examples, see [`references/file-formats.md`](references/file-formats.md).\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Resources & Bugs\\n\\nResources are linked to board elements via DSL mutations. Two types:\\n- **Precondition** — inputs for the task (instructions, specs, design docs). Used by `task-board spawn` to compose agent prompts.\\n- **Outcome** — work products (research results, implementation notes). Persisted for other agents/tasks to consume.\\n\\n```bash\\n# Create resource\\ntask-board m 'add_resource(TASK-260218-abc123, name=TASK-260218-abc123_design.md, content=\\\"...\\\", type=precondition, description=\\\"Architecture design\\\")'\\ntask-board m 'add_resource(TASK-260218-abc123, name=TASK-260218-abc123_results.md, content=\\\"...\\\", type=outcome, description=\\\"Research findings\\\")'\\n\\n# Replace existing resource\\ntask-board m 'update_resource(TASK-260218-abc123, name=TASK-260218-abc123_results.md, content=\\\"revised findings\\\", type=outcome, description=\\\"Research findings v2\\\")'\\n\\n# Delete resource\\ntask-board m --confirm 'delete_resource(TASK-260218-abc123, name=TASK-260218-abc123_design.md)'\\n```\\n\\nResources are stored flat in `.task-board/.resources/<elementID>/` and referenced from `progress.md`. Bugs live inside stories alongside tasks.\\n\\nFor resource workflow and bug tracking — see [`references/resources-and-bugs.md`](references/resources-and-bugs.md).\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Workflow\\n\\n### Full Development Cycle (MANDATORY)\\n\\nWhen the user asks to build/implement something, the agent MUST follow this end-to-end flow:\\n\\n#### Spec\\n- Specs live in the `.spec/` directory (one or more `.md` files), not in a single `SPEC.md`\\n- Create `.spec/` if it doesn't exist; add focused spec files per topic/feature (e.g. `.spec/auth.md`, `.spec/api.md`)\\n- Clarify requirements with the user if anything is ambiguous\\n- `.spec/` is the source of truth — all work traces back to it\\n\\n#### Plan on the Board\\n- Create epics and stories: `task-board m 'create(type=epic, name=..., description=\\\"...\\\")'`\\n- Refine each element: query with `task-board q 'get(ID) { full }'`, clarify gaps, update via `task-board m 'set_details(ID, description=\\\"...\\\")'`\\n- Perform spec-to-board decomposition inline as the Orchestrator; do not create a meta-task or spawn `solution-architect` merely to lay out a settled spec\\n- No element should have placeholder text — description and AC must be complete and unambiguous\\n\\n#### Story Detailing, Phase Planning, Execution (MANDATORY)\\n\\nAfter the Orchestrator completes story detailing inline, use [`references/planning-workflow.md`](references/planning-workflow.md) for phase planning, user approval, pre-launch checks, specialist execution, supervision, and visualization. Its legacy decomposition-spawn examples do not override this inline mandate and must not be used for settled-spec board layout.\\n\\nKey points:\\n- **Story detailing before planning** — the Orchestrator creates and refines the required stories and tasks inline before phase planning\\n- **No meta-delegated board layout** — do not create a meta-task or spawn `solution-architect` solely to translate a settled spec into board elements; reserve that role for genuine architecture decisions such as system-design tradeoffs or unresolved cross-module contracts\\n- **Use `task-board q 'plan(...)'` as the only planning source** — default JSON returns nested phases and `criticalPath`; compact output returns flat `waves` and a single-line `criticalPath`. Unresolvable dependencies fail with a typed validation error instead of producing misleading phases. Agents never need a rendered graph to plan. Use `task-board plan --save` only to persist a human-readable snapshot; never build phases manually or write plan files by hand\\n- **Dump plan to `.planning/`** — after planning, save to `.planning/{YYMMDD}_{HHmmss}_{topic}.md` for session recovery. Use `task-board plan --save`\\n- **User approval MANDATORY** — never start execution without user confirmation\\n- **Pre-launch checklist** — model, status, assignment, concrete IDs, first command\\n- **Coordinator is SUPERVISOR** — never writes code, only delegates to sub-agents\\n\\n#### Research / Analysis Tasks\\n\\nResearch workflow, resource linking, document structure, status flow — see [`references/research-workflow.md`](references/research-workflow.md).\\n\\nKey points:\\n- Research results are linked to board elements via `task-board m 'add_resource(..., type=outcome)'` on first write and `update_resource(...)` on revisions\\n- Broadly useful research also persists in **`.research/`** directory (project-level)\\n- Status flow: `backlog → analysis → to-review → reviewing → done` (skip `development`; after `to-review`, spawn an explicit reviewer such as `task-board spawn TASK-... --role reviewer --background --agent codex --model gpt-5.6-sol --reasoning-effort max`)\\n- Always link research output as an outcome resource on the task, and include the task ID in the resource name\\n\\n### Iterative Refinement (for individual elements)\\n\\nWhen creating ANY element (epic/story/task/bug), the agent MUST:\\n\\n1. **Create** element via `task-board m 'create(type=..., name=..., description=\\\"...\\\")'`\\n2. **Read** the element: `task-board q 'get(ID) { full }'`\\n3. **Clarify** — if description is vague or has gaps, ask the user\\n4. **Update** via `task-board m 'set_details(ID, description=\\\"...\\\", ac=\\\"...\\\")'`\\n5. **Repeat** steps 2-4 until description is exhaustive\\n6. Element is **ready** only when description and AC are complete and unambiguous\\n\\n### Project Start\\n\\n- Create elements: `task-board m 'create(type=epic, name=auth-system, description=\\\"...\\\")'`\\n- Create stories: `task-board m 'create(type=story, parent=EPIC-260218-abc, name=login)'`\\n- Detail tasks: `task-board m 'create(type=task, parent=STORY-260218-abc, name=impl)'`\\n- Set dependencies: `task-board m 'link(STORY-260218-def, blocked_by=STORY-260218-abc)'`\\n- **Generate phases**: `task-board q 'plan(EPIC-260218-abc, mode=children)'` — only after all stories have tasks and dependencies are set\\n\\n### Spawning Agents for Tasks\\n\\nThe **coordinator never writes code** — it delegates through tracked spawn commands with explicit role/model selection and runtime-affine agent selection.\\n\\nTreat [`references/tracked-background-spawn.md`](references/tracked-background-spawn.md) as the canonical operator-facing spawn contract. The guidance below is the workflow summary agents should follow.\\n\\n**`--role`, `--background`, and `--model` are REQUIRED.** Codex and Qwen spawns also require `--reasoning-effort`; Claude spawns do not accept it. Choose the role based on the task:\\n\\n`--agent` is optional by design. Canonical `spawn.preferred_agentic_system` is an enforced tagged union: `exclusive` pins one provider; `mixed` permits a normalized provider set and retains runtime affinity when it belongs to that set. An explicit `--agent` wins only within the configured set; an out-of-set explicit provider or affinity is refused before task lookup or spawn side effects and names the allowed providers. When the canonical system is absent, legacy `spawn.preferred_agent` retains its ceiling/executable-gated preference and affinity fallback; when both are absent, a Codex orchestrator session spawns a `codex` child and a Claude orchestrator session spawns a `claude` child. The implementation detects Codex from `CODEX_SESSION`, `CODEX_THREAD_ID`, or `CODEX_CI` and otherwise uses Claude.\\n\\nOptional `spawn.schedule.windows[]` overlays that base provider system during disjoint UTC windows. Each window is start-inclusive/end-exclusive, uses `HH:MM`, may cross midnight, and carries its own `preferred_agentic_system`; equal or overlapping bounds are invalid. The active window replaces the base system before provider and registered-model validation, while gaps use the base system or unrestricted runtime affinity when no base exists. Inspect `project_config().spawn.schedule` for the configured windows, `active_window`, and `base_preferred_agentic_system` before selecting a provider.\\n\\n### Goal-Capable Primary Sessions\\n\\nThe fixed primary-session UX is `agents-infra codex` for Codex and\\n`agents-infra claude` for Claude. Both are fully capable of starting\\ngoal-bound tracked owners through `task-board spawn --goal-scope`.\\n\\n- `agents-infra` remains board-agnostic: it has no `task-board` dependency and\\n  does not create, discover, or preserve a caller goal plane.\\n- `task-board codex` is an optional enhanced Codex primary session. It is never\\n  required to own a goal-bound tracked run.\\n- `CODEX_THREAD_ID`, `CODEX_SESSION`, and `CODEX_CI` select Codex affinity only;\\n  they do not identify an endpoint or form an addressable caller goal plane.\\n\\n| Capability | `agents-infra codex` primary | `task-board codex` optional primary | `agents-infra claude` primary |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| Launch the initial orchestrator | Required Codex UX | Optional, not required UX | Required Claude UX |\\n| Start a goal-bound tracked owner | Yes | Yes | Yes |\\n| Bind the native goal before owner work | Dedicated Codex app-server/thread | Dedicated Codex app-server/thread | Fresh Claude process with native `/goal` |\\n| Preserve an existing caller goal | Zero caller RPCs when plane is absent | Exact snapshot/restore when plane is reachable | Not applicable; no caller-session rewrite |\\n| Query/upsert active goal, directives, acceptance, and recovery | Yes | Yes | Yes |\\n| Durable `events`, `watch`, and `wait` observation | Yes | Yes | Yes |\\n| Push terminal state into an active Codex thread | No | Yes | No Codex push; Claude harness background wait wakes the session |\\n| Codex auto-continue nudges | No | Yes | Not a Codex capability |\\n| Caller-session app-server RPC access | No supported endpoint | Yes | Not applicable |\\n\\nParity is behavioral, not mechanical: both providers create a fresh tracked\\nowner, bind a native continuation condition before delivery work, and share the\\nsame immutable revision, scope, directive, recovery, and acceptance contract.\\nCodex uses a dedicated app-server thread; Claude uses a fresh `/goal` process.\\nSee [`references/tracked-background-spawn.md`](references/tracked-background-spawn.md)\\nfor the operator contract.\\n\\n| Task type | Role |\\n|-----------|------|\\n| Research, analysis, investigation | `--role researcher` |\\n| Code implementation, bugfixes | `--role developer` |\\n| Writing tests | `--role tester` |\\n| Genuine architecture decisions: system-design tradeoffs or unresolved cross-module contracts | `--role solution-architect` |\\n| Documentation | `--role doc-writer` |\\n| Code review, quality check | `--role reviewer` |\\n\\n**Workflow:**\\n\\n1. **Preflight runtime** — run `task-board q 'project_config()'` before spawning so you know whether this cwd is using a local board or a remote profile and whether `spawn.enabled` permits launches. If it is `false`, do not attempt a spawn or a flag-based workaround; the project policy must be changed first.\\n2. **Resolve the agent, then select deliberately for quality per token** — inspect `project_config().spawn.preferred_agentic_system` and the active `spawn.schedule` window. Honor an explicit human/task-context request with `--agent` only when it belongs to the effective set; otherwise omit it so `exclusive` pins its provider or `mixed` retains an in-set runtime affinity. Legacy `preferred_agent` remains a best-effort compatibility path. For the selected agent, assess scope, complexity, risk, required autonomy, validation burden, and expected context and token cost; inspect `task-board q --format compact 'models(agent=<selected-agent>)'`; then choose the strongest suitable explicit model and compatible reasoning effort for Codex or Qwen. Task-board supplies no model or reasoning defaults. A configured `model_criterion: equal` resolves every explicit request to that selected agent's configured model without relying on model ranks.\\n3. **Prepare task** — ensure element has clear title, description, scope, AC\\n4. **Add task-specific DoD on the board** — checklist items on the element are the task-specific DoD that spawn validates before launch\\n   ```bash\\n   task-board m 'add_checklist_item(TASK-260218-abc, text=\\\"Handle edge case X\\\")'\\n   task-board m 'add_checklist_item(TASK-260218-abc, text=\\\"Preserve existing API response shape\\\")'\\n   ```\\n5. **Link instruction** (optional) — add precondition resource:\\n   ```bash\\n   # Inline text instruction\\n   task-board m 'add_resource(TASK-260218-abc, name=TASK-260218-abc_impl-guide.md, content=\\\"...\\\", type=precondition, description=\\\"Implementation guide\\\")'\\n\\n   # File or URL attachment\\n   task-board resource add TASK-260218-abc ./TASK-260218-abc_impl-guide.md --type precondition\\n\\n   # Chat attachment only (no local path yet) — requires relux-agents-infra:\\n   # https://github.com/relux-works/relux-agents-infra\\n   agents-attachments materialize\\n   task-board resource add TASK-260218-abc \\\"$(agents-attachments path attachment-001)\\\" --name TASK-260218-abc_attachment-001.bin --type precondition\\n   ```\\n6. **Spawn with the effective provider system, affinity, or an in-set explicit override** — after reading `project_config()`, omit `--agent` when `exclusive` should pin its provider or `mixed` contains the current affinity. Pass `--agent` for a human/task-context provider only when the effective set allows it. With no canonical system or legacy preference, `task-board spawn TASK-260218-abc --role developer --background --model gpt-5.6-sol --reasoning-effort max` follows runtime affinity and auto-composes the prompt from role + README + checklist DoD + precondition resources\\n   - From a Claude-affine orchestrator with no preference, the equivalent shape is `task-board spawn TASK-260218-abc --role developer --background --model claude-opus-4-8`\\n   - With instruction validation: `task-board spawn TASK-260218-abc --role developer --background --model gpt-5.6-sol --reasoning-effort max --instruction TASK-260218-abc_impl-guide.md`\\n   - Explicit override from either runtime: `task-board spawn TASK-260218-abc --role reviewer --background --agent claude --model claude-opus-4-8`\\n   - Before the first producer launch or delivery status mutation for an approved pool, start its tracked owner: `task-board spawn STORY-260218-auth --role orchestrator --goal-scope TASK-260218-a,TASK-260218-b --background --agent codex --model gpt-5.6-sol --reasoning-effort max`\\n   - A goal-bound Codex owner runs in a dedicated app-server thread created by its tracked runner, regardless of whether the primary session came from `agents-infra codex` or `task-board codex`. The goal is bound there before the first turn. If no task-board caller endpoint is declared, preflight persists `caller_goal_plane=absent` and performs zero caller RPCs; a complete reachable declaration uses the exact snapshot/restore transaction. Partial or unreachable declarations fail closed before spawn side effects. Nested producer/reviewer launches therefore cannot replace the parent delivery condition. Do not replace this with an ordinary prompt-only `codex exec` launch.\\n   - A goal-bound child must remain inside the latest active scope. On typed `goal_scope_mismatch`, run the exact revision-bound `task-board spawn goal upsert \\\"$TASK_BOARD_RUN_ID\\\" --if-revision ... --add-scope ... --reason ...` remediation and retry; never silently absorb work.\\n   - A declared parent run context never downgrades silently. While Claude `successor_required` is pending, nested spawn fails side-effect-free with `goal_provider_binding_pending`; if the declared run cannot be loaded, it fails as `goal_parent_context_unavailable`. Retry only from the active rebound successor context.\\n7. **Capture the run id** — tracked background spawn returns `RUN-...`; use it as the coordinator handle for that child execution\\n   - CLI output now prints non-blocking monitor commands with a seeded cursor (`RUN-ID:0`) and preserves the active `task-board` executable / board context\\n   - JSON output exposes the same self-contained contract under `observation` (`cursor`, `eventsCommand`, `watchCommand`, `waitCommand`, `runtime`, `delivery`, `inSessionDelivery`)\\n8. **Monitor** — `task-board spawn status RUN-...`, `task-board spawn events RUN-... --cursor ...`, `task-board spawn watch RUN-...`, `task-board spawn wait RUN-...`, and `task-board q --format compact 'agents()'`\\n   - Directive plane: `task-board spawn directives RUN-...` to inspect durable operator directives and `task-board spawn directive RUN-... <kind>` to request `cancel`, `pause_after_checkpoint`, `restart`, `reroute_after_checkpoint`, `request_progress`, or `nudge`\\n   - Goal plane: `task-board spawn goal RUN-...` before nested spawn, at directive checkpoints, and before success claims. It returns the current objective, resolved leaf scope, and optimistic revision.\\n   - Owner-agent upsert exposes no goal kind or predicate flags. Creation uses `--scope`; updates require `--if-revision` and use only `--add-scope` and/or `--objective-intent`, with a reason for every non-idempotent revision. The mutation response includes `previous_revision`: `0` for creation, the replaced active revision for a change, and the current revision for an idempotent replay.\\n   - If Claude reports `successor_required` for a goal created by an already-running unbound owner, stop normally at the next safe checkpoint. The tracked runner creates and starts the rebound native-`/goal` successor automatically; the predecessor is superseded, never accepted delivery, and cannot launch nested work while rebinding is pending.\\n   - Restart and same-semantics reroute are owner-agent recovery actions that retain the active immutable revision. Operator-only cancellation requires a non-empty reason. Structural reroute uses `reroute_after_checkpoint --goal-scope ... --goal-kind ... --goal-success-predicate ... --goal-objective-intent ... --payload ...`; role compatibility is validated before directive persistence.\\n9. **Review via child run** — when a producer sets `to-review`, immediately spawn and monitor a reviewer with explicit model selection; never hand routine review to the human\\n10. **Own rework through acceptance** — on changes requested, preserve the verdict as rework context, spawn the appropriate producer, wait for a new `to-review`, and spawn another reviewer. Repeat until accepted `done`\\n11. **Recover early exits and parked states** — inspect run events and outcome evidence, then retry or reroute through a focused child; mark `blocked` only when the evidence proves a genuine stop-the-line boundary\\n12. **Escalate only a real boundary** — ask the human only for a concrete external blocker or unresolved human-only platform/product/architecture/tradeoff/approval decision; first record the evidence, failed assumptions/attempts, viable alternatives and tradeoffs, recommendation, and exact decision or external input needed\\n\\nSpawn is task-centric:\\n- Primary inputs are task ID + role\\n- `task-board q 'project_config()'` is the supported preflight check before spawning or auditing\\n- Canonical provider selection is enforced as `preferred_agentic_system`: explicit in-set `--agent`, otherwise exclusive pin or in-set mixed affinity. Without it, legacy usable `preferred_agent` and then runtime affinity retain compatibility.\\n- A legacy preference with no provider ceiling or unavailable executable falls back to affinity without hard failure; response and manifest selection evidence record `agentSelectionPath` / `agent_selection_path`, preferred agent, and fallback reason\\n- An explicit human or task-context agent request must be passed as `--agent`; it wins inside the canonical allow-set and is refused with typed allowed-provider evidence when outside it\\n- Per-agent ceilings are applied only after the agent is selected. Role identity is resolved before ceiling lookup: boundary whitespace and case are normalized, legacy `coordinator` shares the `orchestrator` policy identity, and path-like or unknown roles fail closed with typed errors. Affinity or explicit `--agent` picks the provider; `spawn.ceilings.<agent>` and any role override pin or constrain the model/reasoning fields for that provider and never switch providers\\n- Model selection is always explicit; Codex and Qwen reasoning effort is always explicit and must be supported by the selected model\\n- `spawn.enabled` defaults to `true`; `false` rejects every spawn before board lookup, task mutation, prompt/manifest creation, or runner launch\\n- `spawn.launch_composition.enabled` also defaults to `true`; explicit `false` skips project MCP composition, while unavailable/old/failing/invalid infra keeps the existing bare child launch and records sanitized degradation evidence\\n- launch composition is MCP-only. It must not import primary-session model, reasoning, profile, approval, sandbox, service tier, prompt, or goal policy. Bearer values stay in the inherited process environment; only referenced environment-variable names are safe evidence\\n- `spawn.ceilings.<agent>.model_criterion: equal` means the configured model is exact, not a ranked upper bound. It requires no selection assessment and does not accept `--selection-rationale` or `--ack-resolved`.\\n- `model_criterion: less_or_equal` clamps requests above the configured provider-local `policyRank`; `greater_or_equal` raises requests below it. Reasoning effort follows the same bound direction using the selected provider's ordered vocabulary: Codex supports `low < medium < high < xhigh < max < ultra`, while Qwen supports `low < medium < high < xhigh < max`.\\n- Every ordered-criterion spawn requires `--selection-rationale` with one non-empty line explaining the deliberate pair. Task-board records rationale as `(pair_it_justifies, text)` tuples scoped to the task and spawn role. Pass-through and accepted adjusted tuples are stored in the run manifest and task notes; the first adjusted invocation records the requested-pair tuple in task notes before returning the acknowledgement checkpoint.\\n- An ordered pass-through needs no acknowledgement. If model or effort changes, task-board reports `requested -> resolved`, records the initial rationale against the requested pair, and stops without assignment, prompt/run creation, or runner launch. Reassess the resolved pair against scope, risk, autonomy, and validation burden; re-request within bounds or escalate if inadequate. Otherwise re-invoke with exact `--ack-resolved <model>/<effort>` (Claude uses the model ID alone) and a fresh one-line rationale for the resolved pair. After whitespace normalization, its text must differ byte-for-byte from every rationale already recorded for that task and role; the CLI binds pairs structurally and does not infer rationale semantics.\\n- Task description comes from the board README\\n- Task-specific DoD comes from the board checklist\\n- Role baseline DoD is appended after task-specific DoD\\n- Precondition resources carry additional instructions, screenshots, specs, and artifacts\\n- Hidden legacy `--task-path` and `--dod-path` flags are compatibility-only appenders; they do not replace the board README/checklist workflow and should not be part of the normal contract\\n- Spawn returns a tracked `RUN-...` handle; use `task-board spawn wait RUN-...` only after a run exists and the parent needs an explicit synchronization barrier\\n- Standalone `spawn wait` and fused `spawn --wait` exit zero only for `completed`; `failed` and `cancelled` runs return non-zero, while PID-aware stale repair prevents orphaned queued/running/paused runs from hanging wait/follow observers\\n- Logwork files and system log resources are scoped by task plus `RUN-...`, preserving concurrent same-role run evidence independently\\n- Spawn output includes coordinator observation hints for non-blocking monitoring (`watch`, `events --cursor RUN-ID:0`, `wait`) and keeps them runnable in the same executable / board context that produced the run\\n- `task-board spawn --json` exposes the same contract in `observation.*`; `observation.inSessionDelivery=true` only when the current Codex orchestrator session was launched through `task-board codex`\\n- `observation.runtime` / `observation.delivery` report the orchestrator runtime and its non-blocking completion channel: `codex-session`/`codex-in-session` (app-server push), `claude-session`/`claude-background-wait` (harness background wait wakes the session), or `external`/`external-watch`\\n- Spawn run events use replay cursor tokens (`RUN-ID:seq`) so local CLI, TUI, and remote watchers can resume from the same durable position\\n- Spawn directives are first-class durable records with acknowledgement state; use `spawn directive` / `spawn directives` instead of ad hoc notes for cancel/pause/restart/reroute intent\\n- Goal-bound directive output always includes the full current `active_goal`; each new directive retains its immutable issued `goal_ref`, and legacy records report unknown context rather than guessing\\n- Provider usage/budget limits, transient launch/transport failures, nonzero goal-owner exits, unmet role-handoff status/evidence, missing reviewer verdict branches, and unmet delivery acceptance create a same-revision autonomous successor instead of a terminal failed owner\\n- If successor log preparation or runner startup fails, the successor stays non-terminal `queued` with a persisted deferred-start retry lease. Status/wait/follow observation reacquires an expired lease and retries the same successor without duplicating ownership; never treat a failed start attempt as loss of the same-revision owner.\\n- A role-handoff goal completes only at its immutable role end status with a new or updated task-scoped outcome artifact. Zero exit while parked in `analysis`/`development` or without that evidence is recoverable and queues the same revision again.\\n- Required/light delivery acceptance is `done` plus a producer outcome and a later reviewer run with a persisted evidence-backed `accepted` branch. `changes_requested`, Stop-The-Line, and branchless reviewer exits do not count as acceptance. `review=none` still requires `done`, producer outcome evidence, and an orchestrator evidence check after the latest producer run\\n- Provider `blocked` and board `blocked` role exits share one evidence gate. Only board `blocked` plus a new or updated task-scoped Stop-The-Line packet may terminate with `completion_reason=stop_boundary`; this is never successful delivery. Missing evidence queues the same revision for recovery.\\n- Watcher notifications are external observation surfaces by default; `task-board codex` adds runtime-native terminal delivery back into the active Codex thread\\n- Claude orchestrator sessions (`CLAUDECODE` env set) have no injection API; the non-blocking channel is the harness itself — run each printed `task-board spawn wait RUN-...` as a harness background task (`run_in_background`), fan out one background wait per run, and react as each completion wakes the session. See [`references/tracked-background-spawn.md`](references/tracked-background-spawn.md) Pattern E\\n- Single-child variant: fused `task-board spawn TASK ... --wait` registers the tracked run identically and blocks in-process to terminal state with `spawn wait` exit semantics — one background Bash call per child; the `run_in_background` wrapper itself remains a caller/harness decision. See [`references/tracked-background-spawn.md`](references/tracked-background-spawn.md) \\\"Fused Spawn And Wait\\\"\\n- Soft directives are cooperative checkpoint signals only. Spawned agents can inspect them through `TASK_BOARD_RUN_ID` + `task-board spawn directives \\\"$TASK_BOARD_RUN_ID\\\"`; do not assume live in-session push\\n- Coordinators must not wrap a plain `task-board spawn` in `&`, `nohup`, or runtime background wrappers; `--background` already returns a tracked run. The exception is the fused blocking `spawn ... --wait` barrier, which a Claude orchestrator deliberately runs as a harness background task\\n- Spawn validates that the board task brief is usable: description and acceptance criteria must be real, not placeholders or one-line fluff\\n- Repeated spawn on the same task must be idempotent: existing injected checklist items are not duplicated\\n- When auditing spawn docs/help/source, exclude `.temp/**` from repo-wide searches so stale task-scoped worktrees do not look like current contract files\\n\\n### Review Policy\\n\\nEvery board element has a `review` policy. Set it at creation with `create(..., review=required|light|none)`, change it with `set_details(ID, review=...)`, and inspect it through the `review` q projection. New elements and existing elements without the field default to `required`.\\n\\n- `required` keeps the current producer → reviewer → rework/reviewer loop. A producer handoff at `to-review` must be routed to a reviewer child, and only an accepted reviewer verdict reaches `done`.\\n- `light` still requires a reviewer child, but reviewer selection should favor a cheaper effort tier that can satisfy the same evidence and quality gates. This is orchestration guidance, not a hard CLI enforcement rule.\\n- `none` lets the Orchestrator accept a producer handoff without spawning a reviewer child. After checking the task-specific acceptance criteria, checklist, validation results, and outcome evidence, the Orchestrator may move the element from `to-review` directly to `done` with `set_status`. Missing or inadequate evidence must be routed back to the producer; `none` never means accepting an unevidenced handoff.\\n\\nThis subsection qualifies unconditional reviewer-routing guidance elsewhere in the workflow: that guidance describes the default `required` policy. Human review is not introduced by any policy value.\\n\\n### Model Selection Guide\\n\\n**Agent selection happens before model selection.** Inspect `project_config()` first. Canonical `preferred_agentic_system.exclusive` pins one provider; `mixed` permits an explicit in-set `--agent` or the current in-set affinity. Out-of-set requests fail before side effects. When no canonical system exists, legacy usable `preferred_agent` and then runtime affinity preserve compatibility. Do not hardcode a provider in reusable orchestration instructions.\\n\\nProject ceilings constrain the already selected provider; they never replace it. Canonical provider-system membership does not require a ceiling, while legacy `spawn.preferred_agent` still requires the corresponding provider ceiling to be usable. After provider selection, the matching `spawn.ceilings.<agent>` policy and optional role override pin or constrain model and, for Codex or Qwen, reasoning effort.\\n\\n**`model_criterion=equal` exception.** Inspect the effective target agent/role ceiling in `project_config()` first. When it reports `model_criterion=equal`, skip the per-spawn model selection assessment entirely: do not add `Spawn selection assessment` paragraphs to task notes or resources, and do not pass `--selection-rationale` or `--ack-resolved`. Use the exact configured model and the configured Codex or Qwen reasoning effort, when present, as the pinned pair, passing every required model/effort flag explicitly. A single short note naming the pinned pair at the first spawn is allowed; do not repeat it for later spawns.\\n\\n**Ordered-criterion deliberate selection.** For `less_or_equal` or `greater_or_equal`, write exactly one non-empty rationale line and pass it as `--selection-rationale`. If the request passes through, spawn directly and omit `--ack-resolved`. If policy changes model or effort, the CLI records `(requested_pair, rationale)` in task notes and returns the typed requested-to-resolved checkpoint without creating a run. Deliberately reassess the resolved pair. If it remains fit, re-invoke with exact `--ack-resolved <model>/<effort>` and a fresh rationale line for the resolved pair; after whitespace normalization that text must differ byte-for-byte from every rationale previously recorded for the same task and role. The accepted tuple is `(resolved_pair, fresh_rationale)` and the manifest carries the tuple history. If the resolved pair is inadequate, request another in-bounds pair or escalate the ceiling constraint. Content quality remains the caller's responsibility; the CLI enforces structural pair binding and freshness rather than semantic text analysis.\\n\\n```bash\\n# First ordered request; rationale is recorded when the pair passes through.\\ntask-board spawn TASK-260218-abc --role developer --background --model gpt-5.6-terra --reasoning-effort max --selection-rationale \\\"gpt-5.6-terra at max fits the bounded implementation and validation burden\\\"\\n\\n# After a reported adjustment to gpt-5.6-luna/max, reassess and acknowledge that exact pair.\\ntask-board spawn TASK-260218-abc --role developer --background --model gpt-5.6-terra --reasoning-effort max --selection-rationale \\\"gpt-5.6-luna at max remains adequate for this bounded change and its test gates\\\" --ack-resolved gpt-5.6-luna/max\\n```\\n\\n**Choose explicitly for maximum task quality per token.** Before every board spawn, assess scope, complexity, risk, required autonomy, validation burden, and expected context and token cost. Then select the strongest suitable pair that can pass the existing quality gates; do not reflexively spend the strongest model or highest supported effort.\\n\\n| Task type | Agent | Model | --reasoning-effort | Notes |\\n|-----------|-------|-------|--------------------|-------|\\n| Cross-module, novel, risky, or review-critical work | codex | `gpt-5.6-sol` | **max** | Highest recommended board-managed effort; preserve predictable delegation in task-board |\\n| Balanced agentic work | codex | `gpt-5.6-terra` | **max** | Strong fit for routine work with meaningful autonomy or validation |\\n| Fast, affordable agentic work | codex | `gpt-5.6-luna` | **max** | Luna does not support `ultra` |\\n| Complex coding and research | codex | `gpt-5.5` | **xhigh** | Does not support `max` or `ultra` |\\n| Everyday coding | codex | `gpt-5.4` | **xhigh** | Strong general coding option for narrower scope |\\n| Simpler cost-sensitive work | codex | `gpt-5.4-mini` | **xhigh** | Use only when bounded scope and clear validation retain the same quality gate |\\n| Ultra-fast code iteration | codex | `gpt-5.3-codex-spark` | **xhigh** | Speed-oriented coding model |\\n| Hardest, longest-running Claude work | claude | `claude-fable-5` | - | Most capable Claude model; `--model` is still required; Claude rejects reasoning effort |\\n| Everyday complex Claude work | claude | `claude-opus-4-8` | - | Recommended Claude default for board children |\\n| Routine Claude work | claude | `claude-sonnet-5` | - | Efficient for routine, well-scoped tasks |\\n| Fast, cheap Claude checks | claude | `claude-haiku-4-5` | - | Fastest for quick answers and bounded checks |\\n| Balanced Qwen Token Plan work | qwen | `qwen3.7-plus` | **xhigh** | Recommended registered Qwen Code model; query current context and Team seat/quota metadata |\\n| Highest-capability Qwen preview work | qwen | `qwen3.8-max-preview` | **xhigh** | Preview model; use only when the preview tradeoff fits the task |\\n\\nFor board-managed work, `max` is the highest recommended effort. `ultra` remains explicitly compatible on supported models, but it enables model-internal delegation rather than deeper reasoning. Prefer task-board's tracked `RUN-...` lifecycle, role assignment, dependencies, and reviewer routing as the predictable delegation plane. Lower-cost pairs are good choices only when they can still pass the same required tests, build/validation, outcome, and review gates.\\n\\nCurrent Codex values are `low`, `medium`, `high`, `xhigh` (UI: Extra high), `max`, and `ultra`. Compatibility is model-specific; query `models(agent=codex)` before launch. Deprecated model IDs may retain legacy `minimal`, but it is not a current recommendation.\\n\\nCurrent Qwen Code client values are `low`, `medium`, `high`, `xhigh`, and `max`; task-board passes the selected tier through Qwen's stream-JSON initialize control frame. Query `models(agent=qwen)` for registered model IDs, Qwen Code context windows, supported effort values, and current Token Plan Team seat pricing, monthly quotas, frequency-limit semantics, and plan applicability. Qwen ordinary child runs are tracked like Codex/Claude runs, but Qwen goal-bound owner launches are not supported.\\n\\n**Budget: unlimited by default.** Don't pass `--budget` unless the user explicitly asks to cap spending. Let agents finish their work — a $5 cap on a $3 task means a failed half-done agent + a retry that costs $6 total. Unlimited is cheaper than retries.\\n\\nThe CLI blocks missing model, missing Codex/Qwen reasoning, reasoning on Claude, and known incompatible model/effort pairs before board lookup or mutation. Unknown explicit model IDs remain forward-compatible and produce a warning; known wrong-agent model IDs also warn.\\n\\n### Working on a Task (as sub-agent)\\n\\n1. Estimate + assign + start → `task-board m 'set_estimate(TASK-260218-abc, estimate=estimated, scale=fibonacci, number=5); assign(TASK-260218-abc, agent=agent-name); set_status(TASK-260218-abc, status=development)'`\\n2. Work until the task is genuinely complete or objectively blocked. Do not stop with the task parked in `analysis`, `development`, `testing`, or `reviewing` just because one local action finished.\\n3. Stop and escalate if completion would require a forced fit: document the constraint, failed assumption, viable options, and exact human/product/architecture decision needed. Do not pile on compensating tests, stubs, flags, or priority rules to make an invalid model appear to work.\\n4. Update checklist → `task-board m 'check_item(TASK-260218-abc, item=1)'`\\n5. Save results → `task-board m 'add_resource(TASK-260218-abc, name=TASK-260218-abc_results.md, content=\\\"...\\\", type=outcome, description=\\\"Implementation notes\\\")'`\\n6. Ready for review → `task-board m 'set_status(TASK-260218-abc, status=to-review)'`\\n7. Or blocked → only for a concrete external blocker or unresolved human-only stop-the-line decision; attach the evidence, failed assumptions/attempts, viable alternatives and tradeoffs, recommendation, and exact decision or external input needed, then set `blocked`\\n8. Orchestrator confirms producer evidence, spawns a tracked reviewer child (`task-board spawn TASK-260218-abc --role reviewer --background --agent codex --model gpt-5.6-sol --reasoning-effort max`), waits or monitors the returned `RUN-...`, then routes by the reviewer-child verdict: accepted → `done`; changes requested → the appropriate producer through `to-dev` or `analysis`, followed by a new reviewer; genuine stop-the-line boundary → `blocked` with the full evidence packet\\n\\nHuman-facing language is part of the contract: a producer ending at `to-review` says \\\"ready for review\\\" or \\\"handed off to review\\\", not \\\"done\\\"/\\\"complete\\\"/\\\"finished\\\"/\\\"final\\\"/\\\"готово\\\". Only a reviewer/coordinator may call the board task done, and only after the board status is actually `done`.\\n\\n### Dependencies\\n\\nTask/bug dependencies are execution ground truth. An unfinished stored\\n`blockedBy` link makes `isBlocked=true`, and status enforcement refuses to start\\nthat task or bug. For story/epic containers, only an unfinished explicitly\\nstored manual link is a hard block and makes `isBlocked=true`.\\n\\nNew Task/Bug records also require an explicit estimated value before their\\nfirst transition into `development`. Query `{ estimate }` to inspect the nested\\nenum and use `set_estimate(...)` to change it. An absent Estimate section is a\\nlegacy persistence state and does not activate the gate retroactively.\\nStory and Epic estimates are never persisted or mutated. Their `{ estimate }`\\nprojection rolls up descendant Task/Bug values to `estimated(fibonacci(sum))`,\\nor to `partiallyEstimated` with the known `rolledEstimate`, an `N/total`\\n`notEstimatedCount`, and sorted `unestimatedTaskIds`.\\n\\nCross-container waiting derived from the live task DAG is advisory scheduling\\nstate: it is never persisted, never changes `isBlocked`, and never adds a\\ncontainer-level status-enforcement rule. Query projections expose active\\nadvisory waiting as `isAdvisoryBlocked=true` and identify its source through\\n`derivedBlockedBy`. `summary()` and `list(blocked=true)` report both hard and\\nadvisory entries and annotate each with `isBlocked` and\\n`isAdvisoryBlocked`; a mixed container has both flags set. Use these annotations\\nbefore deciding whether an orchestrator is seeing manual intent or computed\\nordering.\\n\\n```bash\\n# TASK-B depends on TASK-A\\ntask-board m 'link(TASK-260218-def, blocked_by=TASK-260218-abc)'\\n\\n# CLI will refuse to move TASK-B to development while TASK-A is not done\\ntask-board m 'set_status(TASK-260218-def, status=development)'\\n# Error: cannot start TASK-260218-def — blocked by: TASK-260218-abc\\n\\n# Remove dependency\\ntask-board m 'unlink(TASK-260218-def, blocked_by=TASK-260218-abc)'\\n\\n# Inspect hard and advisory scheduling constraints together\\ntask-board q --format compact 'list(blocked=true) { id isBlocked isAdvisoryBlocked blockedBy derivedBlockedBy }'\\n```\\n\\n### Board Review\\n\\n```bash\\n# Queries (use --format compact for token efficiency)\\ntask-board q 'schema()'                                                          # discover fields, presets, operations\\ntask-board q 'project_config()'                                                  # confirm runtime, launch gate, and model criterion before agent work\\ntask-board q --format compact 'summary()'                                        # quick overview\\ntask-board q --format compact 'get(TASK-260218-abc) { full }'                    # full element details\\ntask-board q --format compact 'list(type=task, status=development) { overview }'  # what's in progress\\ntask-board q --format compact 'list(type=task, blocked=true) { id name status }'  # what's blocked\\ntask-board q --format compact 'agents()'                                         # agent dashboard\\ntask-board q --format compact 'plan(EPIC-260218-abc, mode=children)'             # execution waves (super-compact planning shape)\\ntask-board q 'plan(scope=[EPIC-260218-abc, TASK-260218-def], mode=children)'     # plan over a set of subtrees (union)\\n\\n# Full-text search (local only)\\ntask-board grep \\\"AudioRecorder\\\"\\n\\n# Validation (local only)\\ntask-board validate\\ntask-board repair-links\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Querying & Mutating the Board\\n\\nThree primary access modes following the [agent-facing-api](https://github.com/relux-works/skill-agent-facing-api) pattern — all through one CLI binary:\\n\\n- **`q` (query DSL)** — structured reads: `task-board q --format compact 'list(type=task, status=development) { overview }'`\\n- **`grep` (full-text search)** — scoped text search: `task-board grep \\\"AudioRecorder\\\"` (local only)\\n- **`m` (mutation DSL)** — structured writes: `task-board m 'set_status(TASK-260218-abc, status=done)'`\\n\\nPlus a CLI plan facade for pretty-print / render / save: `task-board plan ...`, and local-only utilities such as `validate` (structure check).\\n\\nField presets: `minimal` (id, status), `default` (id, name, status), `overview` (+ assignee, parent), `full` (all 27 fields in the current schema; use `schema()` for the evolving list), `progress` (workflow/progress fields, including estimate), `planning` (status, dependencies, parent).\\n\\nWhen mentioning board elements in human-facing responses, always include both the concrete ID and the human-readable title/name. This applies to epics, stories, tasks, and bugs. ID-only references are not enough for people scanning the chat because they should not have to resolve identifiers manually.\\n\\nFor DSL operations, mutations reference, field projection, and decision table — see [`references/querying.md`](references/querying.md).\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Tips\\n\\n- **Detail stories before planning** — create tasks for all stories before phase planning, so the plan shows real scope\\n- **AC must be verifiable** — \\\"works\\\" is bad, \\\"test passes\\\" is good\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n/Users/iv/Developer/ReluxWorks/curator/.task-board/.resources/TASK-260728-1a52au/TASK-260728-1a52au_spawn-log_-implementer--doc-writer--codex-_RUN-260727-a527c3.log:21:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_9\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"sed -n '401,800p' /Users/iv/.claude/skills/project-management/SKILL.md\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"## File Formats\\n\\nEach element directory contains two files:\\n- **README.md** — static description (what the element is, scope, acceptance criteria). Does not change during execution.\\n- **progress.md** — dynamic lifecycle journal (status, Task/Bug estimate, assignment, timestamps, dependencies, checklist, notes). Updated throughout execution.\\n\\nBoth files use source-aware, injection-safe Markdown persistence. Unchanged\\nbytes and custom sections are retained during rewrites; free-text payload fields\\nround-trip byte-exact. Mutation boundaries normalize identifier-like fields\\n(`AssignedTo`, `BlockedBy`, `Blocks`) by trimming boundary whitespace and reject\\nempty or reserved `(none)` values with a typed error. Checklist text is rejected\\nonly when empty-after-trim and is otherwise persisted verbatim.\\n\\nTask/bug dependencies are bidirectional ground truth. Story/epic ordering is derived from the live task DAG at read time; task links never materialize container side-effect links. Explicit stored container links remain manual state, while projections expose task-derived blockers separately.\\n\\nFor detailed format specifications, field descriptions, and examples, see [`references/file-formats.md`](references/file-formats.md).\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Resources & Bugs\\n\\nResources are linked to board elements via DSL mutations. Two types:\\n- **Precondition** — inputs for the task (instructions, specs, design docs). Used by `task-board spawn` to compose agent prompts.\\n- **Outcome** — work products (research results, implementation notes). Persisted for other agents/tasks to consume.\\n\\n```bash\\n# Create resource\\ntask-board m 'add_resource(TASK-260218-abc123, name=TASK-260218-abc123_design.md, content=\\\"...\\\", type=precondition, description=\\\"Architecture design\\\")'\\ntask-board m 'add_resource(TASK-260218-abc123, name=TASK-260218-abc123_results.md, content=\\\"...\\\", type=outcome, description=\\\"Research findings\\\")'\\n\\n# Replace existing resource\\ntask-board m 'update_resource(TASK-260218-abc123, name=TASK-260218-abc123_results.md, content=\\\"revised findings\\\", type=outcome, description=\\\"Research findings v2\\\")'\\n\\n# Delete resource\\ntask-board m --confirm 'delete_resource(TASK-260218-abc123, name=TASK-260218-abc123_design.md)'\\n```\\n\\nResources are stored flat in `.task-board/.resources/<elementID>/` and referenced from `progress.md`. Bugs live inside stories alongside tasks.\\n\\nFor resource workflow and bug tracking — see [`references/resources-and-bugs.md`](references/resources-and-bugs.md).\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Workflow\\n\\n### Full Development Cycle (MANDATORY)\\n\\nWhen the user asks to build/implement something, the agent MUST follow this end-to-end flow:\\n\\n#### Spec\\n- Specs live in the `.spec/` directory (one or more `.md` files), not in a single `SPEC.md`\\n- Create `.spec/` if it doesn't exist; add focused spec files per topic/feature (e.g. `.spec/auth.md`, `.spec/api.md`)\\n- Clarify requirements with the user if anything is ambiguous\\n- `.spec/` is the source of truth — all work traces back to it\\n\\n#### Plan on the Board\\n- Create epics and stories: `task-board m 'create(type=epic, name=..., description=\\\"...\\\")'`\\n- Refine each element: query with `task-board q 'get(ID) { full }'`, clarify gaps, update via `task-board m 'set_details(ID, description=\\\"...\\\")'`\\n- Perform spec-to-board decomposition inline as the Orchestrator; do not create a meta-task or spawn `solution-architect` merely to lay out a settled spec\\n- No element should have placeholder text — description and AC must be complete and unambiguous\\n\\n#### Story Detailing, Phase Planning, Execution (MANDATORY)\\n\\nAfter the Orchestrator completes story detailing inline, use [`references/planning-workflow.md`](references/planning-workflow.md) for phase planning, user approval, pre-launch checks, specialist execution, supervision, and visualization. Its legacy decomposition-spawn examples do not override this inline mandate and must not be used for settled-spec board layout.\\n\\nKey points:\\n- **Story detailing before planning** — the Orchestrator creates and refines the required stories and tasks inline before phase planning\\n- **No meta-delegated board layout** — do not create a meta-task or spawn `solution-architect` solely to translate a settled spec into board elements; reserve that role for genuine architecture decisions such as system-design tradeoffs or unresolved cross-module contracts\\n- **Use `task-board q 'plan(...)'` as the only planning source** — default JSON returns nested phases and `criticalPath`; compact output returns flat `waves` and a single-line `criticalPath`. Unresolvable dependencies fail with a typed validation error instead of producing misleading phases. Agents never need a rendered graph to plan. Use `task-board plan --save` only to persist a human-readable snapshot; never build phases manually or write plan files by hand\\n- **Dump plan to `.planning/`** — after planning, save to `.planning/{YYMMDD}_{HHmmss}_{topic}.md` for session recovery. Use `task-board plan --save`\\n- **User approval MANDATORY** — never start execution without user confirmation\\n- **Pre-launch checklist** — model, status, assignment, concrete IDs, first command\\n- **Coordinator is SUPERVISOR** — never writes code, only delegates to sub-agents\\n\\n#### Research / Analysis Tasks\\n\\nResearch workflow, resource linking, document structure, status flow — see [`references/research-workflow.md`](references/research-workflow.md).\\n\\nKey points:\\n- Research results are linked to board elements via `task-board m 'add_resource(..., type=outcome)'` on first write and `update_resource(...)` on revisions\\n- Broadly useful research also persists in **`.research/`** directory (project-level)\\n- Status flow: `backlog → analysis → to-review → reviewing → done` (skip `development`; after `to-review`, spawn an explicit reviewer such as `task-board spawn TASK-... --role reviewer --background --agent codex --model gpt-5.6-sol --reasoning-effort max`)\\n- Always link research output as an outcome resource on the task, and include the task ID in the resource name\\n\\n### Iterative Refinement (for individual elements)\\n\\nWhen creating ANY element (epic/story/task/bug), the agent MUST:\\n\\n1. **Create** element via `task-board m 'create(type=..., name=..., description=\\\"...\\\")'`\\n2. **Read** the element: `task-board q 'get(ID) { full }'`\\n3. **Clarify** — if description is vague or has gaps, ask the user\\n4. **Update** via `task-board m 'set_details(ID, description=\\\"...\\\", ac=\\\"...\\\")'`\\n5. **Repeat** steps 2-4 until description is exhaustive\\n6. Element is **ready** only when description and AC are complete and unambiguous\\n\\n### Project Start\\n\\n- Create elements: `task-board m 'create(type=epic, name=auth-system, description=\\\"...\\\")'`\\n- Create stories: `task-board m 'create(type=story, parent=EPIC-260218-abc, name=login)'`\\n- Detail tasks: `task-board m 'create(type=task, parent=STORY-260218-abc, name=impl)'`\\n- Set dependencies: `task-board m 'link(STORY-260218-def, blocked_by=STORY-260218-abc)'`\\n- **Generate phases**: `task-board q 'plan(EPIC-260218-abc, mode=children)'` — only after all stories have tasks and dependencies are set\\n\\n### Spawning Agents for Tasks\\n\\nThe **coordinator never writes code** — it delegates through tracked spawn commands with explicit role/model selection and runtime-affine agent selection.\\n\\nTreat [`references/tracked-background-spawn.md`](references/tracked-background-spawn.md) as the canonical operator-facing spawn contract. The guidance below is the workflow summary agents should follow.\\n\\n**`--role`, `--background`, and `--model` are REQUIRED.** Codex and Qwen spawns also require `--reasoning-effort`; Claude spawns do not accept it. Choose the role based on the task:\\n\\n`--agent` is optional by design. Canonical `spawn.preferred_agentic_system` is an enforced tagged union: `exclusive` pins one provider; `mixed` permits a normalized provider set and retains runtime affinity when it belongs to that set. An explicit `--agent` wins only within the configured set; an out-of-set explicit provider or affinity is refused before task lookup or spawn side effects and names the allowed providers. When the canonical system is absent, legacy `spawn.preferred_agent` retains its ceiling/executable-gated preference and affinity fallback; when both are absent, a Codex orchestrator session spawns a `codex` child and a Claude orchestrator session spawns a `claude` child. The implementation detects Codex from `CODEX_SESSION`, `CODEX_THREAD_ID`, or `CODEX_CI` and otherwise uses Claude.\\n\\nOptional `spawn.schedule.windows[]` overlays that base provider system during disjoint UTC windows. Each window is start-inclusive/end-exclusive, uses `HH:MM`, may cross midnight, and carries its own `preferred_agentic_system`; equal or overlapping bounds are invalid. The active window replaces the base system before provider and registered-model validation, while gaps use the base system or unrestricted runtime affinity when no base exists. Inspect `project_config().spawn.schedule` for the configured windows, `active_window`, and `base_preferred_agentic_system` before selecting a provider.\\n\\n### Goal-Capable Primary Sessions\\n\\nThe fixed primary-session UX is `agents-infra codex` for Codex and\\n`agents-infra claude` for Claude. Both are fully capable of starting\\ngoal-bound tracked owners through `task-board spawn --goal-scope`.\\n\\n- `agents-infra` remains board-agnostic: it has no `task-board` dependency and\\n  does not create, discover, or preserve a caller goal plane.\\n- `task-board codex` is an optional enhanced Codex primary session. It is never\\n  required to own a goal-bound tracked run.\\n- `CODEX_THREAD_ID`, `CODEX_SESSION`, and `CODEX_CI` select Codex affinity only;\\n  they do not identify an endpoint or form an addressable caller goal plane.\\n\\n| Capability | `agents-infra codex` primary | `task-board codex` optional primary | `agents-infra claude` primary |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| Launch the initial orchestrator | Required Codex UX | Optional, not required UX | Required Claude UX |\\n| Start a goal-bound tracked owner | Yes | Yes | Yes |\\n| Bind the native goal before owner work | Dedicated Codex app-server/thread | Dedicated Codex app-server/thread | Fresh Claude process with native `/goal` |\\n| Preserve an existing caller goal | Zero caller RPCs when plane is absent | Exact snapshot/restore when plane is reachable | Not applicable; no caller-session rewrite |\\n| Query/upsert active goal, directives, acceptance, and recovery | Yes | Yes | Yes |\\n| Durable `events`, `watch`, and `wait` observation | Yes | Yes | Yes |\\n| Push terminal state into an active Codex thread | No | Yes | No Codex push; Claude harness background wait wakes the session |\\n| Codex auto-continue nudges | No | Yes | Not a Codex capability |\\n| Caller-session app-server RPC access | No supported endpoint | Yes | Not applicable |\\n\\nParity is behavioral, not mechanical: both providers create a fresh tracked\\nowner, bind a native continuation condition before delivery work, and share the\\nsame immutable revision, scope, directive, recovery, and acceptance contract.\\nCodex uses a dedicated app-server thread; Claude uses a fresh `/goal` process.\\nSee [`references/tracked-background-spawn.md`](references/tracked-background-spawn.md)\\nfor the operator contract.\\n\\n| Task type | Role |\\n|-----------|------|\\n| Research, analysis, investigation | `--role researcher` |\\n| Code implementation, bugfixes | `--role developer` |\\n| Writing tests | `--role tester` |\\n| Genuine architecture decisions: system-design tradeoffs or unresolved cross-module contracts | `--role solution-architect` |\\n| Documentation | `--role doc-writer` |\\n| Code review, quality check | `--role reviewer` |\\n\\n**Workflow:**\\n\\n1. **Preflight runtime** — run `task-board q 'project_config()'` before spawning so you know whether this cwd is using a local board or a remote profile and whether `spawn.enabled` permits launches. If it is `false`, do not attempt a spawn or a flag-based workaround; the project policy must be changed first.\\n2. **Resolve the agent, then select deliberately for quality per token** — inspect `project_config().spawn.preferred_agentic_system` and the active `spawn.schedule` window. Honor an explicit human/task-context request with `--agent` only when it belongs to the effective set; otherwise omit it so `exclusive` pins its provider or `mixed` retains an in-set runtime affinity. Legacy `preferred_agent` remains a best-effort compatibility path. For the selected agent, assess scope, complexity, risk, required autonomy, validation burden, and expected context and token cost; inspect `task-board q --format compact 'models(agent=<selected-agent>)'`; then choose the strongest suitable explicit model and compatible reasoning effort for Codex or Qwen. Task-board supplies no model or reasoning defaults. A configured `model_criterion: equal` resolves every explicit request to that selected agent's configured model without relying on model ranks.\\n3. **Prepare task** — ensure element has clear title, description, scope, AC\\n4. **Add task-specific DoD on the board** — checklist items on the element are the task-specific DoD that spawn validates before launch\\n   ```bash\\n   task-board m 'add_checklist_item(TASK-260218-abc, text=\\\"Handle edge case X\\\")'\\n   task-board m 'add_checklist_item(TASK-260218-abc, text=\\\"Preserve existing API response shape\\\")'\\n   ```\\n5. **Link instruction** (optional) — add precondition resource:\\n   ```bash\\n   # Inline text instruction\\n   task-board m 'add_resource(TASK-260218-abc, name=TASK-260218-abc_impl-guide.md, content=\\\"...\\\", type=precondition, description=\\\"Implementation guide\\\")'\\n\\n   # File or URL attachment\\n   task-board resource add TASK-260218-abc ./TASK-260218-abc_impl-guide.md --type precondition\\n\\n   # Chat attachment only (no local path yet) — requires relux-agents-infra:\\n   # https://github.com/relux-works/relux-agents-infra\\n   agents-attachments materialize\\n   task-board resource add TASK-260218-abc \\\"$(agents-attachments path attachment-001)\\\" --name TASK-260218-abc_attachment-001.bin --type precondition\\n   ```\\n6. **Spawn with the effective provider system, affinity, or an in-set explicit override** — after reading `project_config()`, omit `--agent` when `exclusive` should pin its provider or `mixed` contains the current affinity. Pass `--agent` for a human/task-context provider only when the effective set allows it. With no canonical system or legacy preference, `task-board spawn TASK-260218-abc --role developer --background --model gpt-5.6-sol --reasoning-effort max` follows runtime affinity and auto-composes the prompt from role + README + checklist DoD + precondition resources\\n   - From a Claude-affine orchestrator with no preference, the equivalent shape is `task-board spawn TASK-260218-abc --role developer --background --model claude-opus-4-8`\\n   - With instruction validation: `task-board spawn TASK-260218-abc --role developer --background --model gpt-5.6-sol --reasoning-effort max --instruction TASK-260218-abc_impl-guide.md`\\n   - Explicit override from either runtime: `task-board spawn TASK-260218-abc --role reviewer --background --agent claude --model claude-opus-4-8`\\n   - Before the first producer launch or delivery status mutation for an approved pool, start its tracked owner: `task-board spawn STORY-260218-auth --role orchestrator --goal-scope TASK-260218-a,TASK-260218-b --background --agent codex --model gpt-5.6-sol --reasoning-effort max`\\n   - A goal-bound Codex owner runs in a dedicated app-server thread created by its tracked runner, regardless of whether the primary session came from `agents-infra codex` or `task-board codex`. The goal is bound there before the first turn. If no task-board caller endpoint is declared, preflight persists `caller_goal_plane=absent` and performs zero caller RPCs; a complete reachable declaration uses the exact snapshot/restore transaction. Partial or unreachable declarations fail closed before spawn side effects. Nested producer/reviewer launches therefore cannot replace the parent delivery condition. Do not replace this with an ordinary prompt-only `codex exec` launch.\\n   - A goal-bound child must remain inside the latest active scope. On typed `goal_scope_mismatch`, run the exact revision-bound `task-board spawn goal upsert \\\"$TASK_BOARD_RUN_ID\\\" --if-revision ... --add-scope ... --reason ...` remediation and retry; never silently absorb work.\\n   - A declared parent run context never downgrades silently. While Claude `successor_required` is pending, nested spawn fails side-effect-free with `goal_provider_binding_pending`; if the declared run cannot be loaded, it fails as `goal_parent_context_unavailable`. Retry only from the active rebound successor context.\\n7. **Capture the run id** — tracked background spawn returns `RUN-...`; use it as the coordinator handle for that child execution\\n   - CLI output now prints non-blocking monitor commands with a seeded cursor (`RUN-ID:0`) and preserves the active `task-board` executable / board context\\n   - JSON output exposes the same self-contained contract under `observation` (`cursor`, `eventsCommand`, `watchCommand`, `waitCommand`, `runtime`, `delivery`, `inSessionDelivery`)\\n8. **Monitor** — `task-board spawn status RUN-...`, `task-board spawn events RUN-... --cursor ...`, `task-board spawn watch RUN-...`, `task-board spawn wait RUN-...`, and `task-board q --format compact 'agents()'`\\n   - Directive plane: `task-board spawn directives RUN-...` to inspect durable operator directives and `task-board spawn directive RUN-... <kind>` to request `cancel`, `pause_after_checkpoint`, `restart`, `reroute_after_checkpoint`, `request_progress`, or `nudge`\\n   - Goal plane: `task-board spawn goal RUN-...` before nested spawn, at directive checkpoints, and before success claims. It returns the current objective, resolved leaf scope, and optimistic revision.\\n   - Owner-agent upsert exposes no goal kind or predicate flags. Creation uses `--scope`; updates require `--if-revision` and use only `--add-scope` and/or `--objective-intent`, with a reason for every non-idempotent revision. The mutation response includes `previous_revision`: `0` for creation, the replaced active revision for a change, and the current revision for an idempotent replay.\\n   - If Claude reports `successor_required` for a goal created by an already-running unbound owner, stop normally at the next safe checkpoint. The tracked runner creates and starts the rebound native-`/goal` successor automatically; the predecessor is superseded, never accepted delivery, and cannot launch nested work while rebinding is pending.\\n   - Restart and same-semantics reroute are owner-agent recovery actions that retain the active immutable revision. Operator-only cancellation requires a non-empty reason. Structural reroute uses `reroute_after_checkpoint --goal-scope ... --goal-kind ... --goal-success-predicate ... --goal-objective-intent ... --payload ...`; role compatibility is validated before directive persistence.\\n9. **Review via child run** — when a producer sets `to-review`, immediately spawn and monitor a reviewer with explicit model selection; never hand routine review to the human\\n10. **Own rework through acceptance** — on changes requested, preserve the verdict as rework context, spawn the appropriate producer, wait for a new `to-review`, and spawn another reviewer. Repeat until accepted `done`\\n11. **Recover early exits and parked states** — inspect run events and outcome evidence, then retry or reroute through a focused child; mark `blocked` only when the evidence proves a genuine stop-the-line boundary\\n12. **Escalate only a real boundary** — ask the human only for a concrete external blocker or unresolved human-only platform/product/architecture/tradeoff/approval decision; first record the evidence, failed assumptions/attempts, viable alternatives and tradeoffs, recommendation, and exact decision or external input needed\\n\\nSpawn is task-centric:\\n- Primary inputs are task ID + role\\n- `task-board q 'project_config()'` is the supported preflight check before spawning or auditing\\n- Canonical provider selection is enforced as `preferred_agentic_system`: explicit in-set `--agent`, otherwise exclusive pin or in-set mixed affinity. Without it, legacy usable `preferred_agent` and then runtime affinity retain compatibility.\\n- A legacy preference with no provider ceiling or unavailable executable falls back to affinity without hard failure; response and manifest selection evidence record `agentSelectionPath` / `agent_selection_path`, preferred agent, and fallback reason\\n- An explicit human or task-context agent request must be passed as `--agent`; it wins inside the canonical allow-set and is refused with typed allowed-provider evidence when outside it\\n- Per-agent ceilings are applied only after the agent is selected. Role identity is resolved before ceiling lookup: boundary whitespace and case are normalized, legacy `coordinator` shares the `orchestrator` policy identity, and path-like or unknown roles fail closed with typed errors. Affinity or explicit `--agent` picks the provider; `spawn.ceilings.<agent>` and any role override pin or constrain the model/reasoning fields for that provider and never switch providers\\n- Model selection is always explicit; Codex and Qwen reasoning effort is always explicit and must be supported by the selected model\\n- `spawn.enabled` defaults to `true`; `false` rejects every spawn before board lookup, task mutation, prompt/manifest creation, or runner launch\\n- `spawn.launch_composition.enabled` also defaults to `true`; explicit `false` skips project MCP composition, while unavailable/old/failing/invalid infra keeps the existing bare child launch and records sanitized degradation evidence\\n- launch composition is MCP-only. It must not import primary-session model, reasoning, profile, approval, sandbox, service tier, prompt, or goal policy. Bearer values stay in the inherited process environment; only referenced environment-variable names are safe evidence\\n- `spawn.ceilings.<agent>.model_criterion: equal` means the configured model is exact, not a ranked upper bound. It requires no selection assessment and does not accept `--selection-rationale` or `--ack-resolved`.\\n- `model_criterion: less_or_equal` clamps requests above the configured provider-local `policyRank`; `greater_or_equal` raises requests below it. Reasoning effort follows the same bound direction using the selected provider's ordered vocabulary: Codex supports `low < medium < high < xhigh < max < ultra`, while Qwen supports `low < medium < high < xhigh < max`.\\n- Every ordered-criterion spawn requires `--selection-rationale` with one non-empty line explaining the deliberate pair. Task-board records rationale as `(pair_it_justifies, text)` tuples scoped to the task and spawn role. Pass-through and accepted adjusted tuples are stored in the run manifest and task notes; the first adjusted invocation records the requested-pair tuple in task notes before returning the acknowledgement checkpoint.\\n- An ordered pass-through needs no acknowledgement. If model or effort changes, task-board reports `requested -> resolved`, records the initial rationale against the requested pair, and stops without assignment, prompt/run creation, or runner launch. Reassess the resolved pair against scope, risk, autonomy, and validation burden; re-request within bounds or escalate if inadequate. Otherwise re-invoke with exact `--ack-resolved <model>/<effort>` (Claude uses the model ID alone) and a fresh one-line rationale for the resolved pair. After whitespace normalization, its text must differ byte-for-byte from every rationale already recorded for that task and role; the CLI binds pairs structurally and does not infer rationale semantics.\\n- Task description comes from the board README\\n- Task-specific DoD comes from the board checklist\\n- Role baseline DoD is appended after task-specific DoD\\n- Precondition resources carry additional instructions, screenshots, specs, and artifacts\\n- Hidden legacy `--task-path` and `--dod-path` flags are compatibility-only appenders; they do not replace the board README/checklist workflow and should not be part of the normal contract\\n- Spawn returns a tracked `RUN-...` handle; use `task-board spawn wait RUN-...` only after a run exists and the parent needs an explicit synchronization barrier\\n- Standalone `spawn wait` and fused `spawn --wait` exit zero only for `completed`; `failed` and `cancelled` runs return non-zero, while PID-aware stale repair prevents orphaned queued/running/paused runs from hanging wait/follow observers\\n- Logwork files and system log resources are scoped by task plus `RUN-...`, preserving concurrent same-role run evidence independently\\n- Spawn output includes coordinator observation hints for non-blocking monitoring (`watch`, `events --cursor RUN-ID:0`, `wait`) and keeps them runnable in the same executable / board context that produced the run\\n- `task-board spawn --json` exposes the same contract in `observation.*`; `observation.inSessionDelivery=true` only when the current Codex orchestrator session was launched through `task-board codex`\\n- `observation.runtime` / `observation.delivery` report the orchestrator runtime and its non-blocking completion channel: `codex-session`/`codex-in-session` (app-server push), `claude-session`/`claude-background-wait` (harness background wait wakes the session), or `external`/`external-watch`\\n- Spawn run events use replay cursor tokens (`RUN-ID:seq`) so local CLI, TUI, and remote watchers can resume from the same durable position\\n- Spawn directives are first-class durable records with acknowledgement state; use `spawn directive` / `spawn directives` instead of ad hoc notes for cancel/pause/restart/reroute intent\\n- Goal-bound directive output always includes the full current `active_goal`; each new directive retains its immutable issued `goal_ref`, and legacy records report unknown context rather than guessing\\n- Provider usage/budget limits, transient launch/transport failures, nonzero goal-owner exits, unmet role-handoff status/evidence, missing reviewer verdict branches, and unmet delivery acceptance create a same-revision autonomous successor instead of a terminal failed owner\\n- If successor log preparation or runner startup fails, the successor stays non-terminal `queued` with a persisted deferred-start retry lease. Status/wait/follow observation reacquires an expired lease and retries the same successor without duplicating ownership; never treat a failed start attempt as loss of the same-revision owner.\\n- A role-handoff goal completes only at its immutable role end status with a new or updated task-scoped outcome artifact. Zero exit while parked in `analysis`/`development` or without that evidence is recoverable and queues the same revision again.\\n- Required/light delivery acceptance is `done` plus a producer outcome and a later reviewer run with a persisted evidence-backed `accepted` branch. `changes_requested`, Stop-The-Line, and branchless reviewer exits do not count as acceptance. `review=none` still requires `done`, producer outcome evidence, and an orchestrator evidence check after the latest producer run\\n- Provider `blocked` and board `blocked` role exits share one evidence gate. Only board `blocked` plus a new or updated task-scoped Stop-The-Line packet may terminate with `completion_reason=stop_boundary`; this is never successful delivery. Missing evidence queues the same revision for recovery.\\n- Watcher notifications are external observation surfaces by default; `task-board codex` adds runtime-native terminal delivery back into the active Codex thread\\n- Claude orchestrator sessions (`CLAUDECODE` env set) have no injection API; the non-blocking channel is the harness itself — run each printed `task-board spawn wait RUN-...` as a harness background task (`run_in_background`), fan out one background wait per run, and react as each completion wakes the session. See [`references/tracked-background-spawn.md`](references/tracked-background-spawn.md) Pattern E\\n- Single-child variant: fused `task-board spawn TASK ... --wait` registers the tracked run identically and blocks in-process to terminal state with `spawn wait` exit semantics — one background Bash call per child; the `run_in_background` wrapper itself remains a caller/harness decision. See [`references/tracked-background-spawn.md`](references/tracked-background-spawn.md) \\\"Fused Spawn And Wait\\\"\\n- Soft directives are cooperative checkpoint signals only. Spawned agents can inspect them through `TASK_BOARD_RUN_ID` + `task-board spawn directives \\\"$TASK_BOARD_RUN_ID\\\"`; do not assume live in-session push\\n- Coordinators must not wrap a plain `task-board spawn` in `&`, `nohup`, or runtime background wrappers; `--background` already returns a tracked run. The exception is the fused blocking `spawn ... --wait` barrier, which a Claude orchestrator deliberately runs as a harness background task\\n- Spawn validates that the board task brief is usable: description and acceptance criteria must be real, not placeholders or one-line fluff\\n- Repeated spawn on the same task must be idempotent: existing injected checklist items are not duplicated\\n- When auditing spawn docs/help/source, exclude `.temp/**` from repo-wide searches so stale task-scoped worktrees do not look like current contract files\\n\\n### Review Policy\\n\\nEvery board element has a `review` policy. Set it at creation with `create(..., review=required|light|none)`, change it with `set_details(ID, review=...)`, and inspect it through the `review` q projection. New elements and existing elements without the field default to `required`.\\n\\n- `required` keeps the current producer → reviewer → rework/reviewer loop. A producer handoff at `to-review` must be routed to a reviewer child, and only an accepted reviewer verdict reaches `done`.\\n- `light` still requires a reviewer child, but reviewer selection should favor a cheaper effort tier that can satisfy the same evidence and quality gates. This is orchestration guidance, not a hard CLI enforcement rule.\\n- `none` lets the Orchestrator accept a producer handoff without spawning a reviewer child. After checking the task-specific acceptance criteria, checklist, validation results, and outcome evidence, the Orchestrator may move the element from `to-review` directly to `done` with `set_status`. Missing or inadequate evidence must be routed back to the producer; `none` never means accepting an unevidenced handoff.\\n\\nThis subsection qualifies unconditional reviewer-routing guidance elsewhere in the workflow: that guidance describes the default `required` policy. Human review is not introduced by any policy value.\\n\\n### Model Selection Guide\\n\\n**Agent selection happens before model selection.** Inspect `project_config()` first. Canonical `preferred_agentic_system.exclusive` pins one provider; `mixed` permits an explicit in-set `--agent` or the current in-set affinity. Out-of-set requests fail before side effects. When no canonical system exists, legacy usable `preferred_agent` and then runtime affinity preserve compatibility. Do not hardcode a provider in reusable orchestration instructions.\\n\\nProject ceilings constrain the already selected provider; they never replace it. Canonical provider-system membership does not require a ceiling, while legacy `spawn.preferred_agent` still requires the corresponding provider ceiling to be usable. After provider selection, the matching `spawn.ceilings.<agent>` policy and optional role override pin or constrain model and, for Codex or Qwen, reasoning effort.\\n\\n**`model_criterion=equal` exception.** Inspect the effective target agent/role ceiling in `project_config()` first. When it reports `model_criterion=equal`, skip the per-spawn model selection assessment entirely: do not add `Spawn selection assessment` paragraphs to task notes or resources, and do not pass `--selection-rationale` or `--ack-resolved`. Use the exact configured model and the configured Codex or Qwen reasoning effort, when present, as the pinned pair, passing every required model/effort flag explicitly. A single short note naming the pinned pair at the first spawn is allowed; do not repeat it for later spawns.\\n\\n**Ordered-criterion deliberate selection.** For `less_or_equal` or `greater_or_equal`, write exactly one non-empty rationale line and pass it as `--selection-rationale`. If the request passes through, spawn directly and omit `--ack-resolved`. If policy changes model or effort, the CLI records `(requested_pair, rationale)` in task notes and returns the typed requested-to-resolved checkpoint without creating a run. Deliberately reassess the resolved pair. If it remains fit, re-invoke with exact `--ack-resolved <model>/<effort>` and a fresh rationale line for the resolved pair; after whitespace normalization that text must differ byte-for-byte from every rationale previously recorded for the same task and role. The accepted tuple is `(resolved_pair, fresh_rationale)` and the manifest carries the tuple history. If the resolved pair is inadequate, request another in-bounds pair or escalate the ceiling constraint. Content quality remains the caller's responsibility; the CLI enforces structural pair binding and freshness rather than semantic text analysis.\\n\\n```bash\\n# First ordered request; rationale is recorded when the pair passes through.\\ntask-board spawn TASK-260218-abc --role developer --background --model gpt-5.6-terra --reasoning-effort max --selection-rationale \\\"gpt-5.6-terra at max fits the bounded implementation and validation burden\\\"\\n\\n# After a reported adjustment to gpt-5.6-luna/max, reassess and acknowledge that exact pair.\\ntask-board spawn TASK-260218-abc --role developer --background --model gpt-5.6-terra --reasoning-effort max --selection-rationale \\\"gpt-5.6-luna at max remains adequate for this bounded change and its test gates\\\" --ack-resolved gpt-5.6-luna/max\\n```\\n\\n**Choose explicitly for maximum task quality per token.** Before every board spawn, assess scope, complexity, risk, required autonomy, validation burden, and expected context and token cost. Then select the strongest suitable pair that can pass the existing quality gates; do not reflexively spend the strongest model or highest supported effort.\\n\\n| Task type | Agent | Model | --reasoning-effort | Notes |\\n|-----------|-------|-------|--------------------|-------|\\n| Cross-module, novel, risky, or review-critical work | codex | `gpt-5.6-sol` | **max** | Highest recommended board-managed effort; preserve predictable delegation in task-board |\\n| Balanced agentic work | codex | `gpt-5.6-terra` | **max** | Strong fit for routine work with meaningful autonomy or validation |\\n| Fast, affordable agentic work | codex | `gpt-5.6-luna` | **max** | Luna does not support `ultra` |\\n| Complex coding and research | codex | `gpt-5.5` | **xhigh** | Does not support `max` or `ultra` |\\n| Everyday coding | codex | `gpt-5.4` | **xhigh** | Strong general coding option for narrower scope |\\n| Simpler cost-sensitive work | codex | `gpt-5.4-mini` | **xhigh** | Use only when bounded scope and clear validation retain the same quality gate |\\n| Ultra-fast code iteration | codex | `gpt-5.3-codex-spark` | **xhigh** | Speed-oriented coding model |\\n| Hardest, longest-running Claude work | claude | `claude-fable-5` | - | Most capable Claude model; `--model` is still required; Claude rejects reasoning effort |\\n| Everyday complex Claude work | claude | `claude-opus-4-8` | - | Recommended Claude default for board children |\\n| Routine Claude work | claude | `claude-sonnet-5` | - | Efficient for routine, well-scoped tasks |\\n| Fast, cheap Claude checks | claude | `claude-haiku-4-5` | - | Fastest for quick answers and bounded checks |\\n| Balanced Qwen Token Plan work | qwen | `qwen3.7-plus` | **xhigh** | Recommended registered Qwen Code model; query current context and Team seat/quota metadata |\\n| Highest-capability Qwen preview work | qwen | `qwen3.8-max-preview` | **xhigh** | Preview model; use only when the preview tradeoff fits the task |\\n\\nFor board-managed work, `max` is the highest recommended effort. `ultra` remains explicitly compatible on supported models, but it enables model-internal delegation rather than deeper reasoning. Prefer task-board's tracked `RUN-...` lifecycle, role assignment, dependencies, and reviewer routing as the predictable delegation plane. Lower-cost pairs are good choices only when they can still pass the same required tests, build/validation, outcome, and review gates.\\n\\nCurrent Codex values are `low`, `medium`, `high`, `xhigh` (UI: Extra high), `max`, and `ultra`. Compatibility is model-specific; query `models(agent=codex)` before launch. Deprecated model IDs may retain legacy `minimal`, but it is not a current recommendation.\\n\\nCurrent Qwen Code client values are `low`, `medium`, `high`, `xhigh`, and `max`; task-board passes the selected tier through Qwen's stream-JSON initialize control frame. Query `models(agent=qwen)` for registered model IDs, Qwen Code context windows, supported effort values, and current Token Plan Team seat pricing, monthly quotas, frequency-limit semantics, and plan applicability. Qwen ordinary child runs are tracked like Codex/Claude runs, but Qwen goal-bound owner launches are not supported.\\n\\n**Budget: unlimited by default.** Don't pass `--budget` unless the user explicitly asks to cap spending. Let agents finish their work — a $5 cap on a $3 task means a failed half-done agent + a retry that costs $6 total. Unlimited is cheaper than retries.\\n\\nThe CLI blocks missing model, missing Codex/Qwen reasoning, reasoning on Claude, and known incompatible model/effort pairs before board lookup or mutation. Unknown explicit model IDs remain forward-compatible and produce a warning; known wrong-agent model IDs also warn.\\n\\n### Working on a Task (as sub-agent)\\n\\n1. Estimate + assign + start → `task-board m 'set_estimate(TASK-260218-abc, estimate=estimated, scale=fibonacci, number=5); assign(TASK-260218-abc, agent=agent-name); set_status(TASK-260218-abc, status=development)'`\\n2. Work until the task is genuinely complete or objectively blocked. Do not stop with the task parked in `analysis`, `development`, `testing`, or `reviewing` just because one local action finished.\\n3. Stop and escalate if completion would require a forced fit: document the constraint, failed assumption, viable options, and exact human/product/architecture decision needed. Do not pile on compensating tests, stubs, flags, or priority rules to make an invalid model appear to work.\\n4. Update checklist → `task-board m 'check_item(TASK-260218-abc, item=1)'`\\n5. Save results → `task-board m 'add_resource(TASK-260218-abc, name=TASK-260218-abc_results.md, content=\\\"...\\\", type=outcome, description=\\\"Implementation notes\\\")'`\\n6. Ready for review → `task-board m 'set_status(TASK-260218-abc, status=to-review)'`\\n7. Or blocked → only for a concrete external blocker or unresolved human-only stop-the-line decision; attach the evidence, failed assumptions/attempts, viable alternatives and tradeoffs, recommendation, and exact decision or external input needed, then set `blocked`\\n8. Orchestrator confirms producer evidence, spawns a tracked reviewer child (`task-board spawn TASK-260218-abc --role reviewer --background --agent codex --model gpt-5.6-sol --reasoning-effort max`), waits or monitors the returned `RUN-...`, then routes by the reviewer-child verdict: accepted → `done`; changes requested → the appropriate producer through `to-dev` or `analysis`, followed by a new reviewer; genuine stop-the-line boundary → `blocked` with the full evidence packet\\n\\nHuman-facing language is part of the contract: a producer ending at `to-review` says \\\"ready for review\\\" or \\\"handed off to review\\\", not \\\"done\\\"/\\\"complete\\\"/\\\"finished\\\"/\\\"final\\\"/\\\"готово\\\". Only a reviewer/coordinator may call the board task done, and only after the board status is actually `done`.\\n\\n### Dependencies\\n\\nTask/bug dependencies are execution ground truth. An unfinished stored\\n`blockedBy` link makes `isBlocked=true`, and status enforcement refuses to start\\nthat task or bug. For story/epic containers, only an unfinished explicitly\\nstored manual link is a hard block and makes `isBlocked=true`.\\n\\nNew Task/Bug records also require an explicit estimated value before their\\nfirst transition into `development`. Query `{ estimate }` to inspect the nested\\nenum and use `set_estimate(...)` to change it. An absent Estimate section is a\\nlegacy persistence state and does not activate the gate retroactively.\\nStory and Epic estimates are never persisted or mutated. Their `{ estimate }`\\nprojection rolls up descendant Task/Bug values to `estimated(fibonacci(sum))`,\\nor to `partiallyEstimated` with the known `rolledEstimate`, an `N/total`\\n`notEstimatedCount`, and sorted `unestimatedTaskIds`.\\n\\nCross-container waiting derived from the live task DAG is advisory scheduling\\nstate: it is never persisted, never changes `isBlocked`, and never adds a\\ncontainer-level status-enforcement rule. Query projections expose active\\nadvisory waiting as `isAdvisoryBlocked=true` and identify its source through\\n`derivedBlockedBy`. `summary()` and `list(blocked=true)` report both hard and\\nadvisory entries and annotate each with `isBlocked` and\\n`isAdvisoryBlocked`; a mixed container has both flags set. Use these annotations\\nbefore deciding whether an orchestrator is seeing manual intent or computed\\nordering.\\n\\n```bash\\n# TASK-B depends on TASK-A\\ntask-board m 'link(TASK-260218-def, blocked_by=TASK-260218-abc)'\\n\\n# CLI will refuse to move TASK-B to development while TASK-A is not done\\ntask-board m 'set_status(TASK-260218-def, status=development)'\\n# Error: cannot start TASK-260218-def — blocked by: TASK-260218-abc\\n\\n# Remove dependency\\ntask-board m 'unlink(TASK-260218-def, blocked_by=TASK-260218-abc)'\\n\\n# Inspect hard and advisory scheduling constraints together\\ntask-board q --format compact 'list(blocked=true) { id isBlocked isAdvisoryBlocked blockedBy derivedBlockedBy }'\\n```\\n\\n### Board Review\\n\\n```bash\\n# Queries (use --format compact for token efficiency)\\ntask-board q 'schema()'                                                          # discover fields, presets, operations\\ntask-board q 'project_config()'                                                  # confirm runtime, launch gate, and model criterion before agent work\\ntask-board q --format compact 'summary()'                                        # quick overview\\ntask-board q --format compact 'get(TASK-260218-abc) { full }'                    # full element details\\ntask-board q --format compact 'list(type=task, status=development) { overview }'  # what's in progress\\ntask-board q --format compact 'list(type=task, blocked=true) { id name status }'  # what's blocked\\ntask-board q --format compact 'agents()'                                         # agent dashboard\\ntask-board q --format compact 'plan(EPIC-260218-abc, mode=children)'             # execution waves (super-compact planning shape)\\ntask-board q 'plan(scope=[EPIC-260218-abc, TASK-260218-def], mode=children)'     # plan over a set of subtrees (union)\\n\\n# Full-text search (local only)\\ntask-board grep \\\"AudioRecorder\\\"\\n\\n# Validation (local only)\\ntask-board validate\\ntask-board repair-links\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Querying & Mutating the Board\\n\\nThree primary access modes following the [agent-facing-api](https://github.com/relux-works/skill-agent-facing-api) pattern — all through one CLI binary:\\n\\n- **`q` (query DSL)** — structured reads: `task-board q --format compact 'list(type=task, status=development) { overview }'`\\n- **`grep` (full-text search)** — scoped text search: `task-board grep \\\"AudioRecorder\\\"` (local only)\\n- **`m` (mutation DSL)** — structured writes: `task-board m 'set_status(TASK-260218-abc, status=done)'`\\n\\nPlus a CLI plan facade for pretty-print / render / save: `task-board plan ...`, and local-only utilities such as `validate` (structure check).\\n\\nField presets: `minimal` (id, status), `default` (id, name, status), `overview` (+ assignee, parent), `full` (all 27 fields in the current schema; use `schema()` for the evolving list), `progress` (workflow/progress fields, including estimate), `planning` (status, dependencies, parent).\\n\\nWhen mentioning board elements in human-facing responses, always include both the concrete ID and the human-readable title/name. This applies to epics, stories, tasks, and bugs. ID-only references are not enough for people scanning the chat because they should not have to resolve identifiers manually.\\n\\nFor DSL operations, mutations reference, field projection, and decision table — see [`references/querying.md`](references/querying.md).\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Tips\\n\\n- **Detail stories before planning** — create tasks for all stories before phase planning, so the plan shows real scope\\n- **AC must be verifiable** — \\\"works\\\" is bad, \\\"test passes\\\" is good\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n/Users/iv/Developer/ReluxWorks/curator/.task-board/.resources/TASK-260728-1a52au/TASK-260728-1a52au_spawn-log_-implementer--doc-writer--codex-_RUN-260727-a527c3.log:23:{\"type\":\"item.started\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_11\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc 'shasum -a 256 .task-board/.resources/TASK-260720-1nvomm/TASK-260720-1nvomm_external-build-repositories-architecture-v6.md .task-board/.resources/TASK-260720-1nvomm/TASK-260720-1nvomm_external-build-repositories-review-v6.md && wc -l .task-board/.resources/TASK-260720-1nvomm/TASK-260720-1nvomm_external-build-repositories-architecture-v6.md .task-board/.resources/TASK-260720-1nvomm/TASK-260720-1nvomm_external-build-repositories-review-v6.md'\",\"aggregated_output\":\"\",\"exit_code\":null,\"status\":\"in_progress\"}}\n/Users/iv/Developer/ReluxWorks/curator/.task-board/.resources/TASK-260728-1a52au/TASK-260728-1a52au_spawn-log_-implementer--doc-writer--codex-_RUN-260727-a527c3.log:24:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_10\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"task-board q --format compact 'get(TASK-260728-1a52au) { full }'\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"id:TASK-260728-1a52au\\ntype:task\\nname:schema-v7-external-repository-core-contract\\ntitle:TASK-260728-1a52au: schema-v7-external-repository-core-contract\\nstatus:development\\nreview:required\\ntask_class:docs\\nestimate:{\\\"kind\\\":\\\"estimated\\\",\\\"scale\\\":{\\\"kind\\\":\\\"fibonacci\\\",\\\"number\\\":5}}\\nobservability:{\\\"readiness\\\":{\\\"advisoryBlockedElementIds\\\":[],\\\"blockedElementIds\\\":[],\\\"coverage\\\":{\\\"complete\\\":true,\\\"estimated\\\":1,\\\"ratio\\\":\\\"1/1\\\",\\\"total\\\":1,\\\"unestimated\\\":0},\\\"estimateKind\\\":\\\"estimated\\\",\\\"isAdvisoryBlocked\\\":false,\\\"isBlocked\\\":false,\\\"rolledEstimate\\\":{\\\"kind\\\":\\\"estimated\\\",\\\"scale\\\":{\\\"kind\\\":\\\"fibonacci\\\",\\\"number\\\":5}}},\\\"scope\\\":{\\\"id\\\":\\\"TASK-260728-1a52au\\\",\\\"name\\\":\\\"schema-v7-external-repository-core-contract\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"task\\\"},\\\"summary\\\":{\\\"active\\\":[{\\\"id\\\":\\\"TASK-260728-1a52au\\\",\\\"name\\\":\\\"schema-v7-external-repository-core-contract\\\",\\\"status\\\":\\\"development\\\"}],\\\"blocked\\\":[],\\\"byType\\\":{\\\"bug\\\":{\\\"total\\\":0,\\\"todo\\\":0,\\\"active\\\":0,\\\"done\\\":0,\\\"closed\\\":0,\\\"blocked\\\":0},\\\"epic\\\":{\\\"total\\\":0,\\\"todo\\\":0,\\\"active\\\":0,\\\"done\\\":0,\\\"closed\\\":0,\\\"blocked\\\":0},\\\"story\\\":{\\\"total\\\":0,\\\"todo\\\":0,\\\"active\\\":0,\\\"done\\\":0,\\\"closed\\\":0,\\\"blocked\\\":0},\\\"task\\\":{\\\"total\\\":1,\\\"todo\\\":0,\\\"active\\\":1,\\\"done\\\":0,\\\"closed\\\":0,\\\"blocked\\\":0}}}}\\nparent:STORY-260728-10wxx2\\nassignee:[implementer] doc-writer (codex)\\ndescription:Translate the accepted external-build-repository architecture into curator-spec normative decision and core security text. Define schema-7 versioning, first-class build_repositories, declared/effective source identity, immutable object-format plus full-commit locks, optional exact-tag assertion, repository descriptor trust boundary, audit equivalence, failure taxonomy, signing ownership, and closed future-driver admission.\\nscope:curator-spec decision records and normative protocol/security prose only. Use architecture-v6 SHA-256 2abae77d80eba6789f9911db7e9722595b4f21ba47391ca9eafd0064af03d67e and its accepted review as binding input. Do not implement schemas, generated vectors, or manager code in this task.\\nac:Normative text uses MUST/MUST NOT language for every accepted boundary; schemas 1-6, go-v1, receipt v1, marker v1/v2, and rc.4 are explicitly frozen; exact source access, protected offline snapshot, audit-before-cache/compiler, manager-derived command/output, credential and signing ownership, monorepo target selection, typed failures, and future closed-driver rules are complete and internally consistent; protocol validation and documentation checks pass; an outcome resource maps every architecture-v6 section to the resulting spec text.\\nblockedBy:[\\\"TASK-260720-1nvomm\\\"]\\nblocks:[\\\"TASK-260728-17sclp\\\",\\\"TASK-260728-wy3dsw\\\"]\\nderivedBlockedBy:[]\\nderivedBlocks:[]\\nchecklist:[{\\\"text\\\":\\\"Map every architecture-v6 normative section to owned spec text and record exclusions\\\",\\\"done\\\":false},{\\\"text\\\":\\\"Run protocol documentation and full curator-spec validation gates\\\",\\\"done\\\":false},{\\\"text\\\":\\\"Docs updated and consistent with current code\\\",\\\"done\\\":false},{\\\"text\\\":\\\"No discrepancies between code and description\\\",\\\"done\\\":false},{\\\"text\\\":\\\"Result linked as a new task-scoped outcome resource\\\",\\\"done\\\":false},{\\\"text\\\":\\\"Important findings, decisions, anomalies, or regressions recorded in logbook when relevant\\\",\\\"done\\\":false}]\\nnotes:Execution directive (2026-07-28): Target repository is /Users/iv/Developer/ReluxWorks/curator-spec, while the task board is in /Users/iv/Developer/ReluxWorks/curator. The curator-spec checkout contains accepted uncommitted rc.4 foundation changes in SECURITY.md, profiles/manager.md, protocol/core.md, and decisions/0004-compile-only-build-drivers.md; treat them as required input and never revert or overwrite them. Create an isolated task worktree under /Users/iv/Developer/ReluxWorks/.temp/TASK-260728-1a52au/curator-spec-worktree from the current curator-spec HEAD, seed only those accepted scoped working-tree files byte-for-byte, and implement this task there. Do not copy .DS_Store or .temp content. Do not commit or stage. Read the binding architecture-v6 and review-v6 resources from the curator board. Edit only normative decision/core/security documentation owned by this task; schemas, vectors, generated cases, release metadata, Curator, and cocoaskills are out of scope. Run scoped and full curator-spec validation in the pinned task-local environment, attach a task-scoped outcome and validation evidence to this board task, and route to to-review.\\\\nspawn agent resolution: Agent selection: codex via explicit_override\\\\nspawn launch composition: degraded_contract_unavailable; contract=agents-infra.child-launch-composition; provider=codex; schema=1; diagnostic=composition_contract_unavailable; bare child launch retained\\\\nspawn queued: [implementer] doc-writer (codex) (run=RUN-260727-a527c3, max_parallel=20)\\\\nspawn run started: [implementer] doc-writer (codex) (run=RUN-260727-a527c3)\\ncreated:2026-07-27T20:20:00Z\\nupdated:2026-07-27T20:25:18Z\\nisBlocked:false\\nisAdvisoryBlocked:false\\nchildren:[]\\npreconditionResources:[{\\\"name\\\":\\\"TASK-260728-1a52au_architecture-v6-precondition.md\\\",\\\"uri\\\":\\\"file://TASK-260728-1a52au/TASK-260728-1a52au_architecture-v6-precondition.md\\\",\\\"description\\\":\\\"Accepted external-build-repository architecture and review pointer\\\"}]\\noutcomeResources:[{\\\"name\\\":\\\"TASK-260728-1a52au_spawn-log_-implementer--doc-writer--codex-_RUN-260727-a527c3.log\\\",\\\"uri\\\":\\\"file://TASK-260728-1a52au/TASK-260728-1a52au_spawn-log_-implementer--doc-writer--codex-_RUN-260727-a527c3.log\\\",\\\"description\\\":\\\"System spawn log captured by task-board\\\"}]\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n/Users/iv/Developer/ReluxWorks/curator/.task-board/.resources/TASK-260728-1a52au/TASK-260728-1a52au_spawn-log_-implementer--doc-writer--codex-_RUN-260727-a527c3.log:25:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_11\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc 'shasum -a 256 .task-board/.resources/TASK-260720-1nvomm/TASK-260720-1nvomm_external-build-repositories-architecture-v6.md .task-board/.resources/TASK-260720-1nvomm/TASK-260720-1nvomm_external-build-repositories-review-v6.md && wc -l .task-board/.resources/TASK-260720-1nvomm/TASK-260720-1nvomm_external-build-repositories-architecture-v6.md .task-board/.resources/TASK-260720-1nvomm/TASK-260720-1nvomm_external-build-repositories-review-v6.md'\",\"aggregated_output\":\"2abae77d80eba6789f9911db7e9722595b4f21ba47391ca9eafd0064af03d67e  .task-board/.resources/TASK-260720-1nvomm/TASK-260720-1nvomm_external-build-repositories-architecture-v6.md\\na1992eea16b48a730dee06eff49c7fb5364194be6c22afe1f58ac5e58b7129f7  .task-board/.resources/TASK-260720-1nvomm/TASK-260720-1nvomm_external-build-repositories-review-v6.md\\n    1979 .task-board/.resources/TASK-260720-1nvomm/TASK-260720-1nvomm_external-build-repositories-architecture-v6.md\\n      25 .task-board/.resources/TASK-260720-1nvomm/TASK-260720-1nvomm_external-build-repositories-review-v6.md\\n    2004 total\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n/Users/iv/Developer/ReluxWorks/curator/.task-board/.resources/TASK-260728-1a52au/TASK-260728-1a52au_spawn-log_-implementer--doc-writer--codex-_RUN-260727-a527c3.log:29:{\"type\":\"item.started\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_14\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"rg -n '\\\"'^#{1,6} '\\\"' .task-board/.resources/TASK-260720-1nvomm/TASK-260720-1nvomm_external-build-repositories-architecture-v6.md && cat .task-board/.resources/TASK-260720-1nvomm/TASK-260720-1nvomm_external-build-repositories-review-v6.md\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"\",\"exit_code\":null,\"status\":\"in_progress\"}}\n/Users/iv/Developer/ReluxWorks/curator/.task-board/.resources/TASK-260728-1a52au/TASK-260728-1a52au_spawn-log_-implementer--doc-writer--codex-_RUN-260727-a527c3.log:30:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_14\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"rg -n '\\\"'^#{1,6} '\\\"' .task-board/.resources/TASK-260720-1nvomm/TASK-260720-1nvomm_external-build-repositories-architecture-v6.md && cat .task-board/.resources/TASK-260720-1nvomm/TASK-260720-1nvomm_external-build-repositories-review-v6.md\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"1:# External build repositories: architecture decision and threat model, revision 6\\n10:## 1. Decision summary\\n36:### 1.1 Change required by the fifth independent review\\n55:### 1.2 Justified gap and self-verification\\n69:## 2. Manifest schema 7 and compatibility\\n117:## 3. Exact Git source grammar and immutable lock\\n119:### 3.1 Canonical network identity\\n136:### 3.2 Safe tag and development-ref grammar\\n161:### 3.3 Lock and optional tag assertion\\n180:## 4. Repository descriptor and output boundary\\n215:## 5. Declared and effective source identity\\n217:### 5.1 Source states\\n251:### 5.2 Skillfile.dev schema 2\\n283:## 6. Closed Git child and object boundary\\n285:### 6.1 Trusted distribution, process graph, and clean environment\\n361:### 6.2 Exact private repository initialization\\n397:### 6.3 Exact network fetch and ref flows\\n588:### 6.4 Exact SSH wrapper and OpenSSH invocation\\n720:### 6.5 Local substitution admission: deliberately narrow v1\\n745:#### 6.5.1 Byte-level config admission\\n788:#### 6.5.2 Files-ref parsing and selected HEAD\\n813:#### 6.5.3 Exact object inventory and interoperable pack admission\\n909:### 6.6 Common raw-object reader and complete-object proof\\n915:#### 6.6.1 Exact `cat-file --batch` process\\n994:#### 6.6.2 Manager parsing and graph proof\\n1166:#### 6.6.3 Exact all-blob Git LFS rejection\\n1336:## 7. Snapshot identity and audit equivalence\\n1357:## 8. Receipt v2, cache identity, and mixed schema-7 builds\\n1359:### 8.1 Unsubstituted external receipt input\\n1433:### 8.2 Substituted external receipt input\\n1490:### 8.3 Mixed command behavior\\n1503:## 9. Marker v3, status, repair, deduplication, and GC\\n1566:### 9.1 Snapshot and artifact keys\\n1574:### 9.2 Read-only status\\n1594:### 9.3 Repair\\n1605:### 9.4 Garbage collection roots\\n1616:## 10. Deterministic build, install, PATH, and rollback\\n1645:## 11. Signing and notarization boundary\\n1665:## 12. Future closed-driver rule\\n1686:## 13. Threat model\\n1688:### 13.1 Adversary-controlled\\n1696:### 13.2 Trusted computing base\\n1705:### 13.3 Required controls\\n1730:## 14. Exact impact on existing board work\\n1734:### 14.1 Existing curator-spec schema-6 story\\n1751:### 14.2 Existing Curator implementation story\\n1765:### 14.3 Existing cocoaskills implementation story\\n1771:### 14.4 Existing interoperability story\\n1785:## 15. Rejected alternatives\\n1810:## 16. Primary-source register\\n1861:## 17. Validation and independent-review checks\\n1921:### 17.1 Producer verification evidence\\n# External build repositories v6 — independent review verdict\\n\\nVerdict: **ACCEPTED**. Route `TASK-260720-1nvomm` to `done`.\\n\\nReviewed artifact: `TASK-260720-1nvomm_external-build-repositories-architecture-v6.md`, SHA-256 `2abae77d80eba6789f9911db7e9722595b4f21ba47391ca9eafd0064af03d67e`. Active run `RUN-260727-825e19` is not goal-bound and had no pending directives.\\n\\n## Findings\\n\\nNo blocking or rework finding remains. Revision 6 closes the sole v5 defect without regressing the previously accepted architecture.\\n\\n- The v5-to-v6 diff is limited to optional-tag acquisition/verification, related ordering and error semantics, receipt/marker/repair wording, six new conformance cases, sources, and evidence. Previously accepted schema-7, source identity, Git object boundary, audit, receipt-v2/marker-v3, cache, lifecycle, signing, compatibility, and future-driver text is otherwise byte-identical.\\n- Every unsubstituted declaration with `tag` uses exactly `refs/tags/<tag>:refs/curator/tag` as its sole acquisition attempt in fresh private state. It never attempts the locked OID, branch, all-tags, or another ref as fallback. Untagged declarations alone use the full locked OID.\\n- Manager parsing recomputes lightweight/annotated tag objects with the common network/local byte grammar, enforces target types and chain bounds, and requires the terminal commit to equal `locked_commit.hex`.\\n- Matching, moved, missing, malformed/incomplete, and transport outcomes are normatively distinguished. The tag check gates audit success, artifact-cache lookup, compiler execution, receipt/marker publication, and repair. Syntax-only offline checks only warn; install/update/repair/coverage audit fail before mutation when the exact tag cannot be obtained.\\n- Receipt v2 and marker v3 record the declared tag only after the producing unsubstituted operation has completed the tag/lock check. They do not add a forgeable verification boolean. Read-only status validates protected snapshot/receipt/marker relationships and does not contact the remote merely to retest later tag movement; missing or unprovable protected evidence is not current. Repair always reacquires through the exact-tag-only path.\\n- Existing schema 1-6 and `go-v1` semantics remain frozen; schema 7, `go-repository-v1`, receipt v2, marker v3, and rc.5 remain a separate future delivery subtree.\\n\\n## Independent evidence\\n\\n- All 13 fenced JSON blocks parsed.\\n- Git 2.50.1 six-case smoke passed with `uploadpack.allowAnySHA1InWant` false and true. Matching annotated tags resolved to the lock; moved lightweight tags resolved to a different commit and mapped to `build_repository_ref_moved`; missing tags made fetch exit 128 and mapped to `build_repository_source_unavailable`. Each success exposed only `refs/curator/tag`; all six cases left `FETCH_HEAD` absent; no direct-OID refspec was attempted.\\n- Official Git fetch documentation confirms that explicit tag refspecs fetch the named tag despite `--no-tags`, `--no-write-fetch-head` suppresses `FETCH_HEAD`, and empty `--refmap` ignores configured refspecs. Official Git configuration documentation confirms the direct-object want controls are server policy and therefore irrelevant to this exact-tag-only path.\\n- Pinned validation environment: `tools/validate.py` passed 30 schemas and 93 vector files; `make validate` passed those checks, 8 Python tests, and `go test ./tools/...`; `git diff --check` passed.\\n- The host `python3 tools/validate.py` lacks `jsonschema`; the already pinned task-local venv contains jsonschema 4.25.1 and passed the required validator. This is an environment issue, not an artifact defect.\\n- The first temporary smoke inherited commit-signing configuration and failed before creating its fixture commit; rerunning in fresh temporary state with signing explicitly disabled produced the passing evidence above. No project/spec/schema/code/test/release or producer/prior-review resource was modified.\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n/Users/iv/Developer/ReluxWorks/curator/.task-board/.resources/TASK-260728-1a52au/TASK-260728-1a52au_spawn-log_-implementer--doc-writer--codex-_RUN-260727-a527c3.log:37:{\"type\":\"item.started\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_18\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"sed -n '1,283p' .task-board/.resources/TASK-260720-1nvomm/TASK-260720-1nvomm_external-build-repositories-architecture-v6.md\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"\",\"exit_code\":null,\"status\":\"in_progress\"}}\n/Users/iv/Developer/ReluxWorks/curator/.task-board/.resources/TASK-260728-1a52au/TASK-260728-1a52au_spawn-log_-implementer--doc-writer--codex-_RUN-260727-a527c3.log:38:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_18\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"sed -n '1,283p' .task-board/.resources/TASK-260720-1nvomm/TASK-260720-1nvomm_external-build-repositories-architecture-v6.md\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"# External build repositories: architecture decision and threat model, revision 6\\n\\n**Task:** `TASK-260720-1nvomm`  \\n**Date:** 2026-07-27  \\n**Status:** Superseding architecture proposal for independent review  \\n**Supersedes:** `TASK-260720-1nvomm_external-build-repositories-architecture-v5.md`\\n**Review input:** `TASK-260720-1nvomm_external-build-repositories-review-v5.md`\\n**Scope:** Architecture only. No protocol, schema, manager, test, CLI, release, or prior decision artifact was changed.\\n\\n## 1. Decision summary\\n\\nExternal build repositories require a new, explicitly versioned protocol surface. They MUST NOT be folded into manifest schema 6 or broaden the accepted `go-v1` driver.\\n\\nThe recommended transition remains:\\n\\n- manifest schema 7;\\n- a strict top-level `build_repositories` map;\\n- a new closed command driver, `go-repository-v1`;\\n- one fixed repository-root descriptor, `curator-build.json`, schema 1;\\n- build receipt schema 2 and install marker schema 3;\\n- a new conformance claim and release identity, recommended as protocol `1.0.0-rc.5` with claim schema 3;\\n- unchanged readers and meanings for manifest schemas 1 through 6, `go-v1`, receipt v1, and markers v1/v2.\\n\\nThe consuming skill owns the executable command name. The external repository owns only a logical target definition: driver, `build_root`, and `source_dir`. The manager continues to derive:\\n\\n```text\\nartifact-relative path = bin/<manifest-command-key>[.exe]\\nstaging path           = manager-private and implementation-specific\\nshim name              = <manifest-command-key>\\n```\\n\\n`curator-build.json` therefore MUST NOT name a binary or output location. A descriptor target is a logical selector, not a filename. Allowing repository-controlled names or paths would reopen the output-selection boundary closed by schema 6.\\n\\nAn external source is first-class compiler input. Before an artifact-cache lookup or compiler child, the manager MUST resolve or load the exact effective Git commit, freeze its complete regular-file snapshot, validate and hash it, and audit it as a separate subject from the consuming skill. This ordering applies to real operations, cache hits, and dry-runs that claim source verification.\\n\\n### 1.1 Change required by the fifth independent review\\n\\nThis revision preserves every accepted revision-5 section and closes its one\\nbounded tag-verification finding:\\n\\n1. An unsubstituted declaration containing `tag` now always uses one fresh\\n   exact-tag fetch as both acquisition and verification. It never tries the\\n   full locked object ID first. Manager code recomputes and peels the fetched\\n   lightweight or annotated tag chain and requires the terminal commit to\\n   equal `locked_commit.hex` before audit success, artifact-cache lookup, or a\\n   compiler child. A moved tag is `build_repository_ref_moved`; an absent or\\n   inaccessible exact tag is `build_repository_source_unavailable`. Direct\\n   full-object fetch remains available only for declarations without `tag`.\\n\\nThe prior LFS detector, common commit/tag grammar, exact Git process/object/pack\\ncontrols, source identity, receipt/marker/cache behavior, audit ordering,\\nstatus/repair/GC, output, signing, future-driver, and board-impact decisions\\nare unchanged.\\n\\n### 1.2 Justified gap and self-verification\\n\\n**Missing piece:** schema 6 can compile only source stored in the skill snapshot. It cannot name, lock, audit, or cache compiler source from a separate Git repository.\\n\\n**Requirement exposed by the scope delta:** Go CLI commands need named external build repositories, while source access, audit-before-compiler, no-hooks, manager-derived output, protected caches, currentness, rollback, signing separation, and future language drivers remain closed and deterministic.\\n\\n**Consequence if omitted:** implementations would overload `go-v1`, compile an unbound checkout, or accept package-selected Git/build/output behavior. Cache keys and audit coverage would be incomplete.\\n\\n**Gap closure:** schema 7, `curator-build.json`, `go-repository-v1`, receipt v2, and marker v3 define the missing source, target, identity, lifecycle, and compatibility surfaces without changing rc.4 meanings.\\n\\n**Sections checked:** accepted contract sections 2 through 10, including its complete rejected/out-of-scope list. The contract excludes generic hooks, arbitrary argv/environment/output, unsafe build systems, physical cache-path standardization, receipt-only provenance, and unreviewed drivers. It does not exclude a new versioned external-source driver. This packet preserves every exclusion.\\n\\nNo further research or human product decision is open. Board refinement may occur only after this architecture passes independent review.\\n\\n## 2. Manifest schema 7 and compatibility\\n\\nIllustrative consuming manifest:\\n\\n```json\\n{\\n  \\\"schema_version\\\": 7,\\n  \\\"build_repositories\\\": {\\n    \\\"golden-tools\\\": {\\n      \\\"git\\\": \\\"https://github.com/example/golden-tools.git\\\",\\n      \\\"locked_commit\\\": {\\n        \\\"object_format\\\": \\\"sha1\\\",\\n        \\\"hex\\\": \\\"0123456789abcdef0123456789abcdef01234567\\\"\\n      },\\n      \\\"tag\\\": \\\"v1.4.0\\\"\\n    }\\n  },\\n  \\\"commands\\\": {\\n    \\\"golden-tool\\\": {\\n      \\\"type\\\": \\\"build\\\",\\n      \\\"driver\\\": \\\"go-repository-v1\\\",\\n      \\\"repository\\\": \\\"golden-tools\\\",\\n      \\\"target\\\": \\\"golden-tool\\\"\\n    }\\n  },\\n  \\\"capabilities\\\": {\\n    \\\"network\\\": \\\"none\\\",\\n    \\\"filesystem\\\": \\\"repo\\\",\\n    \\\"exec\\\": \\\"none\\\",\\n    \\\"secrets\\\": \\\"none\\\",\\n    \\\"env_read\\\": []\\n  },\\n  \\\"dependencies\\\": {}\\n}\\n```\\n\\nNormative recommendations:\\n\\n1. `build_repositories` is legal only in schema 7 or later and is a strict map keyed by portable identifiers.\\n2. Each entry contains exactly `git` and `locked_commit`, plus OPTIONAL `tag`.\\n3. `locked_commit.object_format` is exactly `sha1` or `sha256`. `hex` is the full lowercase commit object ID: 40 hexadecimal characters for SHA-1 or 64 for SHA-256.\\n4. Branches, ranges, abbreviated IDs, `HEAD`, raw revision expressions, and package-selected local paths are forbidden.\\n5. Every declaration is selected by at least one command. Every repository command selects exactly one declaration and one descriptor target.\\n6. A command contains exactly `type`, `driver`, `repository`, and `target`. It cannot contain a program, argv, environment, output, filename, toolchain, signing identity, hook, generator, plugin, build script, or fallback.\\n7. The command driver equals the descriptor target driver. Unknown or mismatched drivers fail before cache lookup or compiler execution.\\n8. Schema 7 may mix local `go-v1` commands and external `go-repository-v1` commands. Local `build_roots` keep their schema-6 context-exclusion meaning. External repository files never enter agent context or runtime copying.\\n9. Schemas 1 through 6 reject all new fields and driver IDs. No rc.4 schema, receipt, marker, decision, or claim is reinterpreted.\\n\\n## 3. Exact Git source grammar and immutable lock\\n\\n### 3.1 Canonical network identity\\n\\nSchema 7 reuses protocol core section 6.1 canonical source identity, narrowed to HTTPS and SSH:\\n\\n- HTTPS form is `https://host/path`. Userinfo, password, port, query, fragment, percent escape, backslash, and empty path are forbidden.\\n- SSH URI form is `ssh://[user@]host/path`.\\n- SCP form is `[user@]host:path`.\\n- SSH username is optional ASCII matching `[A-Za-z0-9][A-Za-z0-9._-]{0,63}`. It affects the trusted SSH connection but is removed from canonical identity. HTTPS has no username.\\n- Host is ASCII `[A-Za-z0-9][A-Za-z0-9.-]*`, has no explicit port, and is lowercased.\\n- Repository path is valid Unicode scalar text encoded as UTF-8, has non-empty portable components, and contains no whitespace, `%`, `?`, `#`, backslash, empty component, `.` component, or `..` component.\\n- Leading and trailing path slashes are removed. Path case is preserved.\\n- Exactly one case-sensitive trailing `.git` is removed. `repo.GIT` is not changed.\\n- HTTPS, SSH URI, and SCP spellings that differ only by transport or permitted SSH username therefore produce the same `host/path` value.\\n- The canonical value is at most 4096 Unicode scalar values. Invalid network-looking forms are rejected and never treated as local.\\n\\nThe declared receipt identity records the resulting `{\\\"kind\\\":\\\"network-git\\\",\\\"value\\\":\\\"host/path\\\"}` plus the selected transport. The effective identity applies the same algorithm to an operator network substitution.\\n\\n### 3.2 Safe tag and development-ref grammar\\n\\nThe manifest `tag` is a tag name without the `refs/tags/` prefix. It is accepted only when:\\n\\n- it is valid Unicode scalar text whose UTF-8 encoding is 1 through 255 bytes;\\n- it does not start or end with `/`, contain `//`, or end with `.`;\\n- no slash-separated component starts with `.` or ends with `.lock`;\\n- it contains none of NUL, ASCII control bytes, DEL, space, `~`, `^`, `:`, `?`, `*`, `[`, or backslash;\\n- it contains neither `..` nor `@{` and is not the single character `@`;\\n- the exact constructed `refs/tags/<tag>` passes the protocol-defined equivalent of `git check-ref-format` without normalization.\\n\\nThe manager never passes an unvalidated tag as a revision expression. Network\\nacquisition passes only exact `refs/tags/<tag>` or `refs/heads/<branch>` fetch\\nsources; manager code performs tag peeling after object-ID recomputation. If a\\nseparately specified trusted Git verifier is ever used, its only permitted\\nrevision spelling is `refs/tags/<tag>^{commit}` after validation and after\\n`--end-of-options` where supported.\\n\\nOperator network substitutions use a structured ref:\\n\\n- `revision` is a full lowercase object ID for the effective repository object format;\\n- `tag` uses the rules above and constructs `refs/tags/<value>^{commit}`;\\n- `branch` applies the same grammar to `refs/heads/<value>` and constructs only that ref;\\n- no other revision operators or reflog syntax are accepted.\\n\\n### 3.3 Lock and optional tag assertion\\n\\nThe full object ID is the immutable lock. The tag is only a human-facing assertion:\\n\\n1. The effective declared-source repository object format MUST equal `locked_commit.object_format`.\\n2. The object named by `locked_commit.hex` MUST exist and be a commit.\\n3. If `tag` is present on an unsubstituted declaration, the manager MUST\\n   acquire exactly `refs/tags/<tag>` in fresh operation-private state, recompute\\n   and peel its lightweight or annotated tag chain under\\n   `--no-replace-objects`, and require the terminal commit to equal\\n   `locked_commit.hex`. Mismatch is `build_repository_ref_moved`; a missing or\\n   inaccessible exact ref is `build_repository_source_unavailable`.\\n4. Install, repair, status, and audit never rewrite a lock. An authoring command may propose a manifest change, but it is outside installation.\\n5. A declaration without `tag` acquires only the full locked object ID. A\\n   declaration with `tag` acquires only the exact tag ref; server permission to\\n   fetch a direct object ID is irrelevant and MUST NOT change this choice.\\n   Failure never permits a branch, all-tags, alternate-ref, or direct-object\\n   fallback.\\n\\n## 4. Repository descriptor and output boundary\\n\\nThe root tree of the effective commit contains exactly one descriptor named `curator-build.json`, schema 1:\\n\\n```json\\n{\\n  \\\"schema_version\\\": 1,\\n  \\\"targets\\\": {\\n    \\\"golden-tool\\\": {\\n      \\\"driver\\\": \\\"go-repository-v1\\\",\\n      \\\"build_root\\\": \\\".\\\",\\n      \\\"source_dir\\\": \\\"cmd/golden-tool\\\"\\n    },\\n    \\\"admin-tool\\\": {\\n      \\\"driver\\\": \\\"go-repository-v1\\\",\\n      \\\"build_root\\\": \\\"tools/admin\\\",\\n      \\\"source_dir\\\": \\\"tools/admin/cmd/admin\\\"\\n    }\\n  }\\n}\\n```\\n\\nThe descriptor and every target are strict. For `go-repository-v1`:\\n\\n1. A target contains exactly `driver`, `build_root`, and `source_dir`.\\n2. Paths are repository-relative. The single value `.` denotes the repository root; otherwise existing portable-path rules apply.\\n3. `source_dir` equals or is below the selected `build_root`.\\n4. `build_root` contains `go.mod` directly, and that file is the nearest ancestor `go.mod` of `source_dir`.\\n5. Targets may share one build root. Nested module roots are allowed only through explicit target selection and the nearest-module rule; discovery is forbidden.\\n6. Every non-standard module/package/source/embed/vendor input in the fixed `go list` graph stays below the selected build root. No input comes from the consuming skill, another external repository, a sibling module, a parent workspace, a host module cache, or the network.\\n7. The whole repository snapshot participates in raw validation, audit, and conservative source identity. Only the selected build root is compiler-visible.\\n8. The descriptor cannot contain a command name, output basename/path, install destination, alias, PATH edit, signing policy, credential, flag, environment, build system, hook, plugin, or arbitrary target argument.\\n\\nAllowing `.` for an external repository does not weaken schema 6. The entire external snapshot is compile-only and never prompt-visible or runtime-copied; a schema-6 local root still cannot be `.`.\\n\\n## 5. Declared and effective source identity\\n\\n### 5.1 Source states\\n\\nEvery external command binds two source states:\\n\\n- **Declared:** canonical manifest network identity, transport, immutable object-format/commit lock, and optional tag.\\n- **Effective:** exact source used after operator substitutions, including identity kind/value, transport where applicable, actual object format, full commit, substitution flag/type, and `curator-build-source-v1`.\\n\\nFor an unsubstituted source:\\n\\n- `effective.substituted` is `false`;\\n- `effective.substitution` is absent;\\n- effective identity equals declared canonical identity;\\n- effective object format and commit equal the lock.\\n\\nFor a substitution:\\n\\n- `effective.substituted` is `true`;\\n- `effective.substitution` is required and names exactly `local-path` or `network-git`;\\n- the declared state remains present and unchanged;\\n- effective identity, object format, full commit, and digest describe the substituted snapshot.\\n\\nA network substitution uses the same canonical network identity and records its transport and structured ref. A local substitution has no network identity. Its effective identity is:\\n\\n```text\\nkind  = operator-local-git\\nvalue = sha256(CCJ-1({\\n  \\\"algorithm\\\":\\\"curator-operator-local-git-v1\\\",\\n  \\\"project\\\":<canonical-project-identity>,\\n  \\\"selector\\\":<normalized project-relative substitution path>\\n}))\\n```\\n\\nThe selector uses `/`, valid Unicode scalars without normalization, removes `.` components, cancels an ordinary component followed by `..`, preserves unmatched leading `..`, and contains no empty component. This makes equivalent operator spellings stable across managers without placing an absolute host path in a receipt. The identity is not a network source identity or authorization token. Exact resolved bytes remain bound by object format, full commit, and build-source digest.\\n\\n### 5.2 Skillfile.dev schema 2\\n\\nOperator substitutions live only in ignored `Skillfile.dev.json` schema 2:\\n\\n```json\\n{\\n  \\\"schema_version\\\": 2,\\n  \\\"substitutions\\\": {},\\n  \\\"build_repository_substitutions\\\": {\\n    \\\"golden-skill\\\": {\\n      \\\"golden-tools\\\": {\\n        \\\"path\\\": \\\"../golden-tools\\\"\\n      }\\n    }\\n  }\\n}\\n```\\n\\nThe outer key is the consuming skill; the inner key is its repository ID. An entry is exactly one:\\n\\n- operator project-relative local Git `path`; or\\n- operator `git` plus one structured exact `ref`.\\n\\nRules:\\n\\n1. Package data cannot create or change substitutions.\\n2. Local paths resolve relative to the canonical project root. The manager obtains the repository's exact HEAD commit; dirty and untracked worktree bytes are not compiler input.\\n3. A network substitution uses the same HTTPS/SSH grammar and safe structured-ref rules.\\n4. The substitution replaces source acquisition only. It cannot change repository ID, descriptor target, driver, command name, output, compiler policy, credentials, or signing.\\n5. Strict audit fails for any substitution. Advisory audit reports it and still audits the exact effective snapshot.\\n6. Status compares the current effective substitution state to marker v3. Removing, changing, or moving a substitution makes the installation non-current.\\n\\n## 6. Closed Git child and object boundary\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n/Users/iv/Developer/ReluxWorks/curator/.task-board/.resources/TASK-260728-1a52au/TASK-260728-1a52au_spawn-log_-implementer--doc-writer--codex-_RUN-260727-a527c3.log:39:{\"type\":\"item.started\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_19\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"sed -n '284,720p' .task-board/.resources/TASK-260720-1nvomm/TASK-260720-1nvomm_external-build-repositories-architecture-v6.md\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"\",\"exit_code\":null,\"status\":\"in_progress\"}}\n/Users/iv/Developer/ReluxWorks/curator/.task-board/.resources/TASK-260728-1a52au/TASK-260728-1a52au_spawn-log_-implementer--doc-writer--codex-_RUN-260727-a527c3.log:40:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_19\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"sed -n '284,720p' .task-board/.resources/TASK-260720-1nvomm/TASK-260720-1nvomm_external-build-repositories-architecture-v6.md\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"\\n### 6.1 Trusted distribution, process graph, and clean environment\\n\\nThe manager resolves an operator-trusted Git distribution before reading a\\npackage or substitution path. The absolute `git` executable, its complete\\n`GIT_EXEC_PATH`, HTTPS helper, and every Git subprogram that the fixed fetch can\\nstart MUST be bundled, fingerprinted, or pinned by trusted operator policy. The\\nselected release family MUST be one tested against the driver vectors and MUST\\nsupport `--object-format`, `--ref-format=files`, `--no-lazy-fetch`,\\n`--no-write-fetch-head`, and `--no-auto-maintenance`. A newer or older unknown\\nfamily is rejected, not approximated.\\n\\nThe permitted child graph is exactly:\\n\\n- manager -> absolute trusted `git` for manager-owned `init` and `fetch`;\\n- manager -> the same absolute trusted `git` for one manager-owned, read-only\\n  `cat-file --batch` session after network acquisition or inert local-store\\n  copying;\\n- `git fetch` -> regular executables below the fingerprinted `GIT_EXEC_PATH`\\n  that the selected Git family uses for fetch/pack ingestion;\\n- HTTPS only: the fingerprinted `git-remote-https` and an optional\\n  manager-owned credential broker;\\n- SSH only: a manager-owned binary SSH wrapper and one operator-trusted\\n  OpenSSH executable;\\n- no shell, arbitrary remote helper, local/source upload-pack, maintenance,\\n  submodule child, checkout filter, hook, LFS client, pager, editor, proxy\\n  command, package program, or produced artifact.\\n\\nThe `cat-file` child MUST NOT start another process. An observed local child\\noutside that graph is a conformance failure. The\\npackage/descriptor can select only a validated HTTPS or SSH source spelling; it\\ncannot select the Git binary, exec path, helper, upload-pack, credential\\nbroker, SSH client, object-reader arguments, or child arguments.\\n\\nEvery Git child uses a manager-owned empty CWD and an environment constructed\\nfrom empty. It contains only indispensable OS process variables, private\\nhome/config/temp paths, normalized locale, the fingerprinted exec path, and:\\n\\n```text\\nGIT_CONFIG_GLOBAL=<manager-owned zero-byte regular file>\\nGIT_CONFIG_SYSTEM=<manager-owned zero-byte regular file>\\nGIT_CONFIG_NOSYSTEM=1\\nGIT_NO_REPLACE_OBJECTS=1\\nGIT_NO_LAZY_FETCH=1\\nGIT_OPTIONAL_LOCKS=0\\nGIT_TERMINAL_PROMPT=0\\nGIT_PAGER=cat\\nGIT_PROTOCOL_FROM_USER=0\\nGIT_LITERAL_PATHSPECS=1\\nGIT_ATTR_NOSYSTEM=1\\nGIT_EXEC_PATH=<fingerprinted trusted Git exec path>\\nHOME=<operation-private>/home\\nXDG_CONFIG_HOME=<operation-private>/config\\nLC_ALL=C\\nLANG=C\\n```\\n\\nFor SSH it additionally sets only\\n`GIT_SSH=<absolute-manager-wrapper>`, `GIT_SSH_VARIANT=ssh`, and the\\nmanager-private policy-FD number. `GIT_PROTOCOL` is absent and the fixed\\nconfiguration uses protocol version 0, so Git MUST NOT add an SSH\\n`SendEnv=GIT_PROTOCOL` option. For HTTPS, `GIT_ASKPASS` names either the\\nmanager credential broker or a fail-closed manager broker.\\n\\nThe manager unsets `GIT_DIR`, `GIT_WORK_TREE`, `GIT_COMMON_DIR`,\\n`GIT_OBJECT_DIRECTORY`, `GIT_ALTERNATE_OBJECT_DIRECTORIES`, `GIT_NAMESPACE`,\\n`GIT_REPLACE_REF_BASE`, `GIT_ATTR_SOURCE`, `GIT_SSL_NO_VERIFY`,\\n`GIT_SSH_COMMAND`, `SSH_AUTH_SOCK`, `SSH_ASKPASS`, `GIT_CONFIG_COUNT`,\\n`GIT_CONFIG_KEY_*`, `GIT_CONFIG_VALUE_*`, all trace variables, all HTTP/HTTPS\\nproxy variables, and every other inherited `GIT_*`/`SSH_*` variable. `PATH` is\\na manager-owned empty directory except for exact helper basenames that the\\nfingerprinted Git family cannot launch by absolute exec-path resolution.\\n\\nHTTPS verifies TLS and does not follow redirects. An operator proxy or\\ncredential may be supplied only through manager policy/broker state, never the\\nmanifest, descriptor, source repository, or compiler environment.\\n\\n### 6.2 Exact private repository initialization\\n\\nBefore each network attempt the manager exclusively creates a new empty\\noperation-private directory, an actual zero-entry template directory, empty\\nhooks directory, and private bare-repository path. It invokes exactly:\\n\\n```json\\n[\\n  \\\"<absolute-trusted-git>\\\",\\n  \\\"--git-dir=<operation-private>/repo.git\\\",\\n  \\\"-c\\\",\\n  \\\"init.defaultBranch=curator-invalid\\\",\\n  \\\"init\\\",\\n  \\\"--bare\\\",\\n  \\\"--quiet\\\",\\n  \\\"--template=<operation-private>/empty-template\\\",\\n  \\\"--object-format=<sha1-or-sha256>\\\",\\n  \\\"--ref-format=files\\\"\\n]\\n```\\n\\nThere is no repository path operand, global/user/system template, initial\\nbranch from configuration, shared mode, separate Git directory, worktree, or\\nsource-provided config. After exit, manager code opens the result with\\nno-follow/rooted access and requires:\\n\\n- one ordinary bare repository inside operation-private state;\\n- `core.repositoryformatversion`/`extensions.objectFormat` consistent with the\\n  requested object format and files refs;\\n- no remote, alternate, promisor, partial-clone, worktree, replace, graft,\\n  shallow, hook, filter, or unknown extension state;\\n- no link, reparse escape, special file, unexpected owner/ACL, or writable\\n  boundary outside the operation principal.\\n\\nFailure discards the directory. The manager never repairs or adopts it.\\n\\n### 6.3 Exact network fetch and ref flows\\n\\nAll fetches use a direct validated URL, a fresh private repository, one\\nmanager-chosen destination under `refs/curator/`, and this exact common shape:\\n\\n```json\\n[\\n  \\\"<absolute-trusted-git>\\\",\\n  \\\"--git-dir=<operation-private>/repo.git\\\",\\n  \\\"--no-replace-objects\\\",\\n  \\\"--no-lazy-fetch\\\",\\n  \\\"--no-optional-locks\\\",\\n  \\\"-c\\\",\\n  \\\"protocol.allow=never\\\",\\n  \\\"-c\\\",\\n  \\\"protocol.<https-or-ssh>.allow=always\\\",\\n  \\\"-c\\\",\\n  \\\"protocol.version=0\\\",\\n  \\\"-c\\\",\\n  \\\"credential.helper=\\\",\\n  \\\"-c\\\",\\n  \\\"core.askPass=<manager-broker>\\\",\\n  \\\"-c\\\",\\n  \\\"core.hooksPath=<operation-private>/empty-hooks\\\",\\n  \\\"-c\\\",\\n  \\\"core.fsmonitor=false\\\",\\n  \\\"-c\\\",\\n  \\\"core.untrackedCache=false\\\",\\n  \\\"-c\\\",\\n  \\\"submodule.recurse=false\\\",\\n  \\\"-c\\\",\\n  \\\"fetch.recurseSubmodules=false\\\",\\n  \\\"-c\\\",\\n  \\\"maintenance.auto=false\\\",\\n  \\\"-c\\\",\\n  \\\"fetch.writeCommitGraph=false\\\",\\n  \\\"-c\\\",\\n  \\\"fetch.fsckObjects=true\\\",\\n  \\\"-c\\\",\\n  \\\"transfer.fsckObjects=true\\\",\\n  \\\"-c\\\",\\n  \\\"http.followRedirects=false\\\",\\n  \\\"-c\\\",\\n  \\\"http.sslVerify=true\\\",\\n  \\\"-c\\\",\\n  \\\"http.proxy=\\\",\\n  \\\"-c\\\",\\n  \\\"https.proxy=\\\",\\n  \\\"fetch\\\",\\n  \\\"--quiet\\\",\\n  \\\"--atomic\\\",\\n  \\\"--no-tags\\\",\\n  \\\"--no-recurse-submodules\\\",\\n  \\\"--no-auto-maintenance\\\",\\n  \\\"--no-write-fetch-head\\\",\\n  \\\"--no-write-commit-graph\\\",\\n  \\\"--refmap=\\\",\\n  \\\"--jobs=1\\\",\\n  \\\"--upload-pack=git-upload-pack\\\",\\n  \\\"--\\\",\\n  \\\"<validated-url>\\\",\\n  \\\"<one-manager-refspec>\\\"\\n]\\n```\\n\\nThe selected transport is the only `protocol.*.allow=always` entry; the other\\ntransport and every remote-helper protocol remain denied. There is no\\n`--filter`, `--server-option`, remote name, configured refspec, stdin refspec,\\nprune, mirror, depth/shallow option, tag auto-follow, or package-selected\\nargument. Top-level `git fetch` receives closed stdin and bounded captured\\ndiagnostics.\\n\\nThe one refspec is selected as follows:\\n\\n1. **Unsubstituted declaration without `tag`:**\\n   Fetch `<full-locked-oid>:refs/curator/locked`. A full hexadecimal object\\n   name is the only non-ref source admitted. On success, manager code still\\n   parses and recomputes that object and requires a commit. A nonzero fetch is\\n   `build_repository_source_unavailable`. There is no tag, branch, advertised\\n   ref, or all-refs fallback.\\n2. **Unsubstituted declaration with `tag`:**\\n   Always fetch\\n   `refs/tags/<tag>:refs/curator/tag` as the sole acquisition attempt. The\\n   manager MUST NOT first or later fetch `<full-locked-oid>`, even when the\\n   server permits direct object-ID wants. Manager code requires exactly the\\n   fixed destination ref, parses and recomputes a lightweight or annotated-tag\\n   chain with section 6.6.2's common grammar, and requires the terminal object\\n   to be a commit exactly equal to `locked_commit.hex`. A different terminal\\n   commit is `build_repository_ref_moved`. A missing exact ref, nonzero fetch,\\n   or transport failure is `build_repository_source_unavailable`. A malformed\\n   or incomplete fetched object graph retains the section 6.6 stable semantic\\n   or incomplete-source error. There is no direct-object, branch, all-tags, or\\n   alternate-ref fallback.\\n3. **Operator network substitution:**  \\n   A full `revision` fetches\\n   `<full-oid>:refs/curator/effective`; a `tag` fetches\\n   `refs/tags/<tag>:refs/curator/effective`; a `branch` fetches\\n   `refs/heads/<branch>:refs/curator/effective`. There is no fallback between\\n   forms. The declared repository lock's object format initializes the private\\n   repository and MUST match the substituted remote; the manager never retries\\n   under another hash family. The exact resulting commit becomes the effective\\n   receipt identity.\\n\\nEvery destination is fixed manager namespace text; remote data cannot choose a\\nlocal ref, refmap, refspec, helper, upload-pack, filter, or server option.\\n`FETCH_HEAD`, remote-tracking refs, local heads/tags, commit-graphs, and\\nmaintenance state MUST remain absent. A nonzero fetch, unexpected local ref,\\nunexpected child, or any write outside the private repository fails and\\ndiscards the attempt.\\n\\nFor an unsubstituted tagged source, exact-tag fetch and terminal-lock equality\\nare preconditions of source resolution. They run before source audit can\\nsucceed, before artifact-cache lookup, and before any compiler child. A failure\\npublishes no snapshot, audit success, receipt, marker, or artifact. A\\nsyntax-only offline check that cannot perform the exact-tag fetch emits only\\n`build_repository_unverified_offline`; install, update, repair, and\\ncoverage-claiming audit return `build_repository_source_unavailable` before\\nmutation.\\n\\nThe conformance matrix is invariant to direct object-ID want policy because a\\ntagged declaration never issues such a want:\\n\\n```json\\n[\\n  {\\n    \\\"name\\\": \\\"tag-match-direct-oid-wants-denied\\\",\\n    \\\"server_direct_oid_wants\\\": \\\"deny\\\",\\n    \\\"fetch_refspec\\\": \\\"refs/tags/v1.4.0:refs/curator/tag\\\",\\n    \\\"direct_oid_fetch_attempted\\\": false,\\n    \\\"tag_terminal_commit\\\": \\\"0123456789abcdef0123456789abcdef01234567\\\",\\n    \\\"locked_commit\\\": \\\"0123456789abcdef0123456789abcdef01234567\\\",\\n    \\\"result\\\": \\\"source-resolved\\\"\\n  },\\n  {\\n    \\\"name\\\": \\\"tag-moved-direct-oid-wants-denied\\\",\\n    \\\"server_direct_oid_wants\\\": \\\"deny\\\",\\n    \\\"fetch_refspec\\\": \\\"refs/tags/v1.4.0:refs/curator/tag\\\",\\n    \\\"direct_oid_fetch_attempted\\\": false,\\n    \\\"tag_terminal_commit\\\": \\\"89abcdef0123456789abcdef0123456789abcdef\\\",\\n    \\\"locked_commit\\\": \\\"0123456789abcdef0123456789abcdef01234567\\\",\\n    \\\"expected_error\\\": \\\"build_repository_ref_moved\\\",\\n    \\\"audit_started\\\": false,\\n    \\\"artifact_cache_lookup\\\": false,\\n    \\\"compiler_started\\\": false\\n  },\\n  {\\n    \\\"name\\\": \\\"tag-missing-direct-oid-wants-denied\\\",\\n    \\\"server_direct_oid_wants\\\": \\\"deny\\\",\\n    \\\"fetch_refspec\\\": \\\"refs/tags/v1.4.0:refs/curator/tag\\\",\\n    \\\"direct_oid_fetch_attempted\\\": false,\\n    \\\"remote_ref_present\\\": false,\\n    \\\"expected_error\\\": \\\"build_repository_source_unavailable\\\",\\n    \\\"audit_started\\\": false,\\n    \\\"artifact_cache_lookup\\\": false,\\n    \\\"compiler_started\\\": false\\n  },\\n  {\\n    \\\"name\\\": \\\"tag-match-direct-oid-wants-permitted\\\",\\n    \\\"server_direct_oid_wants\\\": \\\"permit\\\",\\n    \\\"fetch_refspec\\\": \\\"refs/tags/v1.4.0:refs/curator/tag\\\",\\n    \\\"direct_oid_fetch_attempted\\\": false,\\n    \\\"tag_terminal_commit\\\": \\\"0123456789abcdef0123456789abcdef01234567\\\",\\n    \\\"locked_commit\\\": \\\"0123456789abcdef0123456789abcdef01234567\\\",\\n    \\\"result\\\": \\\"source-resolved\\\"\\n  },\\n  {\\n    \\\"name\\\": \\\"tag-moved-direct-oid-wants-permitted\\\",\\n    \\\"server_direct_oid_wants\\\": \\\"permit\\\",\\n    \\\"fetch_refspec\\\": \\\"refs/tags/v1.4.0:refs/curator/tag\\\",\\n    \\\"direct_oid_fetch_attempted\\\": false,\\n    \\\"tag_terminal_commit\\\": \\\"89abcdef0123456789abcdef0123456789abcdef\\\",\\n    \\\"locked_commit\\\": \\\"0123456789abcdef0123456789abcdef01234567\\\",\\n    \\\"expected_error\\\": \\\"build_repository_ref_moved\\\",\\n    \\\"audit_started\\\": false,\\n    \\\"artifact_cache_lookup\\\": false,\\n    \\\"compiler_started\\\": false\\n  },\\n  {\\n    \\\"name\\\": \\\"tag-missing-direct-oid-wants-permitted\\\",\\n    \\\"server_direct_oid_wants\\\": \\\"permit\\\",\\n    \\\"fetch_refspec\\\": \\\"refs/tags/v1.4.0:refs/curator/tag\\\",\\n    \\\"direct_oid_fetch_attempted\\\": false,\\n    \\\"remote_ref_present\\\": false,\\n    \\\"expected_error\\\": \\\"build_repository_source_unavailable\\\",\\n    \\\"audit_started\\\": false,\\n    \\\"artifact_cache_lookup\\\": false,\\n    \\\"compiler_started\\\": false\\n  }\\n]\\n```\\n\\n### 6.4 Exact SSH wrapper and OpenSSH invocation\\n\\nSSH sources retain the section-3 canonical identity but are further narrowed\\nfor this driver: the raw repository path supplied to the remote shell MUST use\\nonly ASCII letters, digits, `.`, `_`, `-`, and `/`, with the existing\\nnon-empty/no-`.`/no-`..` component rules. A quote, shell metacharacter,\\nwhitespace, escape, or non-ASCII byte is therefore rejected for SSH before\\nGit. HTTPS retains the section-3 path grammar.\\n\\nBefore fetch, the manager creates a protected read-only policy record and\\npasses an already-open descriptor number to the binary wrapper. The record\\ncontains exactly:\\n\\n- the expected Git destination, either `host` or `user@host`;\\n- the expected raw remote path from the validated SSH URI/SCP spelling;\\n- the expected remote command\\n  `git-upload-pack '<raw-path>'`;\\n- absolute operator-trusted SSH executable, empty SSH config, empty global\\n  known-hosts file, selected operator known-hosts file, timeout, and exactly\\n  one authentication mode.\\n\\nBecause the allowed path alphabet excludes `'`, the command has no escaping\\nbranch. The wrapper does not accept a policy pathname from argv/environment and\\ndoes not consult package/project files.\\n\\n`GIT_SSH_VARIANT=ssh` suppresses variant autodetection. With fixed Git protocol\\n0 and no port/IP-family option, the wrapper accepts exactly:\\n\\n```text\\nargv[0] = <absolute-manager-wrapper>\\nargv[1] = <expected host or user@host>\\nargv[2] = <exact expected git-upload-pack command>\\nargc    = 3\\n```\\n\\nIt rejects `-G`, `-p`, `-4`, `-6`, `-o`, `SendEnv`, extra operands, a different\\nhost/user/path/upload-pack, and every other shape. A tested Git family whose\\nactual wrapper call differs is unsupported until a new reviewed wrapper\\nrevision is defined.\\n\\nAfter byte-exact comparison, the wrapper directly executes the\\noperator-trusted SSH binary without a shell. The common argv is exactly:\\n\\n```json\\n[\\n  \\\"<absolute-operator-trusted-ssh>\\\",\\n  \\\"-F\\\",\\n  \\\"<manager-owned-empty-ssh-config>\\\",\\n  \\\"-T\\\",\\n  \\\"-o\\\",\\n  \\\"BatchMode=yes\\\",\\n  \\\"-o\\\",\\n  \\\"NumberOfPasswordPrompts=0\\\",\\n  \\\"-o\\\",\\n  \\\"PasswordAuthentication=no\\\",\\n  \\\"-o\\\",\\n  \\\"KbdInteractiveAuthentication=no\\\",\\n  \\\"-o\\\",\\n  \\\"PreferredAuthentications=publickey\\\",\\n  \\\"-o\\\",\\n  \\\"HostbasedAuthentication=no\\\",\\n  \\\"-o\\\",\\n  \\\"GSSAPIAuthentication=no\\\",\\n  \\\"-o\\\",\\n  \\\"StrictHostKeyChecking=yes\\\",\\n  \\\"-o\\\",\\n  \\\"UserKnownHostsFile=<operator-known-hosts>\\\",\\n  \\\"-o\\\",\\n  \\\"GlobalKnownHostsFile=<manager-owned-empty-known-hosts>\\\",\\n  \\\"-o\\\",\\n  \\\"CheckHostIP=no\\\",\\n  \\\"-o\\\",\\n  \\\"VerifyHostKeyDNS=no\\\",\\n  \\\"-o\\\",\\n  \\\"UpdateHostKeys=no\\\",\\n  \\\"-o\\\",\\n  \\\"ForwardAgent=no\\\",\\n  \\\"-o\\\",\\n  \\\"ForwardX11=no\\\",\\n  \\\"-o\\\",\\n  \\\"ClearAllForwardings=yes\\\",\\n  \\\"-o\\\",\\n  \\\"PermitLocalCommand=no\\\",\\n  \\\"-o\\\",\\n  \\\"ProxyCommand=none\\\",\\n  \\\"-o\\\",\\n  \\\"ProxyJump=none\\\",\\n  \\\"-o\\\",\\n  \\\"ControlMaster=no\\\",\\n  \\\"-o\\\",\\n  \\\"ControlPath=none\\\",\\n  \\\"-o\\\",\\n  \\\"ControlPersist=no\\\",\\n  \\\"-o\\\",\\n  \\\"RequestTTY=no\\\",\\n  \\\"-o\\\",\\n  \\\"EscapeChar=none\\\",\\n  \\\"-o\\\",\\n  \\\"EnableEscapeCommandline=no\\\",\\n  \\\"-o\\\",\\n  \\\"CanonicalizeHostname=no\\\",\\n  \\\"-o\\\",\\n  \\\"ConnectionAttempts=1\\\",\\n  \\\"-o\\\",\\n  \\\"ConnectTimeout=<manager-policy-seconds>\\\",\\n  \\\"<one-authentication-tail>\\\",\\n  \\\"<expected host or user@host>\\\",\\n  \\\"<exact expected git-upload-pack command>\\\"\\n]\\n```\\n\\nThe authentication tail is exactly one of:\\n\\n```text\\noperator identity file:\\n  -o IdentitiesOnly=yes -o IdentityAgent=none -i <operator-selected-identity>\\n\\noperator SSH agent:\\n  -o IdentitiesOnly=no -o IdentityFile=none\\n  -o IdentityAgent=<operator-selected-agent-socket>\\n```\\n\\nThe private `HOME` contains no default identity files. Package data cannot\\nselect the identity, agent, known-hosts, timeout, SSH config, host-key policy,\\nor option. The wrapper passes no ambient `SSH_AUTH_SOCK`.\\n\\nThe top-level fetch has no inherited terminal stdin. SSH standard input/output\\nare necessarily the private Git-upload-pack protocol pipes created by trusted\\nGit; they are not a TTY, user input, package file, or prompt channel. The\\nwrapper neither parses nor replaces that protocol stream. This is the only\\nstdin carried through SSH; closing it would make Git transport impossible.\\n\\n### 6.5 Local substitution admission: deliberately narrow v1\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n/Users/iv/Developer/ReluxWorks/curator/.task-board/.resources/TASK-260728-1a52au/TASK-260728-1a52au_spawn-log_-implementer--doc-writer--codex-_RUN-260727-a527c3.log:41:{\"type\":\"item.started\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_20\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"sed -n '720,1165p' .task-board/.resources/TASK-260720-1nvomm/TASK-260720-1nvomm_external-build-repositories-architecture-v6.md\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"\",\"exit_code\":null,\"status\":\"in_progress\"}}\n/Users/iv/Developer/ReluxWorks/curator/.task-board/.resources/TASK-260728-1a52au/TASK-260728-1a52au_spawn-log_-implementer--doc-writer--codex-_RUN-260727-a527c3.log:42:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_20\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"sed -n '720,1165p' .task-board/.resources/TASK-260720-1nvomm/TASK-260720-1nvomm_external-build-repositories-architecture-v6.md\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"### 6.5 Local substitution admission: deliberately narrow v1\\n\\nLocal substitutions execute no Git command, upload-pack, hook, filter, helper,\\nor LFS process from the source repository. Manager code treats repository\\nadministration files and object stores as untrusted data.\\n\\nV1 admits only an ordinary non-bare worktree whose substitution root is a\\nlink-free directory and whose `.git` entry is a link-free directory directly\\nbelow that root. Stable pre-audit rejections are:\\n\\n- `.git` is a regular gitfile: `build_repository_local_gitfile_unsupported`;\\n- `.git` is absent and the selected path is a bare repository:\\n  `build_repository_local_bare_unsupported`;\\n- `.git/commondir`, `.git/worktrees`, or `.git/config.worktree` exists:\\n  `build_repository_local_linked_worktree_unsupported`;\\n- `.git` or any admitted administration/object component is a link, reparse\\n  point, special file, multiply linked mutable file, or containment escape:\\n  `build_repository_local_layout_unsafe`.\\n\\nThe manager never follows or opens a gitfile/commondir target in v1. This makes\\ntheir containment rule exact: no path outside `<substitution-root>/.git` is\\nadmitted. Supporting ordinary gitfiles, linked worktrees, submodule worktrees,\\nor bare repositories requires a new admission algorithm and conformance\\nvectors, not a fallback to Git discovery.\\n\\n#### 6.5.1 Byte-level config admission\\n\\nThe manager opens `.git/config` no-follow as a bounded regular file and parses\\nthis data-only subset:\\n\\n1. bytes are valid UTF-8 with LF or CRLF records, optional final line ending,\\n   and no BOM, NUL, lone CR, control byte other than tab in a value, or\\n   backslash line continuation;\\n2. section names and variable names are ASCII case-insensitive\\n   `[A-Za-z][A-Za-z0-9-]*` tokens; subsections are double-quoted UTF-8 with\\n   only `\\\\\\\\` and `\\\\\\\"` escapes;\\n3. values are one-line unquoted text or one double-quoted value with only\\n   `\\\\\\\\`, `\\\\\\\"`, `\\\\n`, `\\\\t`, and `\\\\b` escapes; comment markers are recognized\\n   only outside quotes; malformed/unsupported syntax rejects the repository;\\n4. `[include]` and `[includeIf \\\"...\\\"]` are rejected before resolving a path;\\n5. duplicate security-relevant keys reject even when values match.\\n\\nOther well-formed non-extension keys are inert: the manager records but never\\napplies them. Admission reads only:\\n\\n- `core.repositoryformatversion`, required as integer `0` or `1`;\\n- `core.bare`, required `false`;\\n- `extensions.*`;\\n- every `remote \\\"<name>\\\".promisor` and\\n  `remote \\\"<name>\\\".partialCloneFilter`.\\n\\nAllowed format states are exactly:\\n\\n- SHA-1: repository format `0`, no `extensions.*`;\\n- SHA-256: repository format `1`, exactly\\n  `extensions.objectFormat=sha256`, with optional\\n  `extensions.refStorage=files`.\\n\\nBoolean values are accepted only as case-insensitive `true`/`yes`/`on`/`1` or\\n`false`/`no`/`off`/`0`; an absent value means true as in Git config.\\n\\n`extensions.refStorage=reftable`, `extensions.partialClone`,\\n`extensions.worktreeConfig`, `extensions.compatObjectFormat`, `noop`,\\n`preciousObjects`, every unknown extension, a true promisor value, or a\\nnon-empty partial-clone filter fails\\n`build_repository_local_format_unsupported`. Reftable is deliberately rejected;\\ndummy `HEAD`/`refs` files are never mistaken for the files backend.\\n\\n#### 6.5.2 Files-ref parsing and selected HEAD\\n\\n`HEAD`, every loose ref below `.git/refs`, and optional `.git/packed-refs` are\\nbounded, link-free regular files. The parser:\\n\\n- accepts `HEAD` as either one full lowercase object ID or\\n  `ref: refs/heads/<safe-name>` plus exactly one LF/CRLF or EOF;\\n- permits only one level of HEAD symbolic reference; the selected loose/packed\\n  head itself contains one full lowercase object ID, never another symref;\\n- validates every ref name with the section-3 safe ref grammar and the\\n  protocol-defined equivalent of `git check-ref-format`;\\n- parses `packed-refs` as an optional `# pack-refs with:` header whose unique\\n  space-separated traits are drawn only from `peeled`, `fully-peeled`, and\\n  `sorted`, then\\n  `<full-oid> SP <refname>` records and optional `^<full-oid>` peeled records\\n  only immediately after a tag record; it rejects duplicates, unsupported\\n  headers, wrong hash lengths/case, malformed lines, and ref/peeled conflicts;\\n- rejects any loose or packed `refs/replace/*` entry, regardless of whether it\\n  is reachable or selected.\\n\\nLoose refs have Git's normal precedence over packed refs, but contradictory\\nduplicate loose files and platform-colliding ref paths reject rather than\\nchoose. The selected HEAD object ID is the effective commit recorded in the\\nreceipt. Dirty index/worktree/untracked bytes are never opened as source.\\n\\n#### 6.5.3 Exact object inventory and interoperable pack admission\\n\\nBefore interpreting an object, the manager freezes one inventory generation and\\ncopies the allowed bytes into operation-private inert holding state. It\\nre-stats and rehashes every admitted source file after copy; any addition,\\nremoval, rename, metadata identity change, or byte change aborts.\\n\\nAllowed object-store entries are exactly:\\n\\n- loose objects at\\n  `objects/[0-9a-f]{2}/[0-9a-f]{38}` for SHA-1 or\\n  `objects/[0-9a-f]{2}/[0-9a-f]{62}` for SHA-256;\\n- zero or more exact\\n  `objects/pack/pack-<full-object-format-hex>.pack` and matching `.idx` pairs.\\n\\nEvery loose/pack/index entry is a singly linked regular file. A pack without\\none matching index, duplicate basename, unexpected suffix, or hash-family\\nmismatch rejects. V1 rejects all other object sidecars/state, including:\\n\\n```text\\nobjects/info/*\\nobjects/pack/*.promisor\\nobjects/pack/*.keep\\nobjects/pack/*.bitmap\\nobjects/pack/*.rev\\nobjects/pack/*.mtimes\\nobjects/pack/multi-pack-index*\\nshallow\\ninfo/grafts\\n```\\n\\nIt also rejects alternates/http-alternates, cruft/promisor packs, namespaces,\\nand unexpected object directories. The source `.idx` is captured only as\\nuntrusted inert input. Manager code validates its fanout, sorted names,\\noffsets, object count, pack checksum, and index checksum against the paired\\npack before using an offset.\\n\\nThe portable local v1 subset is exact:\\n\\n1. `H` is 20 for SHA-1 and 32 for SHA-256. Every object ID and checksum below\\n   has exactly `H` bytes and uses that repository hash.\\n2. A `.pack` begins with ASCII `PACK`, unsigned 32-bit big-endian version `2`\\n   or `3`, and an unsigned 32-bit big-endian object count `N`. Its last `H`\\n   bytes equal `HASH(all preceding pack bytes)`. The lowercase checksum hex is\\n   exactly the `pack-<hex>` basename.\\n3. A paired `.idx` is version 2 only: bytes `ff 74 4f 63`, unsigned 32-bit\\n   big-endian version `2`, 256 monotonically nondecreasing unsigned 32-bit\\n   fanout entries, and final fanout value `N`.\\n4. The index then contains exactly `N` object names of width `H`, strictly\\n   increasing by unsigned byte order; `N` unsigned 32-bit CRC32 entries; and\\n   `N` unsigned 32-bit offsets. An offset with its high bit clear is a pack\\n   offset. An offset with its high bit set indexes the following table of\\n   unsigned 64-bit big-endian large offsets after clearing that bit. If any\\n   large offset is referenced, the referenced indices are unique and exactly\\n   the dense range `0..M-1`; otherwise `M` is zero. The table contains exactly\\n   `M` entries. Every resolved pack offset is unique, is at least 12, and\\n   begins before the pack trailer. Sorting the resolved offsets defines each\\n   packed-entry byte range through the next offset or the pack trailer; the\\n   corresponding index CRC32 MUST equal zlib CRC32 over that complete range.\\n5. The index ends with the paired pack checksum followed by an index checksum\\n   equal to `HASH(all preceding index bytes)`. There are no omitted or trailing\\n   bytes, duplicate object names, duplicate offsets, decreasing fanout values,\\n   count mismatches, or cross-family hash widths.\\n6. Pack versions other than 2/3, index versions other than 2, version-1\\n   indexes, unknown sidecars, checksum failures, or malformed tables all fail the single stable error\\n   `build_repository_local_object_format_unsupported`.\\n\\nThis admission parser validates the physical container only; it does not trust\\nthe index's claim about an object's identity or decompress source-selected\\nobjects itself. After validation, the manager creates a fresh private bare\\nrepository using section 6.2 with the admitted object format, copies only the\\nvalidated loose objects and paired `.pack`/`.idx` files beneath its own\\n`objects/` directory with no-follow/rooted writes, and seals that directory\\nread-only for the object reader. It writes manager-generated repository config\\nand no source config, refs, hooks, attributes, LFS data, alternates, commit\\ngraph, multi-pack index, bitmap, reverse index, or maintenance state.\\n\\nThe manager then uses the common trusted `cat-file` contract in section 6.6.\\nAn OFS_DELTA base remains within its paired pack; a REF_DELTA base may be in\\nanother admitted pack or loose object in the same sealed private store. No base\\nmay come from an alternate, lazy/promisor fetch, source worktree, or network.\\nMissing/cyclic/oversized delta resolution makes `cat-file` fail and therefore\\nfails complete-source proof; the manager never widens the store.\\nEvery consumed object is independently recomputed as:\\n\\n```text\\nobject_id = HASH(type || \\\" \\\" || decimal_byte_length || NUL || exact_content)\\n```\\n\\nunder SHA-1 or SHA-256 and MUST equal both the requested and returned full ID.\\nThus trusted Git performs bounded decompression/delta resolution from inert\\nbytes, while manager code retains authority over identity, graph semantics, and\\nmaterialized snapshot bytes. An implementation unable to validate or stage\\nthis exact subset MUST reject it; it MUST NOT invoke the source repository's\\nGit configuration or select a private implementation-specific subset.\\n\\n### 6.6 Common raw-object reader and complete-object proof\\n\\nNetwork fetches already end in a manager-created private repository. Local\\nsubstitutions are copied into an equivalent manager-created private repository\\nunder section 6.5.3. From this point the two source kinds use one contract.\\n\\n#### 6.6.1 Exact `cat-file --batch` process\\n\\nAfter all physical-store checks and before audit or artifact-cache lookup, the\\nmanager launches exactly one direct child:\\n\\n```json\\n[\\n  \\\"<absolute-trusted-git>\\\",\\n  \\\"--git-dir=<operation-private>/repo.git\\\",\\n  \\\"--no-replace-objects\\\",\\n  \\\"--no-lazy-fetch\\\",\\n  \\\"--no-optional-locks\\\",\\n  \\\"-c\\\",\\n  \\\"core.hooksPath=<operation-private>/empty-hooks\\\",\\n  \\\"-c\\\",\\n  \\\"core.fsmonitor=false\\\",\\n  \\\"-c\\\",\\n  \\\"core.untrackedCache=false\\\",\\n  \\\"-c\\\",\\n  \\\"maintenance.auto=false\\\",\\n  \\\"cat-file\\\",\\n  \\\"--batch=%(objectname) %(objecttype) %(objectsize)\\\"\\n]\\n```\\n\\nThe child uses section 6.1's empty CWD and clean environment. In particular\\n`GIT_NO_REPLACE_OBJECTS=1`, `GIT_NO_LAZY_FETCH=1`,\\n`GIT_OPTIONAL_LOCKS=0`, empty global/system config, no alternates, no worktree,\\nno object-directory override, no credential/SSH/HTTP variables, and a\\nmanager-owned empty `PATH` remain in force. Its repository and object store are\\nread-only. Standard error is bounded diagnostic data. The child has a\\ndeadline, output/object/aggregate-byte limits, process/memory/disk limits, and\\nOS network denial. It may not create a child process or modify repository,\\naudit, cache, or installation state.\\n\\nThe exact negative option contract is equally normative: the argv contains no\\n`--batch-command`, `--batch-all-objects`, `--buffer`, `--follow-symlinks`,\\n`--unordered`, `--textconv`, `--filters`, `--filter`, `--use-mailmap`,\\n`--mailmap`, `--path`, `-Z`, or `-z`. There is no `git ls-tree`, `rev-parse`,\\n`show`, `archive`, `checkout`, `read-tree`, `verify-pack`, or source-selected\\nGit command. Absence of the transformation flags means `cat-file --batch`\\nreturns raw uncompressed object contents, not checkout/filter/textconv/mailmap\\noutput.\\n\\nThe manager sends requests sequentially. Each request is exactly:\\n\\n```text\\n<full-lowercase-object-id> LF\\n```\\n\\nThe ID is 40 lowercase hexadecimal bytes for SHA-1 or 64 for SHA-256 and is\\neither the already validated selected ID or an ID parsed from a recomputed\\ncommit, tag, or tree object. No abbreviated ID, whitespace, object/path suffix,\\nrevision operator, ref, command word, NUL, CR, or second line is admitted.\\n\\nFor each request, stdout is parsed as exactly:\\n\\n```text\\n<same-full-lowercase-object-id> SP <type> SP <decimal-size> LF\\n<exactly decimal-size content bytes> LF\\n```\\n\\n`type` is one ASCII token from `commit`, `tag`, `tree`, or `blob`. Size is `0`\\nor `[1-9][0-9]*`, fits unsigned 64-bit and manager policy, and has no sign or\\nleading zero. The returned ID MUST byte-equal the request. The manager reads\\nexactly the declared content size and one terminal LF before issuing another\\nrequest. `missing`, `ambiguous`, `excluded`, `submodule`, unexpected type,\\nmalformed/truncated/extra bytes, early EOF, trailing output after stdin close,\\nnonzero exit, timeout, or limit violation fails\\n`build_repository_incomplete_source`. Stdin closes after the last required\\nobject; a clean child exits zero.\\n\\nThe manager bounds unique object count, individual object bytes, total expanded\\nbytes, tree depth, path depth/length, tag peel depth, diagnostics, and wall\\ntime. Duplicate graph references reuse already verified bytes by full ID and\\ndo not consume another logical object slot. Limits are manager policy with\\nportable minimums fixed by conformance; exceeding any limit fails before audit\\nand never creates a partial snapshot claim.\\n\\n#### 6.6.2 Manager parsing and graph proof\\n\\nFor both network and local sources, manager code—not Git pretty-printers—uses\\none byte-level semantic parser after object-ID recomputation. Let `H` be 40\\nlowercase hexadecimal bytes for SHA-1 and 64 for SHA-256. The parser applies\\nthe same limits and result codes regardless of acquisition path.\\n\\nThe common **header-record grammar** is:\\n\\n1. An object is bounded before parsing and contains no NUL byte anywhere.\\n2. Its header block uses LF only. CR is forbidden before the header/body\\n   separator. The separator is the first exact `LF LF` and is required even\\n   when the message is empty. A lone final header LF is not enough.\\n3. A primary header line is\\n   `<key> SP <value> LF`. `key` is one or more ASCII letters, digits, or `-`,\\n   begins with an ASCII letter, and has no normalization. `value` is a bounded\\n   byte string with no LF, CR, or NUL and may be empty only for an\\n   implementation-ignored extra header.\\n4. Zero or more continuation lines may follow an extra header only. Each is\\n   `SP <continuation-bytes> LF`; the continuation bytes may be empty and\\n   contain no LF, CR, or NUL. A continuation before the first header or after a\\n   required structural header is invalid. This admits ordinary `gpgsig`,\\n   `gpgsig-sha256`, and `mergetag` records without interpreting or verifying\\n   their signatures.\\n5. Bytes after the separator are a bounded opaque message. They may contain\\n   LF, CR, non-UTF-8 bytes, and signature armor, but not NUL. Message bytes\\n   never select a ref, tree, target, cache key field, credential, or process.\\n\\nA selected **commit** MUST match this ordered grammar:\\n\\n1. The first record is exactly `tree <H-hex>` with no continuation.\\n2. Zero or more `parent <H-hex>` records follow contiguously, each without a\\n   continuation. Their byte order is retained as object semantics; the source\\n   snapshot does not traverse parent history. Repeated parent IDs are allowed\\n   because they do not select compiler input.\\n3. Exactly one non-empty `author <opaque-ident>` record and then exactly one\\n   non-empty `committer <opaque-ident>` record follow, both without\\n   continuation. Their bounded values are metadata only; the manager does not\\n   reinterpret names, email, time, timezone, or signature trust.\\n4. Zero or more extra records may follow the committer and may use the common\\n   continuation grammar. An extra record MUST NOT use any reserved key\\n   `tree`, `parent`, `author`, or `committer`; this makes a duplicate or\\n   misplaced structural header invalid rather than implementation-selected.\\n   Non-reserved extra keys may repeat and remain ordered opaque metadata.\\n5. The exact separator follows the last record. The one tree ID is the only\\n   commit field used to select snapshot content.\\n\\nA selected **annotated tag** MUST match this ordered grammar:\\n\\n1. The first record is exactly `object <H-hex>` and the second is exactly\\n   `type <commit-or-tag>`, neither with a continuation.\\n2. The third is exactly `tag <safe-tag-name>` with no continuation. It uses\\n   section 3.2's byte grammar; the outermost tag record selected through an\\n   exact tag ref MUST name that requested tag. Nested tag names are validated\\n   but need not equal the outer ref.\\n3. At most one non-empty `tagger <opaque-ident>` record may follow, without a\\n   continuation. Early tag objects may omit it.\\n4. Zero or more non-reserved extra records may then use the common\\n   continuation grammar. `object`, `type`, `tag`, and `tagger` are reserved\\n   and cannot reappear or occur out of order. Signed tag armor belongs to the\\n   opaque message after the separator and is accepted without verification.\\n5. After requesting and independently recomputing `object`, the actual\\n   `cat-file` type MUST byte-equal the tag's declared `type`. `tag` continues\\n   peeling; `commit` terminates it. A `tree` or `blob` target is invalid for\\n   this driver. The chain contains at most 16 annotated tag objects, and no\\n   full object ID may repeat. Lightweight refs point directly to a commit and\\n   consume zero tag-depth slots.\\n\\nAny malformed commit/tag header, wrong width/case, duplicate or misplaced\\nreserved header, bad/missing separator, unexpected/declared type mismatch,\\ntag cycle, or tag-depth overflow fails the single stable pre-audit error:\\n\\n```text\\nbuild_repository_git_object_semantics_invalid\\n```\\n\\nThe manager maps the common semantic parser to this result on both network and\\nlocal-store paths. A network fetch may reject corrupt bytes earlier through\\nthe already required transfer fsck, but it MUST NOT turn a known object\\nsemantic rejection into availability, cache-hit, or audit success.\\n\\nAfter a terminal commit passes:\\n\\n1. the manager requests its one tree ID, recomputes it, and requires actual\\n   type `tree`;\\n2. it parses each tree as the Git binary sequence\\n   `<octal-mode> SP <name-bytes> NUL <H-byte-object-id>`, consuming the entire\\n   object with no empty/duplicate name, slash, NUL, `.`/`..`, invalid UTF-8,\\n   nonportable component, or platform collision;\\n3. it accepts only tree mode `40000` and blob modes `100644`/`100755`; symlink\\n   `120000`, gitlink `160000`, unknown modes, and mode/type mismatch fail;\\n4. it reads and recomputes every reachable tree and blob, proving every\\n   required object locally available without replace, graft, alternate,\\n   promisor, lazy-fetch, checkout, attribute, filter, textconv, mailmap, or\\n   archive transformation;\\n5. it runs section 6.6.3's all-blob detector before materialization or audit;\\n6. it materializes exact verified blob bytes into a frozen regular-file\\n   snapshot with manager filesystem code, preserving only the executable bit\\n   distinction from `100755`/`100644`.\\n\\nGitlinks fail before reading `.gitmodules`. Parent history, human identity,\\ncommit/tag messages, signatures, and extra headers remain untrusted bounded\\nmetadata and are never used as provenance.\\n\\nRequired cross-language semantic fixtures use UTF-8 only for compact display;\\nthe parser itself is byte-oriented:\\n\\n```json\\n[\\n  {\\n    \\\"name\\\": \\\"valid-commit-with-signed-and-extra-headers\\\",\\n    \\\"source_paths\\\": [\\\"network-private-store\\\", \\\"local-inert-copy\\\"],\\n    \\\"object_type\\\": \\\"commit\\\",\\n    \\\"object_format\\\": \\\"sha1\\\",\\n    \\\"content_utf8\\\": \\\"tree aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa\\\\nparent bbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbb\\\\nauthor A U Thor <a@example.test> 1 +0000\\\\ncommitter C O Mitter <c@example.test> 2 +0000\\\\ngpgsig -----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\\\\n payload\\\\n -----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\\\\nx-curator-fixture opaque\\\\n\\\\nmessage\\\\r\\\\nbody\\\\n\\\",\\n    \\\"expected\\\": \\\"valid\\\",\\n    \\\"tree\\\": \\\"aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa\\\"\\n  },\\n  {\\n    \\\"name\\\": \\\"reject-duplicate-tree-header\\\",\\n    \\\"source_paths\\\": [\\\"network-private-store\\\", \\\"local-inert-copy\\\"],\\n    \\\"object_type\\\": \\\"commit\\\",\\n    \\\"object_format\\\": \\\"sha1\\\",\\n    \\\"content_utf8\\\": \\\"tree aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa\\\\ntree bbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbb\\\\nauthor A <a@b> 1 +0000\\\\ncommitter C <c@d> 2 +0000\\\\n\\\\nmessage\\\\n\\\",\\n    \\\"expected_error\\\": \\\"build_repository_git_object_semantics_invalid\\\"\\n  },\\n  {\\n    \\\"name\\\": \\\"reject-misordered-tree-after-parent\\\",\\n    \\\"source_paths\\\": [\\\"network-private-store\\\", \\\"local-inert-copy\\\"],\\n    \\\"object_type\\\": \\\"commit\\\",\\n    \\\"object_format\\\": \\\"sha1\\\",\\n    \\\"content_utf8\\\": \\\"parent bbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbb\\\\ntree aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa\\\\nauthor A <a@b> 1 +0000\\\\ncommitter C <c@d> 2 +0000\\\\n\\\\nmessage\\\\n\\\",\\n    \\\"expected_error\\\": \\\"build_repository_git_object_semantics_invalid\\\"\\n  },\\n  {\\n    \\\"name\\\": \\\"reject-missing-header-message-separator\\\",\\n    \\\"source_paths\\\": [\\\"network-private-store\\\", \\\"local-inert-copy\\\"],\\n    \\\"object_type\\\": \\\"commit\\\",\\n    \\\"object_format\\\": \\\"sha1\\\",\\n    \\\"content_utf8\\\": \\\"tree aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa\\\\nauthor A <a@b> 1 +0000\\\\ncommitter C <c@d> 2 +0000\\\\nmessage\\\\n\\\",\\n    \\\"expected_error\\\": \\\"build_repository_git_object_semantics_invalid\\\"\\n  },\\n  {\\n    \\\"name\\\": \\\"valid-signed-annotated-tag\\\",\\n    \\\"source_paths\\\": [\\\"network-private-store\\\", \\\"local-inert-copy\\\"],\\n    \\\"object_type\\\": \\\"tag\\\",\\n    \\\"object_format\\\": \\\"sha1\\\",\\n    \\\"content_utf8\\\": \\\"object aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa\\\\ntype commit\\\\ntag v1.4.0\\\\ntagger T Agger <t@example.test> 3 +0000\\\\n\\\\nrelease\\\\n-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\\\\nopaque\\\\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\\\\n\\\",\\n    \\\"resolved_target_type\\\": \\\"commit\\\",\\n    \\\"expected\\\": \\\"valid\\\"\\n  },\\n  {\\n    \\\"name\\\": \\\"reject-duplicate-object-and-type-headers\\\",\\n    \\\"source_paths\\\": [\\\"network-private-store\\\", \\\"local-inert-copy\\\"],\\n    \\\"object_type\\\": \\\"tag\\\",\\n    \\\"object_format\\\": \\\"sha1\\\",\\n    \\\"content_utf8\\\": \\\"object aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa\\\\nobject bbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbb\\\\ntype commit\\\\ntype tag\\\\ntag v1.4.0\\\\n\\\\nmessage\\\\n\\\",\\n    \\\"resolved_target_type\\\": \\\"commit\\\",\\n    \\\"expected_error\\\": \\\"build_repository_git_object_semantics_invalid\\\"\\n  },\\n  {\\n    \\\"name\\\": \\\"reject-tag-declared-target-type-mismatch\\\",\\n    \\\"source_paths\\\": [\\\"network-private-store\\\", \\\"local-inert-copy\\\"],\\n    \\\"object_type\\\": \\\"tag\\\",\\n    \\\"object_format\\\": \\\"sha1\\\",\\n    \\\"content_utf8\\\": \\\"object aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa\\\\ntype tag\\\\ntag v1.4.0\\\\n\\\\nmessage\\\\n\\\",\\n    \\\"resolved_target_type\\\": \\\"commit\\\",\\n    \\\"expected_error\\\": \\\"build_repository_git_object_semantics_invalid\\\"\\n  }\\n]\\n```\\n\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n/Users/iv/Developer/ReluxWorks/curator/.task-board/.resources/TASK-260728-1a52au/TASK-260728-1a52au_spawn-log_-implementer--doc-writer--codex-_RUN-260727-a527c3.log:43:{\"type\":\"item.started\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_21\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"sed -n '1166,1502p' .task-board/.resources/TASK-260720-1nvomm/TASK-260720-1nvomm_external-build-repositories-architecture-v6.md\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"\",\"exit_code\":null,\"status\":\"in_progress\"}}\n/Users/iv/Developer/ReluxWorks/curator/.task-board/.resources/TASK-260728-1a52au/TASK-260728-1a52au_spawn-log_-implementer--doc-writer--codex-_RUN-260727-a527c3.log:44:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_21\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"sed -n '1166,1502p' .task-board/.resources/TASK-260720-1nvomm/TASK-260720-1nvomm_external-build-repositories-architecture-v6.md\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"#### 6.6.3 Exact all-blob Git LFS rejection\\n\\nThe manager scans every reachable blob exactly once, regardless of path,\\nextension, mode, selected build root, `.gitattributes`, or later `go list`\\nselection. This happens after object-ID recomputation and before snapshot\\nmaterialization, audit, cache lookup, or compiler execution. The detector never\\ninvokes `git-lfs`, a filter, or the network.\\n\\nThe supported parser-family invariant is exactly\\n`git-lfs-pointer-parser-v3.7.1`: the decoder and encoder behavior in the\\nofficial Git LFS `v3.7.1` `lfs/pointer.go`, exercised by the Git LFS 3.7.1\\nbinary in conformance. A different parser family requires a reviewed detector\\nrevision. The protocol does not claim that the published pointer specification\\nalone describes every byte form accepted by that implementation.\\n\\nA zero-byte blob is admitted because Git LFS encodes the empty file as empty\\nGit content. Every non-empty blob of 1 through 1023 bytes is a candidate.\\nExactly 1024 bytes or more is outside both the pinned decoder cutoff and this\\ndetector, even if a shorter prefix looks like a pointer.\\n\\nFor a candidate, manager code applies this bounded compatibility algorithm:\\n\\n1. Remove the maximal leading and trailing sequence of Unicode White_Space\\n   scalar encodings recognized by Go 1.25's `unicode.IsSpace`: ASCII HT, LF,\\n   VT, FF, CR, SP; U+0085, U+00A0, U+1680, U+2000 through U+200A, U+2028,\\n   U+2029, U+202F, U+205F, and U+3000. Invalid UTF-8 outside those exact\\n   encodings is not trimmed. This is the cross-language definition of the\\n   pinned decoder's `bytes.TrimSpace`.\\n2. Split the remainder at LF. Remove one terminal CR from each line, as Go's\\n   `bufio.ScanLines` does, and ignore empty lines. Embedded CR and invalid\\n   UTF-8 remain ordinary bytes.\\n3. Each non-empty line must contain an ASCII SP. Split at the first SP only;\\n   the bytes before it are `key` and all remaining bytes are `value`.\\n4. Required keys occur exactly once and in parser state order:\\n   `version`, then `oid`, then `size`. While waiting for any required key, an\\n   extension line may occur; no line may occur after `size`.\\n5. `version` equals exactly one of:\\n   `https://git-lfs.github.com/spec/v1`,\\n   `https://hawser.github.com/spec/v1`, or\\n   `http://git-media.io/v/2`.\\n6. `oid` equals `sha256:` plus exactly 64 lowercase hexadecimal bytes.\\n7. `size` is the exact input grammar accepted by Go `strconv.ParseInt` with\\n   base 10 and width 64, and its mathematical value is nonnegative. Thus\\n   `+1`, `00`, and `01` are accepted noncanonical forms; a negative value,\\n   overflow above signed 64-bit, whitespace, or non-decimal byte is not.\\n8. An extension key begins exactly `ext-`, one ASCII digit `0` through `9`,\\n   `-`, then at least one ASCII `[A-Za-z0-9_]` byte. After that first name\\n   byte, every byte other than ASCII SP or LF belongs to the name; there is no\\n   end-anchored name regexp. This deliberately covers lowercase/dot/dash,\\n   `_`, uppercase, and names whose accepted prefix is followed by punctuation\\n   such as `a!`, matching the pinned `\\\\Aext-\\\\d{1}-\\\\w+` behavior. The extension\\n   value uses the exact OID grammar from step 6.\\n9. Extension entries are first collected by their entire raw key, with a later\\n   exact duplicate key replacing the earlier value as in the pinned map.\\n   After collection, distinct surviving keys MUST have unique numeric\\n   priorities. A repeated exact key is accepted but noncanonical; distinct\\n   keys with the same priority are rejected by the pinned family and therefore\\n   do not match this detector.\\n10. Missing required keys, an unknown non-extension key, malformed OID/size,\\n    an extension name not beginning with an ASCII word byte, a bad extension\\n    value, or distinct duplicate priorities ends the match.\\n\\nEvery successful compatibility match is rejected, not merely strict/canonical\\nmatches. Classification is diagnostic only:\\n\\n- `canonical` means the original blob bytes exactly equal the pinned encoder:\\n  current version first; surviving extensions sorted by numeric priority and\\n  emitted as their raw names; then `oid`, `size`, and one final LF, with no\\n  trimming, blank line, duplicate key, or alternate spelling.\\n- a non-empty pointer whose parsed size is zero is always\\n  `legacy-or-noncanonical`, because the pinned encoder emits an empty blob for\\n  size zero;\\n- aliases, CRLF, omitted/folded endings, leading/trailing whitespace, blank\\n  lines, reordered extension placement, exact duplicate keys, signed/zero-\\n  padded sizes, or any other accepted bytes unequal to the encoder are also\\n  `legacy-or-noncanonical`.\\n\\nBoth classifications fail the same stable pre-audit error:\\n\\n```text\\nbuild_repository_git_lfs_unsupported\\n```\\n\\nThe false-positive boundary is closed: only a non-empty blob below 1024 bytes\\nthat completes all ten compatibility steps is rejected. A 1024-byte blob,\\nunknown version, uppercase/bad OID, missing required key, extension beginning\\nwith punctuation, or two distinct extension keys sharing a priority remains\\nordinary blob content. The diagnostic may identify classification and path,\\nbut MUST NOT print the pointer OID as a fetch instruction. The manager never\\nhydrates from `.git/lfs`, an LFS endpoint, a filter, or local operator state.\\n\\nRequired cross-language fixtures:\\n\\n```json\\n[\\n  {\\n    \\\"name\\\": \\\"canonical-current-pointer\\\",\\n    \\\"bytes_utf8\\\": \\\"version https://git-lfs.github.com/spec/v1\\\\noid sha256:aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa\\\\nsize 1\\\\n\\\",\\n    \\\"expected_error\\\": \\\"build_repository_git_lfs_unsupported\\\",\\n    \\\"classification\\\": \\\"canonical\\\"\\n  },\\n  {\\n    \\\"name\\\": \\\"canonical-extension-name-family\\\",\\n    \\\"bytes_utf8\\\": \\\"version https://git-lfs.github.com/spec/v1\\\\next-1-lower.dot-dash sha256:bbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbb\\\\next-2-_under sha256:cccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccc\\\\next-3-A_B sha256:dddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddd\\\\next-4-a! sha256:eeeeeeeeeeeeeeeeeeeeeeeeeeeeeeeeeeeeeeeeeeeeeeeeeeeeeeeeeeeeeeee\\\\noid sha256:aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa\\\\nsize 1\\\\n\\\",\\n    \\\"expected_error\\\": \\\"build_repository_git_lfs_unsupported\\\",\\n    \\\"classification\\\": \\\"canonical\\\",\\n    \\\"git_lfs_3_7_1_strict_exit\\\": 0\\n  },\\n  {\\n    \\\"name\\\": \\\"accepted-crlf-blank-unsorted-and-no-terminal-lf\\\",\\n    \\\"bytes_utf8\\\": \\\"\\\\r\\\\nversion https://git-lfs.github.com/spec/v1\\\\r\\\\next-4-a! sha256:eeeeeeeeeeeeeeeeeeeeeeeeeeeeeeeeeeeeeeeeeeeeeeeeeeeeeeeeeeeeeeee\\\\r\\\\n\\\\r\\\\next-1-lower sha256:bbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbb\\\\r\\\\noid sha256:aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa\\\\r\\\\nsize +01\\\",\\n    \\\"expected_error\\\": \\\"build_repository_git_lfs_unsupported\\\",\\n    \\\"classification\\\": \\\"legacy-or-noncanonical\\\"\\n  },\\n  {\\n    \\\"name\\\": \\\"accepted-exact-duplicate-key-last-value-wins\\\",\\n    \\\"bytes_utf8\\\": \\\"version https://git-lfs.github.com/spec/v1\\\\next-1-a sha256:bbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbb\\\\next-1-a sha256:cccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccc\\\\noid sha256:aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa\\\\nsize 1\\\\n\\\",\\n    \\\"expected_error\\\": \\\"build_repository_git_lfs_unsupported\\\",\\n    \\\"classification\\\": \\\"legacy-or-noncanonical\\\"\\n  },\\n  {\\n    \\\"name\\\": \\\"distinct-duplicate-priority-is-not-accepted\\\",\\n    \\\"bytes_utf8\\\": \\\"version https://git-lfs.github.com/spec/v1\\\\next-1-a sha256:bbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbb\\\\next-1-b sha256:cccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccc\\\\noid sha256:aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa\\\\nsize 1\\\\n\\\",\\n    \\\"expected\\\": \\\"ordinary-blob\\\"\\n  },\\n  {\\n    \\\"name\\\": \\\"nonempty-size-zero-is-deliberately-noncanonical\\\",\\n    \\\"bytes_utf8\\\": \\\"version https://git-lfs.github.com/spec/v1\\\\noid sha256:aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa\\\\nsize 0\\\\n\\\",\\n    \\\"expected_error\\\": \\\"build_repository_git_lfs_unsupported\\\",\\n    \\\"classification\\\": \\\"legacy-or-noncanonical\\\",\\n    \\\"git_lfs_3_7_1_strict_exit\\\": 2\\n  },\\n  {\\n    \\\"name\\\": \\\"cutoff-1023-after-trim\\\",\\n    \\\"construction\\\": {\\n      \\\"prefix_utf8\\\": \\\"version https://git-lfs.github.com/spec/v1\\\\noid sha256:aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa\\\\nsize 1\\\\n\\\",\\n      \\\"pad_byte_hex\\\": \\\"20\\\",\\n      \\\"total_bytes\\\": 1023\\n    },\\n    \\\"expected_error\\\": \\\"build_repository_git_lfs_unsupported\\\",\\n    \\\"classification\\\": \\\"legacy-or-noncanonical\\\"\\n  },\\n  {\\n    \\\"name\\\": \\\"cutoff-1024-is-ordinary\\\",\\n    \\\"construction\\\": {\\n      \\\"prefix_utf8\\\": \\\"version https://git-lfs.github.com/spec/v1\\\\noid sha256:aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa\\\\nsize 1\\\\n\\\",\\n      \\\"pad_byte_hex\\\": \\\"20\\\",\\n      \\\"total_bytes\\\": 1024\\n    },\\n    \\\"expected\\\": \\\"ordinary-blob\\\"\\n  },\\n  {\\n    \\\"name\\\": \\\"near-miss-extension-starts-with-punctuation\\\",\\n    \\\"bytes_utf8\\\": \\\"version https://git-lfs.github.com/spec/v1\\\\next-1-!a sha256:bbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbb\\\\noid sha256:aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa\\\\nsize 1\\\\n\\\",\\n    \\\"expected\\\": \\\"ordinary-blob\\\"\\n  },\\n  {\\n    \\\"name\\\": \\\"near-miss-uppercase-oid\\\",\\n    \\\"bytes_utf8\\\": \\\"version https://git-lfs.github.com/spec/v1\\\\noid sha256:AAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAA\\\\nsize 1\\\\n\\\",\\n    \\\"expected\\\": \\\"ordinary-blob\\\"\\n  }\\n]\\n```\\n\\nNo audit or artifact-cache lookup begins until the object proof, LFS scan,\\nmaterialization, whole-snapshot validation, and\\n`curator-build-source-v1` digest succeed. Object bytes are transport input, not\\nprovenance, and the object store is never agent-facing, runtime-copied, or used\\ndirectly as build source.\\n\\n## 7. Snapshot identity and audit equivalence\\n\\nThe consuming skill snapshot and each effective external repository snapshot are independent untrusted audit subjects.\\n\\nFor every external repository selected by the active plan:\\n\\n1. for an unsubstituted declaration containing `tag`, complete the exact-tag\\n   acquisition and terminal-lock equality check from section 6.3;\\n2. validate the whole repository snapshot;\\n3. compute `curator-build-source-v1` over every regular file, including `curator-build.json` and files outside the selected build root;\\n4. parse the descriptor and select the target;\\n5. audit the complete external snapshot under declared/effective identity, object format, effective commit, build-source digest, target, substitution state, and the verified tag assertion when applicable;\\n6. apply allowlist, revocation, registry, tag-lock, and audit policy gates;\\n7. only then inspect an artifact-cache candidate or start a compiler child.\\n\\nRegistry evidence for the skill does not attest the external repository. External evidence does not attest the skill. Audit cache reuse is allowed only when its subject tuple exactly matches the declared/effective state and snapshot digest; it does not bypass snapshot admission.\\n\\nA syntax-only offline check that cannot obtain an exact protected snapshot warns `build_repository_unverified_offline` and makes no source/audit/build claim. Install, update, repair, and coverage-claiming audit fail before mutation when the exact effective snapshot cannot be obtained and audited.\\n\\nDry-run may use network and removable operation-private fetch state, but it leaves no persistent repository, snapshot, audit response, lock, receipt, marker, or cache mutation. It must validate and audit the exact snapshot before reporting cache-hit or would-build.\\n\\n## 8. Receipt v2, cache identity, and mixed schema-7 builds\\n\\n### 8.1 Unsubstituted external receipt input\\n\\nReceipt-v2 input binds the complete source state:\\n\\n```json\\n{\\n  \\\"schema_version\\\": 2,\\n  \\\"driver\\\": \\\"go-repository-v1\\\",\\n  \\\"source\\\": {\\n    \\\"repository\\\": \\\"golden-tools\\\",\\n    \\\"declared\\\": {\\n      \\\"identity\\\": {\\n        \\\"kind\\\": \\\"network-git\\\",\\n        \\\"value\\\": \\\"github.com/example/golden-tools\\\"\\n      },\\n      \\\"transport\\\": \\\"https\\\",\\n      \\\"locked_commit\\\": {\\n        \\\"object_format\\\": \\\"sha1\\\",\\n        \\\"hex\\\": \\\"0123456789abcdef0123456789abcdef01234567\\\"\\n      },\\n      \\\"tag\\\": \\\"v1.4.0\\\"\\n    },\\n    \\\"effective\\\": {\\n      \\\"identity\\\": {\\n        \\\"kind\\\": \\\"network-git\\\",\\n        \\\"value\\\": \\\"github.com/example/golden-tools\\\"\\n      },\\n      \\\"transport\\\": \\\"https\\\",\\n      \\\"object_format\\\": \\\"sha1\\\",\\n      \\\"commit\\\": \\\"0123456789abcdef0123456789abcdef01234567\\\",\\n      \\\"substituted\\\": false,\\n      \\\"build_source\\\": {\\n        \\\"algorithm\\\": \\\"curator-build-source-v1\\\",\\n        \\\"content_sha256\\\": \\\"sha256:bbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbb\\\"\\n      }\\n    },\\n    \\\"descriptor\\\": {\\n      \\\"path\\\": \\\"curator-build.json\\\",\\n      \\\"target\\\": \\\"golden-tool\\\"\\n    }\\n  },\\n  \\\"command\\\": \\\"golden-tool\\\",\\n  \\\"build_root\\\": \\\".\\\",\\n  \\\"source_dir\\\": \\\"cmd/golden-tool\\\",\\n  \\\"target\\\": {\\n    \\\"goos\\\": \\\"darwin\\\",\\n    \\\"goarch\\\": \\\"arm64\\\",\\n    \\\"tuning\\\": {\\n      \\\"GOARM64\\\": \\\"v8.0\\\"\\n    }\\n  },\\n  \\\"toolchain\\\": {\\n    \\\"algorithm\\\": \\\"curator-go-toolchain-v1\\\",\\n    \\\"go_relpath\\\": \\\"bin/go\\\",\\n    \\\"go_version\\\": \\\"go version go1.26.1 darwin/arm64\\\",\\n    \\\"content_sha256\\\": \\\"sha256:cccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccc\\\"\\n  },\\n  \\\"policy\\\": {\\n    \\\"module_mode\\\": \\\"vendor\\\",\\n    \\\"network\\\": \\\"none\\\",\\n    \\\"workspace\\\": false,\\n    \\\"cgo\\\": false,\\n    \\\"compiler_directives\\\": \\\"reject-nonstandard-cgo-import-dynamic-v1\\\",\\n    \\\"target_mode\\\": \\\"native\\\",\\n    \\\"link_mode\\\": \\\"internal\\\",\\n    \\\"libgcc\\\": \\\"none\\\",\\n    \\\"package_assembly\\\": false,\\n    \\\"host_objects\\\": false,\\n    \\\"telemetry\\\": \\\"off-private\\\",\\n    \\\"source_kind\\\": \\\"locked-external-git-v1\\\"\\n  }\\n}\\n```\\n\\n### 8.2 Substituted external receipt input\\n\\nOnly the source portion changes for a local substitution:\\n\\n```json\\n{\\n  \\\"repository\\\": \\\"golden-tools\\\",\\n  \\\"declared\\\": {\\n    \\\"identity\\\": {\\n      \\\"kind\\\": \\\"network-git\\\",\\n      \\\"value\\\": \\\"github.com/example/golden-tools\\\"\\n    },\\n    \\\"transport\\\": \\\"https\\\",\\n    \\\"locked_commit\\\": {\\n      \\\"object_format\\\": \\\"sha1\\\",\\n      \\\"hex\\\": \\\"0123456789abcdef0123456789abcdef01234567\\\"\\n    },\\n    \\\"tag\\\": \\\"v1.4.0\\\"\\n  },\\n  \\\"effective\\\": {\\n    \\\"identity\\\": {\\n      \\\"kind\\\": \\\"operator-local-git\\\",\\n      \\\"value\\\": \\\"sha256:eeeeeeeeeeeeeeeeeeeeeeeeeeeeeeeeeeeeeeeeeeeeeeeeeeeeeeeeeeeeeeee\\\"\\n    },\\n    \\\"object_format\\\": \\\"sha1\\\",\\n    \\\"commit\\\": \\\"89abcdef0123456789abcdef0123456789abcdef\\\",\\n    \\\"substituted\\\": true,\\n    \\\"substitution\\\": {\\n      \\\"type\\\": \\\"local-path\\\"\\n    },\\n    \\\"build_source\\\": {\\n      \\\"algorithm\\\": \\\"curator-build-source-v1\\\",\\n      \\\"content_sha256\\\": \\\"sha256:ffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffff\\\"\\n    }\\n  },\\n  \\\"descriptor\\\": {\\n    \\\"path\\\": \\\"curator-build.json\\\",\\n    \\\"target\\\": \\\"golden-tool\\\"\\n  }\\n}\\n```\\n\\nA network substitution instead uses `identity.kind: \\\"network-git\\\"`, its canonical value and transport, and `substitution: {\\\"type\\\":\\\"network-git\\\",\\\"ref\\\":<structured-ref>}`.\\n\\nThe complete receipt-v2 input is canonicalized with CCJ-1. Its SHA-256 is the artifact cache key. The key therefore cannot describe declared provenance while compiling substituted bytes.\\n\\nReceipt v2 retains exact canonical stored bytes, manager-derived artifact path, artifact SHA-256, and byte size. It has no trust/provenance boolean. Protected-state admission remains out of band.\\n\\nFor `effective.substituted: false`, the presence of `declared.tag` is a semantic\\nreceipt precondition: the writer may emit and publish the receipt only after\\nthat operation's exact-tag acquisition proves the tag terminates at\\n`declared.locked_commit`. A self-asserted `tag_verified` boolean is not added;\\nit would not prove the fetch. Receipt admission instead depends on the fixed\\noperation ordering and protected snapshot/receipt boundary. A substituted\\nreceipt records the manifest's declared tag only as declared state and makes no\\nclaim that the remote tag was consulted while compiling substituted bytes.\\n\\n### 8.3 Mixed command behavior\\n\\nOne schema-7 installation may contain both command kinds:\\n\\n- local `go-v1` commands keep receipt v1 and the consuming skill's raw `build_source`;\\n- external `go-repository-v1` commands use receipt v2 and per-command external source state;\\n- the two drivers never share receipt interpretation or logical cache keys;\\n- marker v3 represents both simultaneously;\\n- top-level marker `build_source` is present exactly when at least one active local `go-v1` command exists and retains schema-6 meaning;\\n- external-only installations omit top-level skill `build_source`; each external build entry carries its source identity and digest.\\n\\nNo field is inferred from a driver name when parsing a receipt or marker; schemas use explicit conditional branches.\\n\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n/Users/iv/Developer/ReluxWorks/curator/.task-board/.resources/TASK-260728-1a52au/TASK-260728-1a52au_spawn-log_-implementer--doc-writer--codex-_RUN-260727-a527c3.log:45:{\"type\":\"item.started\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_22\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"sed -n '1503,1979p' .task-board/.resources/TASK-260720-1nvomm/TASK-260720-1nvomm_external-build-repositories-architecture-v6.md\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"\",\"exit_code\":null,\"status\":\"in_progress\"}}\n/Users/iv/Developer/ReluxWorks/curator/.task-board/.resources/TASK-260728-1a52au/TASK-260728-1a52au_spawn-log_-implementer--doc-writer--codex-_RUN-260727-a527c3.log:46:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_22\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"sed -n '1503,1979p' .task-board/.resources/TASK-260720-1nvomm/TASK-260720-1nvomm_external-build-repositories-architecture-v6.md\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"## 9. Marker v3, status, repair, deduplication, and GC\\n\\nIllustrative mixed marker excerpt:\\n\\n```json\\n{\\n  \\\"schema_version\\\": 3,\\n  \\\"skill_schema_version\\\": 7,\\n  \\\"build_source\\\": {\\n    \\\"algorithm\\\": \\\"curator-build-source-v1\\\",\\n    \\\"content_sha256\\\": \\\"sha256:aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa\\\"\\n  },\\n  \\\"build_roots\\\": [\\n    \\\"build\\\"\\n  ],\\n  \\\"builds\\\": {\\n    \\\"local-helper\\\": {\\n      \\\"driver\\\": \\\"go-v1\\\",\\n      \\\"receipt_schema_version\\\": 1,\\n      \\\"cache_key\\\": \\\"sha256:1111111111111111111111111111111111111111111111111111111111111111\\\",\\n      \\\"receipt_sha256\\\": \\\"sha256:2222222222222222222222222222222222222222222222222222222222222222\\\",\\n      \\\"artifact_sha256\\\": \\\"sha256:3333333333333333333333333333333333333333333333333333333333333333\\\",\\n      \\\"artifact_path\\\": \\\"bin/local-helper\\\"\\n    },\\n    \\\"golden-tool\\\": {\\n      \\\"driver\\\": \\\"go-repository-v1\\\",\\n      \\\"receipt_schema_version\\\": 2,\\n      \\\"repository\\\": \\\"golden-tools\\\",\\n      \\\"declared_identity\\\": {\\n        \\\"kind\\\": \\\"network-git\\\",\\n        \\\"value\\\": \\\"github.com/example/golden-tools\\\"\\n      },\\n      \\\"declared_locked_commit\\\": {\\n        \\\"object_format\\\": \\\"sha1\\\",\\n        \\\"hex\\\": \\\"0123456789abcdef0123456789abcdef01234567\\\"\\n      },\\n      \\\"declared_tag\\\": \\\"v1.4.0\\\",\\n      \\\"effective_identity\\\": {\\n        \\\"kind\\\": \\\"network-git\\\",\\n        \\\"value\\\": \\\"github.com/example/golden-tools\\\"\\n      },\\n      \\\"object_format\\\": \\\"sha1\\\",\\n      \\\"commit\\\": \\\"0123456789abcdef0123456789abcdef01234567\\\",\\n      \\\"substituted\\\": false,\\n      \\\"build_source\\\": {\\n        \\\"algorithm\\\": \\\"curator-build-source-v1\\\",\\n        \\\"content_sha256\\\": \\\"sha256:bbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbb\\\"\\n      },\\n      \\\"descriptor_target\\\": \\\"golden-tool\\\",\\n      \\\"cache_key\\\": \\\"sha256:4444444444444444444444444444444444444444444444444444444444444444\\\",\\n      \\\"receipt_sha256\\\": \\\"sha256:5555555555555555555555555555555555555555555555555555555555555555\\\",\\n      \\\"artifact_sha256\\\": \\\"sha256:6666666666666666666666666666666666666666666666666666666666666666\\\",\\n      \\\"artifact_path\\\": \\\"bin/golden-tool\\\"\\n    }\\n  }\\n}\\n```\\n\\nFor an unsubstituted external build, `declared_tag` may be written only from a\\nreceipt whose producing operation completed the exact-tag/locked-commit check.\\nMarker parsing does not turn that field into independent provenance, and\\nread-only status does not contact the remote to re-test later tag movement.\\n\\n### 9.1 Snapshot and artifact keys\\n\\n- Protected external snapshot key: effective identity kind/value, effective object format, full effective commit, and external build-source digest.\\n- Artifact key: SHA-256 of the entire receipt input, including both declared and effective states, command key, descriptor target, build root/source directory, target, toolchain, and policy.\\n- Snapshot bytes may be deduplicated only when the complete snapshot key matches. Audit decisions remain subject-specific.\\n- Artifacts may be reused only when the complete receipt input and protected entry validate. Different declarations, substitutions, commands, targets, toolchains, or policy revisions do not alias.\\n- Physical object, snapshot, artifact, receipt, lock, and quarantine paths remain implementation-specific.\\n\\n### 9.2 Read-only status\\n\\nStatus MUST NOT fetch, repair, quarantine, alter permissions, refresh a snapshot, invoke a compiler, or execute an artifact.\\n\\nIt:\\n\\n1. derives the current effective plan from manifest and operator dev configuration;\\n2. validates marker v3 and substitution equality;\\n3. revalidates every referenced protected skill/external snapshot and artifact boundary;\\n4. recomputes available build-source identities, receipt inputs/keys, receipt hashes, and artifact relationships;\\n5. structurally validates manager-owned shims and PATH exposure.\\n\\nOutcomes:\\n\\n- an identity, commit, digest, target, receipt, artifact, shim, or substitution mismatch is non-current;\\n- an untrusted or corrupt snapshot/artifact boundary is non-current;\\n- a missing required snapshot is non-current when the marker claims it should exist;\\n- when existing profile semantics distinguish unavailable evidence from drift, a source that cannot be proved read-only may be reported `unknown`, but MUST NOT be reported current and `status --check` MUST return non-zero;\\n- status never contacts the remote merely to test availability or tag movement.\\n\\n### 9.3 Repair\\n\\nRepair resolves the current effective operator substitution if present, otherwise the declared locked source. It reacquires into operation-private state, repeats object proof, validation, identity, and audit, and rebuilds or reuses only after protected admission. It never adopts an untrusted snapshot or artifact by changing permissions.\\n\\nFor an unsubstituted declaration containing `tag`, repair always reacquires by\\nthe exact-tag-only flow and rechecks terminal equality with the immutable lock.\\nIt cannot use a successful direct-object fetch or an old protected snapshot as\\na substitute for that operation's tag assertion.\\n\\nIf repair cannot obtain the exact source, it fails before installation mutation. Publication, marker/shim changes, rollback, and consumer update use the existing manager-home mutation lock and journal.\\n\\n### 9.4 Garbage collection roots\\n\\nGC revalidates cache boundaries and, under the manager-home mutation lock:\\n\\n- valid marker v3 files mark every referenced local skill raw snapshot, external frozen snapshot key, receipt/artifact key, and manager-generated shim relation;\\n- in-flight transaction journals mark every staged/published snapshot and artifact needed for commit or rollback;\\n- valid marker v1/v2 behavior remains unchanged;\\n- receipt content alone is never a live root;\\n- unreadable markers/journals or unprovable cache boundaries retain uncertain entries conservatively;\\n- GC never executes or adopts source or artifact bytes.\\n\\n## 10. Deterministic build, install, PATH, and rollback\\n\\n`go-repository-v1` reuses the accepted `go-v1` trusted Go session, environment, full dependency checks, directive/native-input rejection, fixed `go list`/`go build` vectors, internal-link policy, artifact checks, and no-execution rule. Its source acquisition and receipt semantics are distinct, so the driver ID is distinct.\\n\\nNormative order:\\n\\n1. Validate schema-7 manifest, URL/lock/tag grammar, substitutions, descriptor-independent collisions, and static local build-root exclusions.\\n2. Resolve every exact effective external commit into operation-private state;\\n   for each unsubstituted tagged declaration this means the sole exact-tag\\n   fetch and terminal-lock equality check, never a direct-object attempt.\\n3. Close the Git object boundary, freeze the whole snapshot, compute its digest, parse the descriptor, and validate target/module containment.\\n4. Audit consuming skill and every external repository independently.\\n5. Probe/fingerprint the trusted Go toolchain and derive all local receipt-v1 and external receipt-v2 inputs.\\n6. Inspect protected artifact candidates. On real misses, run fixed preflight/build into operation-private staging. Dry-run stops before source-aware Go commands as defined by the accepted driver policy.\\n7. Validate/hashes artifacts and generate canonical receipts/marker.\\n8. Under the home mutation lock, recover/revalidate, publish immutable snapshots/artifacts, and commit all targets with the consumer ledger last.\\n9. On failure, reverse rollback under the same lock. Pre-publication source/audit/build failures leave live state unchanged.\\n\\nPATH and shims are verified structurally:\\n\\n- command key passes existing portable/collision rules;\\n- shim is manager-owned and points exactly to the marker-selected protected artifact;\\n- artifact is one regular singly linked executable matching receipt path/hash;\\n- no shim points into a Git object database, frozen source snapshot, checkout, staging directory, or script runtime;\\n- environment exposure contains only the manager-generated bin directory;\\n- validation never calls a shell, `command -v`, or the built output.\\n\\nSeveral commands may select one logical descriptor target, but each manifest command key derives its own output and cache input. The repository still cannot request aliases, sidecars, post-build copies, or secondary PATH entries.\\n\\n## 11. Signing and notarization boundary\\n\\nSigning identities and notarization credentials are operator/platform secrets. They MUST NOT appear in a manifest, descriptor, repository, compiler environment, receipt trust field, or marker.\\n\\nThe first `go-repository-v1` release performs no manager post-signing. It does not invoke `codesign`, `signtool`, timestamping, or a notary service.\\n\\nA future local signer requires a separately reviewed profile/driver revision with:\\n\\n- operator-only opaque keychain/certificate/HSM/service identity;\\n- fixed signer executable, argv, entitlements/options, process graph, and network policy;\\n- signer policy/tool identity and signed bytes in cache/receipt identity;\\n- no package entitlements, certificate selector, timestamp URL, or arguments;\\n- private staging, no artifact execution, protected publication, and rollback.\\n\\nUntil standardized, a platform policy requiring local signing rejects source-built artifacts.\\n\\nApple Developer ID signing/notarization and Windows Authenticode/timestamping remain controlled release-pipeline work. A future prebuilt driver may verify signed release artifacts under its own exact digest and attestation policy; local build receipts do not become release provenance.\\n\\nLinux remains a separate later native-validation task. No Linux conformance claim is made until the same driver, sandbox/resource, shim, and lifecycle vectors pass natively.\\n\\n## 12. Future closed-driver rule\\n\\nThe external Git envelope may be shared, but compiler semantics are never generic. Every language adds a new driver ID and independent protocol/security review covering:\\n\\n- complete compiler-visible inputs and dependency containment;\\n- trusted toolchain/sysroot/runtime identity;\\n- fixed process graph, environment, flags, target, output, and signer boundary;\\n- rejection of hooks, plugins, macros, generators, annotation processors, build tasks, recipes, response files, package linkers, and produced-program execution;\\n- offline dependency model and exact source identity;\\n- receipt/marker/cache identity, dry-run, audit-before-build, rollback/recovery, status/repair/GC, and platform vectors.\\n\\nConsequences remain:\\n\\n- Rust: Cargo `build.rs` and procedural macros are rejected; any direct `rustc` driver needs a closed dependency/link model.\\n- Swift: SwiftPM manifests/plugins/macros are rejected; direct `swiftc` must own SDK, target, graph, linker, and macro policy.\\n- Kotlin/JVM: Maven/Gradle, annotation processors, and compiler plugins are rejected; a direct driver owns class/module path, packaging, and JDK/Kotlin identity.\\n- C/C++: Make/CMake/Meson/Ninja are rejected; a direct driver owns sources, preprocessing, headers/sysroot, response files, compiler/linker plugins, native libraries, and output.\\n- .NET: MSBuild, analyzers, and source generators are rejected; a direct driver owns references, runtime packaging, native host, and signing.\\n\\nAdding an external repository does not make an unsafe build frontend safe.\\n\\n## 13. Threat model\\n\\n### 13.1 Adversary-controlled\\n\\n- consuming skill and manifest;\\n- declared Git URL, refs/tags served by the remote, object bytes, paths/modes, descriptor, source/vendor/embed inputs, sizes, diagnostics, and compiled artifact;\\n- source repository configuration and object-database indirection until rejected;\\n- candidate snapshot/receipt/marker/artifact cache bytes until protected admission;\\n- network failure, replay, moved tag, malicious remote, and compiler resource-exhaustion input.\\n\\n### 13.2 Trusted computing base\\n\\n- manager/protocol implementation;\\n- operator-selected Git distribution, HTTPS/SSH transport, credential and host-verification broker;\\n- operating system, filesystem containment, locks, protected caches, and transaction journal;\\n- operator-trusted Go toolchain and fingerprint;\\n- canonical JSON and hash implementations;\\n- any future separately standardized signer and opaque signing identity.\\n\\n### 13.3 Required controls\\n\\n| Threat | Control |\\n|---|---|\\n| Mutable/symbolic revision | Full object-format commit lock; optional safe tag must peel exactly |\\n| Declared/effective substitution confusion | Both states and substitution type in receipt-v2 input/cache key |\\n| Replace refs or grafts | Reject refs/grafts and use `--no-replace-objects` |\\n| Partial clone or lazy fetch | Reject promisor state and use `--no-lazy-fetch`; prove all tree/blob objects local |\\n| Alternates/object-store escape | Reject alternate files/env and manager-copy into private object DB |\\n| Fetch default or tag fallback widens state/process graph | Exact fresh-repo init/fetch argv; no-tag declarations use only the full lock, tagged declarations use only the exact tag ref; fixed destination; no auto-tags, branches, submodules, `FETCH_HEAD`, maintenance, commit graph, refmap, filter, or server option |\\n| Ambient config/URL rewrite/proxy/helper | Empty global/system config, manager-owned repo, fixed config/env/process graph |\\n| SSH MITM, system config, or variant argv | `GIT_SSH_VARIANT=ssh`; exact wrapper shape; `-F` empty config; operator-known hosts; fixed OpenSSH argv |\\n| Local repository extension/ref ambiguity | Admit only ordinary files-ref layout; byte-level config/ref parser; reject gitfile, commondir, bare, reftable, or unknown extensions |\\n| Malicious/incomplete pack metadata | Pack v2/v3 plus index-v2 admission, hash-family filenames/trailers, inert private copying, fixed trusted `cat-file --batch`, and manager object-ID recomputation |\\n| Object-reader transformation or process escape | One full-OID-only batch grammar; no `ls-tree`, revision/path input, filter, textconv, mailmap, alternate, lazy fetch, or object-reader child |\\n| Commit/tag parser disagreement | One ordered manager byte grammar, reserved-header uniqueness, exact separator, actual target-type checks, and one stable semantic error on both source paths |\\n| Hook/filter/LFS/submodule execution | Raw object extraction; no checkout/archive transforms; pinned Git LFS 3.7.1-family conservative all-blob detector; reject gitlinks |\\n| Package output/argv/signing selection | Strict descriptor; command-key output; closed driver; no signing fields |\\n| Audit bypass on cache hit | Exact snapshot validation/hash/audit before artifact-cache lookup |\\n| Forged cache | Protected boundary plus exact digests; rebuild rather than adopt |\\n| Shim/PATH hijack | Structural no-follow/hash validation; manager-generated shims; never execute output |\\n| Partial/cross-project install | One manager-home journaled transaction; consumer last; reverse rollback |\\n\\nSame-principal/admin compromise remains outside the local protected-cache invariant. Receipt and marker hashes are consistency/currentness identifiers, not signatures.\\n\\n## 14. Exact impact on existing board work\\n\\nThis scope is a new schema-7/rc.5 delivery subtree. It MUST NOT reopen, relabel, or silently expand rc.4 work.\\n\\n### 14.1 Existing curator-spec schema-6 story\\n\\n`STORY-260720-35dck7` (“Protocol schema v6”) and all 12 child tasks remain rc.4 evidence. In particular:\\n\\n- `TASK-260720-1nvomm`, `TASK-260720-17llva`, and `TASK-260720-wajgn8` remain the schema-6 core/profile/manifest contract;\\n- `TASK-260720-12iigs`, `TASK-260720-2zc6k1`, and `TASK-260720-37ei85` remain receipt-v1/marker-v2, claim-v2, and legacy guards;\\n- `TASK-260720-1s1vr6`, `TASK-260720-cw39jh`, `TASK-260720-1u7hes`, `TASK-260720-3lo9jc`, `TASK-260720-q5oy3o`, and `TASK-260720-3ag6pi` remain rc.4 vectors, gates, docs, release metadata, and integrated verification.\\n\\nNo existing schema-6 task should absorb schema 7. After architecture acceptance, the smallest new curator-spec set is:\\n\\n1. core/security/decision contract;\\n2. wire schemas and generated schema cases;\\n3. manager profile/CLI including Git, substitutions, status/repair/GC;\\n4. conformance/release gates including raw-object, mixed-build, and claim-v3 vectors.\\n\\nTask-local tests/docs stay with those deliverables; no ceremonial extra task is justified.\\n\\n### 14.2 Existing Curator implementation story\\n\\n`STORY-260720-3plyvy` (“Curator Go build driver”) remains the local schema-6 `go-v1` implementation. Its manifest, source identity, toolchain, receipt/cache, marker, shim, transaction, lifecycle, docs, and rc.4 verification tasks are prerequisites/reusable foundations, not owners of external Git semantics.\\n\\nA later external-repository Curator story consumes those foundations and adds:\\n\\n- schema-7/repository/descriptor/substitution models;\\n- clean Git acquisition and raw-object snapshot admission;\\n- independent external audit subjects;\\n- receipt v2, marker v3, mixed-command planning, snapshot-cache currentness/GC;\\n- end-to-end rc.5 vectors.\\n\\nDo not change `TASK-260720-1zntv0` or other `go-v1` tasks to accept Git fields or arbitrary build behavior.\\n\\n### 14.3 Existing cocoaskills implementation story\\n\\n`STORY-260720-1uv5gi` (“csk Go build driver”) likewise remains the independent local schema-6 implementation. A new external-repository story should build on its schema model, transaction engine, source/toolchain identities, protected caches, planner, currentness/repair/GC, docs, and E2E gates.\\n\\nThe new work owns Python-specific clean Git/raw-object handling, external audit subjects, receipt-v2/marker-v3 mixed builds, substitutions, and rc.5 vectors. Existing `git archive` behavior is not sufficient unless independently proven byte-identical and free of attributes/filters; the recommended implementation is raw object extraction.\\n\\n### 14.4 Existing interoperability story\\n\\n`STORY-260720-21bsr2` (“Compiled-build interoperability”) remains rc.4/schema-6 evidence. Its 12 tasks, including shared compiled cases, native consumers, black-box runner, authoring guide, language-driver gate, release qualification, and pins, MUST NOT claim schema 7.\\n\\nAfter both managers implement schema 7, a downstream rc.5 interoperability extension adds exact cross-language comparisons for:\\n\\n- canonical URL/ref parsing and SHA-1/SHA-256 locks;\\n- raw snapshot bytes/digest and declared/effective identities;\\n- unsubstituted/substituted/mixed receipt and marker bytes;\\n- unavailable, moved, alternate/replace/graft/promisor, helper/filter/LFS/submodule negatives;\\n- cache hit/miss/corruption, status/repair/GC, shim/PATH, rollback, and release evidence.\\n\\nLinux remains a separate later native-validation item and cannot be listed in claim v3 until it passes.\\n\\n## 15. Rejected alternatives\\n\\n- Add Git/ref/output fields to a build command: duplicates source declarations and reopens output control.\\n- Let the descriptor name a binary/output: violates manager-derived output and collision/path safety.\\n- Reuse `go-v1`: changes source acquisition, identity, receipt, and marker meaning under an rc.4 driver.\\n- Tag-only, branch, abbreviated ID, or raw revision grammar: mutable or ambiguous compiler input.\\n- Trust a source or artifact cache without exact protected admission and audit: bypasses source policy.\\n- Permit replacements, alternates, grafts, partial clones, lazy fetch, local upload-pack, submodules, or LFS: adds unbound objects, processes, credentials, or network.\\n- Use `git clone`, an implicit/default fetch, or a named remote: admits template/config/refspec/tag/submodule/maintenance behavior not selected by the manager.\\n- Fetch the full locked object ID before verifying a declared tag, or use either\\n  form as fallback for the other: permits a successful operation and receipt\\n  without proving the separately promised tag assertion. Tagged declarations\\n  use only the exact tag ref; untagged declarations use only the full lock.\\n- Inherit Git/SSH/proxy/helper/filter configuration: transfers process/network control outside the closed manager policy.\\n- Auto-detect the SSH variant or pass the source SSH configuration: permits variant-dependent argv and system/user proxy, command, identity, forwarding, or control behavior.\\n- Admit gitfiles, linked worktrees, bare repositories, reftable, unknown repository extensions, optional pack sidecars, pack versions outside 2/3, or pack indexes outside v2 in local-substitution v1: requires additional containment/parser semantics and vectors; reject with stable errors instead.\\n- Let each manager choose its own documented pack/index subset: makes the same local substitution non-interoperable; v1 fixes pack 2/3 plus index 2.\\n- Use `ls-tree`, `--batch-command`, pretty-printers, revision/path expressions, checkout, archive, filters, textconv, or mailmap for extraction: adds redundant grammars or repository-selected transformations; one full-OID `cat-file --batch` stream is sufficient.\\n- Let `cat-file` type labels choose among malformed commit/tag headers: local raw objects can contain duplicate or misordered structural headers; the manager applies one exact semantic grammar after recomputing IDs.\\n- Treat recognized Git LFS pointer text as compiler source: a package can make a pointer visible through `go:embed` while the actual bytes remain outside the locked Git graph; v1 rejects every form accepted by the pinned Git LFS 3.7.1 parser family before audit.\\n- Store only effective substitution state: loses declared provenance; store only declared state: misdescribes compiled bytes.\\n- Upgrade every schema-7 command to receipt v2: unnecessarily changes local `go-v1`; mixed explicit versions preserve rc.4 semantics.\\n- Install-time signing/notarization: introduces secrets, post-processing children, mutable services, and new identity not covered by the first driver.\\n- Standardize physical cache/checkouts: machine-local layout remains implementation-specific.\\n\\n## 16. Primary-source register\\n\\nChecked on 2026-07-27:\\n\\n- Git global options and environment isolation, including `--no-replace-objects`, `--no-lazy-fetch`, `GIT_CONFIG_GLOBAL`, `GIT_CONFIG_SYSTEM`, `GIT_CONFIG_NOSYSTEM`, `GIT_SSH`, and prompt controls: https://git-scm.com/docs/git\\n- Exact fetch defaults and controls, including tag auto-follow, `FETCH_HEAD`, auto-maintenance, commit-graph writing, refmap, submodule recursion, upload-pack, filters, and full-object refspecs: https://git-scm.com/docs/git-fetch\\n- Server direct-object want policy, including\\n  `uploadpack.allowTipSHA1InWant`, `uploadpack.allowReachableSHA1InWant`, and\\n  `uploadpack.allowAnySHA1InWant`: https://git-scm.com/docs/git-config\\n- Private repository initialization, object format, files/reftable ref format, and template selection: https://git-scm.com/docs/git-init\\n- Repository format version and mandatory extension recognition/rejection: https://git-scm.com/docs/repository-version\\n- Reftable repository extension, stack, and dummy files that make implicit files-ref parsing unsafe: https://git-scm.com/docs/reftable\\n- Repository layout, gitfiles, commondir/linked worktrees, loose/packed refs, shallow, graft, alternate, and object-pack state: https://git-scm.com/docs/gitrepository-layout\\n- Pack and index structure/checksum surfaces, including pack versions 2/3,\\n  index version 2, SHA-1/SHA-256 widths, fanout/offset tables, and trailers:\\n  https://git-scm.com/docs/gitformat-pack\\n- Git SSH variant selection and documented `ssh` wrapper argv: https://git-scm.com/docs/git-config\\n- OpenSSH `-F` configuration isolation and command-line behavior: https://man.openbsd.org/ssh.1\\n- OpenSSH system configuration, proxy/jump/local-command, known-hosts, identity/agent, forwarding, TTY, and control options: https://man.openbsd.org/ssh_config.5\\n- Replacement refs are honored by default and disabled by the fixed option/environment: https://git-scm.com/docs/git-replace\\n- Partial clones, promisor pack markers, promisor remotes, and dynamic missing-object fetch: https://git-scm.com/docs/partial-clone\\n- Alternate object stores, replace refs, grafts, shallow/commondir repository state: https://git-scm.com/docs/gitrepository-layout\\n- Exact ref-name exclusions used by the tag/branch grammar: https://git-scm.com/docs/git-check-ref-format\\n- URL rewrites, protocol policy, remote proxy/upload-pack/VCS/promisor/server-option configuration: https://git-scm.com/docs/git-config\\n- Credential helpers are transformed into shell-executed commands: https://git-scm.com/docs/gitcredentials\\n- Raw object batch request/response framing, full object-name/type/size atoms,\\n  and the separate filter/textconv/mailmap/follow-symlink options:\\n  https://git-scm.com/docs/git-cat-file\\n- Revision peeling semantics: https://git-scm.com/docs/gitrevisions\\n- Git attributes and checkout filters: https://git-scm.com/docs/gitattributes\\n- Git LFS pointer/content separation and normative pointer byte rules:\\n  https://github.com/git-lfs/git-lfs/blob/v3.7.1/docs/spec.md\\n- Pinned Git LFS 3.7.1 aliases, `<1024` cutoff, `bytes.TrimSpace`,\\n  line-state parser, `\\\\Aext-\\\\d{1}-\\\\w+` prefix behavior, signed-int64 size,\\n  duplicate-priority validation, zero-size encoder, and canonical encoder:\\n  https://github.com/git-lfs/git-lfs/blob/v3.7.1/lfs/pointer.go\\n- Git commit/tree/tag object data model:\\n  https://git-scm.com/docs/gitdatamodel\\n- Git commit required tree/parent/author/committer structure:\\n  https://git-scm.com/docs/git-commit-tree\\n- Commit and tag signature placement, including continuation lines for\\n  `gpgsig` and message-body signatures for annotated tags:\\n  https://git-scm.com/docs/signature-format\\n- Go vendoring and fixed build behavior: https://go.dev/ref/mod#vendoring and https://go.dev/cmd/go/#hdr-Compile_packages_and_dependencies\\n- Go toolchain selection: https://go.dev/doc/toolchain\\n- Apple notarization/signing workflow: https://developer.apple.com/documentation/security/notarizing-macos-software-before-distribution\\n- Microsoft SignTool certificate/timestamp surfaces: https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/seccrypto/signtool\\n- Cargo build scripts: https://doc.rust-lang.org/cargo/reference/build-scripts.html\\n- javac annotation processing: https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/23/docs/specs/man/javac.html\\n- Roslyn source generators: https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/csharp/roslyn-sdk/#source-generators\\n\\n## 17. Validation and independent-review checks\\n\\nRequired packet validation:\\n\\n- every fenced JSON example parses, including the exact init/fetch/SSH and\\n  `cat-file --batch` arrays;\\n- unsubstituted source omits `substitution`;\\n- substituted source requires both `substituted: true` and a typed substitution;\\n- mixed marker v3 carries receipt v1 for local `go-v1`, receipt v2 for external commands, and top-level skill build-source only because a local build is active;\\n- all revision-1 findings remain mapped to sections 3, 5, 6, 8, and 9;\\n- all revision-2 findings map to exact network argv (6.2–6.3), SSH\\n  isolation (6.4), and local format/ref/object admission (6.5–6.6);\\n- all revision-3 findings map to the single raw-object process and byte\\n  protocol (6.1, 6.6.1), pack 2/3 plus index-2 local admission (6.5.3), and the\\n  all-blob LFS detector (6.6.3);\\n- all revision-4 findings map to the pinned parser-family detector and\\n  cross-language LFS fixtures (6.6.3), plus the common commit/tag byte grammar,\\n  semantic result, and network/local parity fixtures (6.6.2);\\n- the revision-5 finding maps to the tag/no-tag acquisition split, typed\\n  ordering, and six-case direct-OID-policy matrix in section 6.3, plus the\\n  audit/receipt/repair rules in sections 7 through 10;\\n- no project/spec/code/schema/test/release/prior-resource file changes.\\n\\nIndependent review should decide:\\n\\n1. Is the declared/effective receipt shape sufficient to prevent substitution/cache confusion?\\n2. Do the exact init/fetch vectors prevent remote/package/default selection of\\n   local refs, helper, refspec, tags, submodules, maintenance, `FETCH_HEAD`,\\n   filter, server option, or upload-pack?\\n3. Does the fixed SSH wrapper admit only the tested protocol-v0 destination and\\n   remote command, and does the OpenSSH vector suppress user/system config,\\n   prompts, TTY, forwarding, proxy/jump/local-command/control behavior while\\n   retaining only the required private Git protocol pipe?\\n4. Does local v1 fail stably for gitfile/linked/bare/reftable/unknown layouts\\n   and unambiguously parse ordinary config, files refs, loose objects, and\\n   exact pack/index pairs without running source Git behavior?\\n5. Does the single full-OID `cat-file --batch` contract close request,\\n   response, transformation, child-process, network, missing-object, and\\n   resource-limit behavior for both network and inert-copied local stores?\\n6. Are pack versions 2/3 and index version 2 defined identically for SHA-1 and\\n   SHA-256 managers, with all other physical formats rejected stably?\\n7. Does the pinned Git LFS 3.7.1-family detector cover every accepted\\n   key/value and encoder form, including extension suffixes, duplicate\\n   behavior, signed/zero-padded sizes, exact cutoff, endings/order, and\\n   non-empty size zero, while keeping a closed ordinary-blob boundary?\\n8. Does the common commit/tag byte grammar accept signed and extra-header\\n   objects while rejecting duplicate/misordered structural headers, malformed\\n   separators, wrong target types, cycles, and depth overflow with the same\\n   semantic result on both source paths?\\n9. Are URL, SSH trust, tag, and structured development-ref rules exact enough for cross-language implementations?\\n10. Are whole-snapshot audit and pre-cache ordering coherent on real, cache-hit, and dry-run paths?\\n11. Can marker v3 represent local-only, external-only, and mixed installations without changing receipt-v1/schema-6 meaning?\\n12. Do status, repair, deduplication, rollback, and GC fail closed without executing source or built output?\\n13. Does logical target selection satisfy repository target selection while preserving manager-derived binary names and paths?\\n14. Are signing and future-driver boundaries still closed?\\n15. For every unsubstituted declaration containing `tag`, is exact-tag fetch\\n    the sole acquisition path regardless of direct-OID server policy, with\\n    matching, moved, missing, offline, audit, receipt, and repair outcomes\\n    ordered before cache or compiler work?\\n\\n### 17.1 Producer verification evidence\\n\\nChecks performed on 2026-07-27:\\n\\n- all thirteen fenced JSON documents parsed with the host Python JSON parser;\\n- targeted topic checks found the exact init/fetch vectors,\\n  `GIT_SSH_VARIANT=ssh`, wrapper argv contract, `-F` SSH isolation, stable\\n  gitfile/bare/linked/reftable errors, config/ref grammar, exact pack-2/3 and\\n  index-2 admission, the full-OID-only batch request/response grammar, manager\\n  object-ID recomputation, common commit/tag semantic grammar, pinned-family\\n  all-blob LFS detector, audit-before-cache/compiler ordering, mixed\\n  receipt/marker behavior, status/repair/GC, signing, future-driver rules, and\\n  board-impact mapping;\\n- the new Git fetch/init/repository-version/reftable/layout/config and OpenSSH\\n  manual pages in section 16 were opened from their primary maintainers and\\n  checked against the claims above;\\n- a temporary local-transport smoke on Git 2.50.1 exercised the exact\\n  init/common-fetch option shape for both full-object and exact-tag refspecs,\\n  verified the fixed `refs/curator/*` destinations, and verified that\\n  `FETCH_HEAD` was absent;\\n- a revision-6 Git 2.50.1 smoke repeated exact-tag-only acquisition with\\n  `uploadpack.allowAnySHA1InWant` disabled and enabled: matching annotated tags\\n  fetched and peeled to the lock, moved lightweight tags fetched but failed the\\n  manager equality check, missing tags made fetch exit 128, no direct object-ID\\n  refspec was attempted, and `FETCH_HEAD` remained absent in all six cases;\\n- a task-local Git 2.50.1 raw-object smoke used the exact `cat-file --batch`\\n  argv against SHA-1 and SHA-256 private repositories; full-ID requests\\n  returned the documented `<oid> <type> <size> LF`, exact content bytes, and\\n  terminal LF, while no filter/textconv/mailmap option or child process was\\n  used;\\n- Git LFS 3.7.1 source and binary checks confirmed the `<1024` cutoff,\\n  `bytes.TrimSpace`/ScanLines behavior, current/alias versions, one-digit\\n  prefix-only extension regexp, exact-key overwrite, distinct-priority\\n  rejection, signed-int64 size parsing, and empty encoding for size zero;\\n- the checked official `v3.7.1/lfs/pointer.go` bytes hashed to\\n  `sha256:3aa4a0df7ed9b3c8f45742a4e01909feeb721017bfbd70e2a166132b2a814b2d`;\\n  the local binaries were Git 2.50.1 and Git LFS 3.7.1;\\n- pinned Git LFS 3.7.1 strict checks returned zero for the canonical lowercase,\\n  dot/dash, underscore, uppercase, and punctuation-suffix extension fixture,\\n  and returned two for the deliberately noncanonical non-empty size-zero\\n  fixture;\\n- independent cross-language fixture checks covered the exact 1023/1024 cutoff,\\n  CRLF/blank/unsorted/no-final-LF forms, exact duplicate keys, distinct\\n  duplicate priorities, non-empty size zero, punctuation-start and uppercase-\\n  OID near misses;\\n- Git 2.50.1 raw-object smoke and the manager grammar fixtures covered valid\\n  signed/extra-header commits and tags, duplicate/misordered tree/object/type\\n  headers, malformed separators, declared/actual target mismatch, and identical\\n  network-private/local-inert expected results;\\n- unchanged curator-spec validation passed:\\n  `validated 30 schemas and 93 vector files`;\\n- the pinned validation environment's full `make validate` passed all 30\\n  schemas, 93 vectors, 8 Python tests, and `go test ./tools/...`;\\n- the official Git fetch and upload-pack configuration documentation was\\n  rechecked for explicit tag refspecs, `--no-tags`, empty refmap,\\n  `--no-write-fetch-head`, and direct-object want policy;\\n- `git diff --check` passed in curator-spec;\\n- this run did not edit product/spec/schema/code/test/release files or any\\n  prior producer/reviewer resource.\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n/Users/iv/Developer/ReluxWorks/curator/.task-board/.resources/TASK-260728-1a52au/TASK-260728-1a52au_spawn-log_-implementer--doc-writer--codex-_RUN-260727-a527c3.log:61:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_30\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"sed -n '1,220p' protocol/core.md\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"# Curator Protocol Core 1.0\\n\\nThis document is normative. It defines the portable objects and deterministic\\nalgorithms shared by conforming Curator Protocol managers. Tool-specific state\\nand user interfaces are defined outside the core.\\n\\n## 1. Data model and versioning\\n\\nProtocol JSON MUST be UTF-8 without a byte-order mark. Parsers MUST reject\\nduplicate object keys, invalid UTF-8, trailing non-whitespace data, and values\\nthat violate the applicable schema under `../schemas/v1/`.\\n\\nJSON object member order and insignificant whitespace have no meaning unless a\\nsection explicitly defines canonical bytes. Array order is significant unless\\nthe field is declared set-like. Writers MUST emit the current schema version;\\nreaders MUST reject unsupported versions with an upgrade error and MUST NOT\\ninfer a newer schema from its fields.\\n\\nSchemas define structural validity. The semantic checks in this document are\\nadditional and REQUIRED.\\n\\n### 1.1 Compatibility identifiers\\n\\nThe filenames `Skillfile.json`, `Skillfile.dev.json`, `agent-skill.json`,\\n`.csk-install.json`, `.csk-managed.json`, and project root `.agents/` are\\nportable protocol identifiers. A manager MUST read and write those exact names.\\nThe filename `csk-skill.json` is a reserved legacy alias for\\n`agent-skill.json`: managers MUST continue to read it throughout protocol 1.x,\\nbut writers MUST emit only `agent-skill.json`.\\n\\nMachine-home directories, cache names, executable names, global environment\\nvariables, and managed comment text are implementation-specific. A manager\\nMUST NOT write machine-local state into another manager's home unless the user\\nexplicitly selected that location.\\n\\n## 2. Portable identifiers and paths\\n\\nSkill names, command names, MCP server names, agent identifiers, and adapter\\nledger entries MUST match:\\n\\n```text\\n^[A-Za-z0-9][A-Za-z0-9._-]*$\\n```\\n\\nThey MUST additionally satisfy the portable filename rules below. Comparison\\nis case-sensitive even on a case-insensitive filesystem.\\n\\nA portable relative path:\\n\\n1. is a non-empty Unicode string encoded as UTF-8;\\n2. uses `/` separators and contains no `\\\\`, NUL, or control character;\\n3. is not absolute and has no empty, `.` or `..` component;\\n4. has no component ending in a space or `.` and no component containing `:`;\\n5. has no component whose case-insensitive basename before its first `.` is\\n   `CON`, `PRN`, `AUX`, `NUL`, `COM1` through `COM9`, or `LPT1` through `LPT9`.\\n\\nImplementations MUST preserve Unicode scalar values exactly and MUST NOT apply\\nnormalization during hashing or comparison. Filesystem extraction MUST detect\\ntwo protocol paths that map to one platform path and fail before writing.\\n\\n## 3. Skill packages\\n\\nA skill package is a git snapshot or a directory within one. Its root MUST\\ncontain `SKILL.md` with YAML frontmatter containing non-empty `name` and\\n`description` strings. The frontmatter `name` MUST equal the declared skill\\nname. `triggers`, when present, is a list of non-empty strings.\\n\\nAn installer MUST NOT execute package-provided code while resolving,\\nvalidating, auditing, or installing a skill. Schema 6 build commands are the\\nonly compilation extension to this rule: a manager MAY pass untrusted Go\\nsource bytes to the closed `go-v1` driver in section 4.2, but MUST NOT transfer\\nexecution control to a package-selected program, argument vector, environment,\\nhook, plugin, generator, build recipe, or compiled output.\\n\\n### 3.1 Context selection\\n\\nOnly these root entries are eligible for agent-facing context:\\n\\n```text\\nSKILL.md\\nagents/\\nreferences/\\n.skill_triggers/\\nassets/\\ntemplates/\\nexamples/\\ndata/\\n```\\n\\n`scripts/` is additionally eligible only when the package exports no commands.\\nAt every depth the following names or glob patterns MUST be excluded:\\n\\n```text\\n.git .github .gitlab-ci.yml .venv __pycache__ *.pyc node_modules\\ntests test __tests__ README* CHANGELOG* LICENSE* Makefile\\nsetup.py pyproject.toml requirements*.txt .DS_Store .gitignore\\n```\\n\\nDirectories declared in `runtime_roots` or `build_roots` are excluded as whole\\nsubtrees even if nested under an eligible context root. The `build_roots`\\nexclusion is computed from the validated manifest before locale rendering,\\ncache lookup, or any compiler command. It is therefore identical for a real\\nbuild, a build-cache hit, and dry-run. Build roots MUST NOT be copied into the\\ncommit-keyed runtime store or any installed context, and generated artifacts\\nMUST remain outside agent-facing context.\\n\\nSelection uses protocol paths, sorts selected files by Unicode scalar value of\\ntheir POSIX path, rejects links, and copies regular file bytes without newline\\nconversion.\\n\\n### 3.2 Localization\\n\\nLocalization is inactive when no locale is selected. When\\n`locales/metadata.json` exists it MUST contain an object `locales`; when\\n`.skill_triggers/` exists it MUST be a directory. A consistent locale has both\\n`locales[locale]` and `.skill_triggers/<locale>.md`. At least one consistent\\nlocale is REQUIRED when either localization surface exists.\\n\\nA locale selector is 1 through 64 ASCII letters, digits, or hyphens, starts and\\nends with a letter or digit, and is compared case-sensitively without\\nnormalization. Every `locales` member name MUST be a locale selector and its\\nvalue MUST be an object. This deliberately uses a safe BCP 47-compatible\\nsurface without attempting language-tag canonicalization.\\n\\nFor a selected consistent locale, a manager replaces only the `description`\\nand `triggers` values in installed `SKILL.md`, preserving `name` and body. List\\nitems beginning with `- ` outside fenced code blocks form the trigger list.\\nWhen present, `agents/openai.yaml` is rendered from `display_name`,\\n`short_description`, and `default_prompt`. If the selected locale is\\nunavailable, source context is installed unchanged and a warning lists the\\navailable consistent locales. The warning is emitted even for an otherwise\\ncurrent installation.\\n\\n## 4. Skill manifest\\n\\n`agent-skill.json` is OPTIONAL for a pure context skill and otherwise conforms\\nto exactly one of `agent-skill-v1.schema.json` through\\n`agent-skill-v6.schema.json`. The legacy filename `csk-skill.json` has exactly\\nthe same object shape and schema-version semantics through\\n`csk-skill-v6.schema.json`.\\n\\nReaders resolve manifests in this order:\\n\\n1. When only `agent-skill.json` exists, read it.\\n2. When only `csk-skill.json` exists, read it as a legacy manifest.\\n3. When both exist, parse and validate both independently. They MUST represent\\n   equal JSON values: object member order and insignificant whitespace are\\n   ignored, array order and JSON value types remain significant. If equal,\\n   `agent-skill.json` is authoritative. If unequal, the reader MUST fail with\\n   `conflicting_skill_manifests` and MUST NOT install either value.\\n4. The existence of an invalid modern manifest is an error and MUST NOT fall\\n   back to the other filename or to `agents/runtime.json`.\\n\\n| Schema | Added behavior |\\n|---|---|\\n| 1 | exported script and system commands |\\n| 2 | `runtime_roots`, command dependencies, strict top-level fields |\\n| 3 | REQUIRED capability declaration |\\n| 4 | transitive skill requirements |\\n| 5 | MCP server requirements |\\n| 6 | declarative compiled commands and context-excluded `build_roots` |\\n\\nVersion gates are downward: a field introduced by a later version MUST be\\nrejected in an earlier one. Schema 1 preserves its deployed extension behavior;\\nschemas 2 through 6 reject unknown fields. In particular, schema 1 through 5\\nMUST reject `build_roots`, a command with `type: \\\"build\\\"`, and every build-only\\nfield. Their script, system, runtime-root, capability, dependency, context,\\nhash, and marker-v1 behavior is unchanged.\\n\\n### 4.1 Runtime roots and commands\\n\\nEvery runtime root and script command path MUST be a portable relative path\\nthat exists in the snapshot. Runtime roots MUST name directories, be unique,\\nand be pairwise disjoint. For schema 2 and later, every script command path\\nMUST fall within one declared runtime root when any roots are declared.\\n\\nA script command declares at least one of `unix_path` and `win_path`. A system\\ncommand declares a non-empty bare executable name and MAY include a hint. A\\nmissing system command fails installation with the hint. The command name is\\nthe shim name and one active name has exactly one owner.\\n\\n### 4.2 Build roots and the closed `go-v1` command\\n\\n`build_roots` and build commands exist only in manifest schema 6. Every build\\nroot MUST be a portable relative path other than `.`, MUST name a real,\\nlink-free directory in the immutable raw snapshot, and MUST be unique and\\npairwise disjoint. No build root may equal, contain, or be contained by a\\nruntime root. Every declared build root MUST be referenced by at least one\\nbuild command.\\n\\nA build command has exactly this package-controlled surface:\\n\\n```json\\n{\\\"type\\\":\\\"build\\\",\\\"driver\\\":\\\"go-v1\\\",\\\"source_dir\\\":\\\"build/cmd/tool\\\"}\\n```\\n\\nThe object MUST contain exactly `type`, `driver`, and `source_dir`; the driver\\nMUST be the closed identifier `go-v1`. `source_dir` MUST be a real, link-free\\ndirectory below exactly one declared build root and MAY equal that root, but\\nMUST NOT be `.`. The containing build root is the command's `build_root`; it\\nMUST contain `go.mod` directly, and that file MUST be the nearest ancestor\\n`go.mod` of `source_dir`. An intervening module is invalid.\\n\\nThe manager MUST derive the artifact-relative path solely from the command\\nname: `bin/<command>` on Unix and `bin/<command>.exe` on Windows. A package\\nMUST NOT select a program, arguments, environment, tags, flags, toolchain,\\noutput path, build script, hook, plugin, generator, or post-build action.\\nUnsupported drivers MUST fail without falling back to a system command or\\ngeneric build facility. Build commands participate in existing activation,\\ndependency-command selection, portable-name collision, shim collision, and\\nprovider-first closure rules exactly like script commands. Within one closure\\nnode, active build command names MUST be processed in Unicode-scalar lexical\\norder.\\n\\n`go-v1` builds exactly one native executable from exactly one package named\\n`main`. The toolchain MUST be Go 1.23 or newer, operator-trusted rather than\\npackage-selected, and from a release family tested by the manager against the\\n`go-v1` conformance vectors. The module is the declared build root, dependency\\nresolution is vendor-only and networkless, and workspaces, toolchain switching,\\ncross-compilation, PGO, cgo, package-controlled assembly, host objects,\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n/Users/iv/Developer/ReluxWorks/curator/.task-board/.resources/TASK-260728-1a52au/TASK-260728-1a52au_spawn-log_-implementer--doc-writer--codex-_RUN-260727-a527c3.log:67:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_33\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"task-board q --format compact 'get(STORY-260728-10wxx2) { full }'\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"id:STORY-260728-10wxx2\\ntype:story\\nname:protocol-schema-v7-external-build-repositories\\ntitle:STORY-260728-10wxx2: protocol-schema-v7-external-build-repositories\\nstatus:development\\nreview:required\\ntask_class:docs\\nestimate:{\\\"kind\\\":\\\"estimated\\\",\\\"scale\\\":{\\\"kind\\\":\\\"fibonacci\\\",\\\"number\\\":29}}\\nobservability:{\\\"readiness\\\":{\\\"advisoryBlockedElementIds\\\":[\\\"STORY-260728-10wxx2\\\"],\\\"blockedElementIds\\\":[\\\"TASK-260728-17sclp\\\",\\\"TASK-260728-3b8qym\\\",\\\"TASK-260728-wy3dsw\\\"],\\\"coverage\\\":{\\\"complete\\\":true,\\\"estimated\\\":4,\\\"ratio\\\":\\\"4/4\\\",\\\"total\\\":4,\\\"unestimated\\\":0},\\\"estimateKind\\\":\\\"estimated\\\",\\\"isAdvisoryBlocked\\\":true,\\\"isBlocked\\\":true,\\\"rolledEstimate\\\":{\\\"kind\\\":\\\"estimated\\\",\\\"scale\\\":{\\\"kind\\\":\\\"fibonacci\\\",\\\"number\\\":29}}},\\\"scope\\\":{\\\"id\\\":\\\"STORY-260728-10wxx2\\\",\\\"name\\\":\\\"protocol-schema-v7-external-build-repositories\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"story\\\"},\\\"summary\\\":{\\\"active\\\":[{\\\"id\\\":\\\"STORY-260728-10wxx2\\\",\\\"name\\\":\\\"protocol-schema-v7-external-build-repositories\\\",\\\"status\\\":\\\"development\\\"},{\\\"id\\\":\\\"TASK-260728-1a52au\\\",\\\"name\\\":\\\"schema-v7-external-repository-core-contract\\\",\\\"status\\\":\\\"development\\\"}],\\\"blocked\\\":[{\\\"blockedBy\\\":[],\\\"derivedBlockedBy\\\":[\\\"STORY-260720-35dck7\\\"],\\\"id\\\":\\\"STORY-260728-10wxx2\\\",\\\"isAdvisoryBlocked\\\":true,\\\"isBlocked\\\":false,\\\"name\\\":\\\"protocol-schema-v7-external-build-repositories\\\"},{\\\"blockedBy\\\":[\\\"TASK-260728-1a52au\\\"],\\\"derivedBlockedBy\\\":[],\\\"id\\\":\\\"TASK-260728-17sclp\\\",\\\"isAdvisoryBlocked\\\":false,\\\"isBlocked\\\":true,\\\"name\\\":\\\"external-repository-wire-schemas-and-generated-cases\\\"},{\\\"blockedBy\\\":[\\\"TASK-260728-17sclp\\\",\\\"TASK-260728-wy3dsw\\\"],\\\"derivedBlockedBy\\\":[],\\\"id\\\":\\\"TASK-260728-3b8qym\\\",\\\"isAdvisoryBlocked\\\":false,\\\"isBlocked\\\":true,\\\"name\\\":\\\"rc5-external-repository-conformance-and-release-gates\\\"},{\\\"blockedBy\\\":[\\\"TASK-260728-1a52au\\\",\\\"TASK-260728-17sclp\\\"],\\\"derivedBlockedBy\\\":[],\\\"id\\\":\\\"TASK-260728-wy3dsw\\\",\\\"isAdvisoryBlocked\\\":false,\\\"isBlocked\\\":true,\\\"name\\\":\\\"external-repository-manager-profile-and-lifecycle-contract\\\"}],\\\"byType\\\":{\\\"bug\\\":{\\\"total\\\":0,\\\"todo\\\":0,\\\"active\\\":0,\\\"done\\\":0,\\\"closed\\\":0,\\\"blocked\\\":0},\\\"epic\\\":{\\\"total\\\":0,\\\"todo\\\":0,\\\"active\\\":0,\\\"done\\\":0,\\\"closed\\\":0,\\\"blocked\\\":0},\\\"story\\\":{\\\"total\\\":1,\\\"todo\\\":0,\\\"active\\\":1,\\\"done\\\":0,\\\"closed\\\":0,\\\"blocked\\\":0},\\\"task\\\":{\\\"total\\\":4,\\\"todo\\\":3,\\\"active\\\":1,\\\"done\\\":0,\\\"closed\\\":0,\\\"blocked\\\":0}}}}\\nparent:EPIC-260720-21aq1i\\nassignee:\\ndescription:Extend curator-spec with the accepted schema-7/rc.5 contract for source-audited external Git repositories that produce manager-installed CLI commands. Preserve schemas 1-6 and go-v1 byte and semantic compatibility while adding build_repositories, immutable commit locks with optional exact-tag assertions, curator-build.json schema 1 logical targets, go-repository-v1, operator substitutions, receipt v2, marker v3, and claim v3.\\nscope:curator-spec normative prose, JSON schemas, generated cases, conformance vectors, manager profile and CLI contract, authoring documentation, and rc.5 release evidence. No Curator or cocoaskills implementation. Normative architecture input is TASK-260720-1nvomm_external-build-repositories-architecture-v6.md at SHA-256 2abae77d80eba6789f9911db7e9722595b4f21ba47391ca9eafd0064af03d67e.\\nac:Schema 7 and every new wire artifact are closed, versioned, and implementation-ready; external source is resolved, raw-snapshot validated, hashed, and independently audited before cache or compiler work; inaccessible sources follow typed offline-warning versus install/audit hard-failure semantics; package-controlled argv, environment, output paths, executable names, credentials, hooks, signing, and generic language drivers remain forbidden; schemas 1-6, go-v1, receipt v1, marker v1/v2, and rc.4 claims remain unchanged; generated cases, validators, compatibility guards, and rc.5 release gates pass.\\nblockedBy:[]\\nblocks:[]\\nderivedBlockedBy:[\\\"STORY-260720-35dck7\\\"]\\nderivedBlocks:[\\\"STORY-260728-1ojb1p\\\"]\\nchecklist:[]\\nnotes:\\ncreated:2026-07-27T20:19:19Z\\nupdated:2026-07-27T20:25:18Z\\nisBlocked:false\\nisAdvisoryBlocked:true\\nchildren:[\\\"TASK-260728-17sclp\\\",\\\"TASK-260728-1a52au\\\",\\\"TASK-260728-3b8qym\\\",\\\"TASK-260728-wy3dsw\\\"]\\npreconditionResources:[]\\noutcomeResources:[]\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n/Users/iv/Developer/ReluxWorks/curator/.task-board/.resources/TASK-260728-1a52au/TASK-260728-1a52au_spawn-log_-implementer--doc-writer--codex-_RUN-260727-a527c3.log:69:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_34\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"task-board q --format compact 'list(type=task, parent=STORY-260728-10wxx2) { full }'\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"